const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Verona. An open place.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine and Proteus.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Cease to persuade, my loving Proteus.","Home-keeping youth have ever homely wits.","Were't not affection chains thy tender days","To the sweet glances of thy honoured love,","I rather would entreat thy company","To see the wonders of the world abroad","Than, living dully sluggardized at home,","Wear out thy youth with shapeless idleness.","But since thou lov'st, love still, and thrive therein,","Even as I would when I to love begin."],"id":2,"m":"Stop trying to talk me round, my dear Proteus. Young men who stay at home always have small-town minds. If love didn't tie your young life to the sweet looks of the girl you adore, I'd rather beg you to come with me and see the wonders of the world than watch you stay at home, dull and lazy, wasting your youth in aimless idleness. But since you are in love, keep loving, and may it go well for you, as I'd hope it would for me if I ever start."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Wilt thou be gone? Sweet Valentine, adieu.","Think on thy Proteus when thou haply seest","Some rare noteworthy object in thy travel.","Wish me partaker in thy happiness","When thou dost meet good hap; and in thy danger,","If ever danger do environ thee,","Commend thy grievance to my holy prayers,","For I will be thy beadsman, Valentine."],"id":3,"m":"Are you really going? Sweet Valentine, goodbye. Think of your Proteus whenever you happen to see something rare and remarkable on your travels. Wish I could share your happiness when good luck comes your way; and if you're ever in danger, hand your troubles over to my prayers, because I'll pray for you, Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["And on a love-book pray for my success?"],"id":4,"m":"Praying for my success on a book of love poems, I suppose?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Upon some book I love I'll pray for thee."],"id":5,"m":"I'll pray for you on some book I love."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["That's on some shallow story of deep love,","How young Leander crossed the Hellespont."],"id":6,"m":"That'll be some shallow story about deep love, like young Leander swimming the Hellespont."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["That's a deep story of a deeper love,","For he was more than over shoes in love."],"id":7,"m":"That's a deep story about an even deeper love, because he was in love over his shoes, right up to the neck."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["'Tis true; for you are over boots in love,","And yet you never swam the Hellespont."],"id":8,"m":"True; and you are in love over your boots, and yet you've never swum the Hellespont."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Over the boots? Nay, give me not the boots."],"id":9,"m":"Over my boots? No, don't mock me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No, I will not, for it boots thee not."],"id":10,"m":"No, I won't, because it would do you no good."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["What?"],"id":11,"m":"What wouldn't?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["To be in love, where scorn is bought with groans,","Coy looks with heart-sore sighs, one fading moment's mirth","With twenty watchful, weary, tedious nights.","If haply won, perhaps a hapless gain;","If lost, why then a grievous labour won;","However, but a folly bought with wit,","Or else a wit by folly vanquished."],"id":12,"m":"Being in love, where you buy scorn with groans, a coy look with heart-sick sighs, one brief moment of happiness with twenty sleepless, weary, endless nights. If you happen to win, it may be an unlucky prize; if you lose, all you've won is misery. Either way, it's foolishness bought with your intelligence, or intelligence beaten by foolishness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["So, by your circumstance, you call me fool."],"id":13,"m":"So by that argument you're calling me a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["So, by your circumstance, I fear you'll prove."],"id":14,"m":"By the way you're going, I'm afraid you'll turn out to be one."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["'Tis love you cavil at. I am not Love."],"id":15,"m":"It's love you're finding fault with. I'm not Love."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Love is your master, for he masters you;","And he that is so yoked by a fool","Methinks should not be chronicled for wise."],"id":16,"m":"Love is your master, because he controls you; and anyone yoked to a fool like that shouldn't go down in history as wise."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Yet writers say, as in the sweetest bud","The eating canker dwells, so eating love","Inhabits in the finest wits of all."],"id":17,"m":"Yet writers say that just as the grub that eats a flower lives in the sweetest bud, so love, which eats away at us, lives in the finest minds of all."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["And writers say, as the most forward bud","Is eaten by the canker ere it blow,","Even so by love the young and tender wit","Is turned to folly, blasting in the bud,","Losing his verdure even in the prime,","And all the fair effects of future hopes.","But wherefore waste I time to counsel thee","That art a votary to fond desire?","Once more adieu. My father at the road","Expects my coming, there to see me shipped."],"id":18,"m":"And writers also say that just as the earliest bud is eaten by the grub before it can open, so love turns a young, tender mind to foolishness. It withers in the bud, losing its freshness in its prime and all the good things it promised for the future. But why am I wasting my time advising you, when you're devoted to foolish desire? Once more, goodbye. My father is waiting at the harbour to see me off on the ship."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["And thither will I bring thee, Valentine."],"id":19,"m":"And I'll go there with you, Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Sweet Proteus, no. Now let us take our leave.","To Milan let me hear from thee by letters","Of thy success in love, and what news else","Betideth here in absence of thy friend;","And I likewise will visit thee with mine."],"id":20,"m":"No, sweet Proteus. Let's say goodbye here. Write to me in Milan about how your love goes and whatever else happens here while your friend is away, and I'll write back in the same way."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["All happiness bechance to thee in Milan."],"id":21,"m":"I wish you every happiness in Milan."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["As much to you at home, and so farewell."],"id":22,"m":"The same to you at home. And so, goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":23},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["He after honour hunts, I after love.","He leaves his friends to dignify them more;","I leave myself, my friends, and all for love.","Thou, Julia, thou hast metamorphosed me,","Made me neglect my studies, lose my time,","War with good counsel, set the world at nought;","Made wit with musing weak, heart sick with thought."],"id":24,"m":"He hunts for honour, I hunt for love. He leaves his friends to bring them more credit; I leave myself, my friends and everything for love. You, Julia, you have transformed me. You've made me neglect my studies, waste my time, fight against good advice and care nothing for the world. You've made my mind weak with daydreaming and my heart sick with worry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Speed.","id":25},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Sir Proteus, 'save you. Saw you my master?"],"id":26,"m":"Sir Proteus, God save you. Have you seen my master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["But now he parted hence to embark for Milan."],"id":27,"m":"He's just left here to board the ship for Milan."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Twenty to one, then, he is shipped already,","And I have played the sheep in losing him."],"id":28,"m":"Then twenty to one he's already on board, and I've been a sheep and lost him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Indeed a sheep doth very often stray,","An if the shepherd be a while away."],"id":29,"m":"Well, a sheep often does stray if the shepherd is away for a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["You conclude that my master is a shepherd then, and I a sheep?"],"id":30,"m":"So you're saying my master is a shepherd and I'm a sheep?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I do."],"id":31,"m":"I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why then, my horns are his horns, whether I wake or sleep."],"id":32,"m":"Then my horns are his horns, whether I'm awake or asleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["A silly answer, and fitting well a sheep."],"id":33,"m":"A silly answer, and just right for a sheep."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["This proves me still a sheep."],"id":34,"m":"That proves I'm still a sheep."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["True, and thy master a shepherd."],"id":35,"m":"True, and your master is a shepherd."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Nay, that I can deny by a circumstance."],"id":36,"m":"No, I can disprove that with an argument."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["It shall go hard but I'll prove it by another."],"id":37,"m":"It'll go hard if I can't prove it with another."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["The shepherd seeks the sheep, and not the sheep the shepherd; but I","seek my master, and my master seeks not me. Therefore I am no sheep."],"id":38,"m":"The shepherd looks for the sheep, not the sheep for the shepherd. But I look for my master, and my master doesn't look for me. So I'm not a sheep."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["The sheep for fodder follow the shepherd; the shepherd for food follows","not the sheep. Thou for wages followest thy master; thy master for","wages follows not thee. Therefore thou art a sheep."],"id":39,"m":"The sheep follows the shepherd for food; the shepherd doesn't follow the sheep for food. You follow your master for wages; your master doesn't follow you for wages. So you are a sheep."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Such another proof will make me cry \"baa\"."],"id":40,"m":"One more proof like that and I'll be bleating \"baa\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["But dost thou hear? Gav'st thou my letter to Julia?"],"id":41,"m":"But listen, did you give my letter to Julia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Ay, sir. I, a lost mutton, gave your letter to her, a laced mutton, and","she, a laced mutton, gave me, a lost mutton, nothing for my labour."],"id":42,"m":"Yes, sir. I, a lost sheep, gave your letter to her, a fancy lady in a laced bodice, and she, the fancy lady, gave me, the lost sheep, nothing for my trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Here's too small a pasture for such store of muttons."],"id":43,"m":"There's too little pasture here for so many sheep."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["If the ground be overcharged, you were best stick her."],"id":44,"m":"If the field's overstocked, you'd better slaughter her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Nay, in that you are astray; 'twere best pound you."],"id":45,"m":"No, you've gone astray there; it would be better to pen you up in a pound."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Nay, sir, less than a pound shall serve me for carrying your letter."],"id":46,"m":"No, sir, less than a pound will do me as payment for carrying your letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["You mistake; I mean the pound, a pinfold."],"id":47,"m":"You've got it wrong. I mean a pound for animals, a pinfold."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["From a pound to a pin? Fold it over and over,","'Tis threefold too little for carrying a letter to your lover."],"id":48,"m":"From a pound to a pin? Fold it over and over, and it's still three times too little for carrying a letter to your lover."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["But what said she?"],"id":49,"m":"But what did she say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["[Nods his head] Ay."],"id":50,"m":"[Nods his head] Ay."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Nod—\"Ay\". Why, that's \"noddy\"."],"id":51,"m":"A nod and \"Ay\": that makes \"noddy\", a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["You mistook, sir. I say she did nod, and you ask me if she did nod; and","I say \"Ay\"."],"id":52,"m":"You've got it wrong, sir. I say she did nod, and you ask me if she nodded, and I say \"Ay\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["And that set together is \"noddy\"."],"id":53,"m":"And put together, that makes \"noddy\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Now you have taken the pains to set it together, take it for your","pains."],"id":54,"m":"Well, since you've taken the trouble to put it together, keep it for your trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["No, no, you shall have it for bearing the letter."],"id":55,"m":"No, no, you can have it for carrying the letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Well, I perceive I must be fain to bear with you."],"id":56,"m":"Well, I see I'll just have to put up with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Why, sir, how do you bear with me?"],"id":57,"m":"Why, sir, how are you putting up with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Marry, sir, the letter, very orderly, having nothing but the word","\"noddy\" for my pains."],"id":58,"m":"Well, sir, by carrying the letter properly and getting nothing but the word \"noddy\" for my trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Beshrew me, but you have a quick wit."],"id":59,"m":"Curse me, but you're quick-witted."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["And yet it cannot overtake your slow purse."],"id":60,"m":"And yet my wit can't catch up with your slow purse."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Come, come, open the matter; in brief, what said she?"],"id":61,"m":"Come on, come on, out with it. In short, what did she say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Open your purse, that the money and the matter may be both at once","delivered."],"id":62,"m":"Open your purse, so that the money and the news can be handed over at the same time."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["[Giving him a coin] Well, sir, here is for your pains. What said","she?"],"id":63,"m":"[Giving him a coin] Well, sir, here's something for your trouble. What did she say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Truly, sir, I think you'll hardly win her."],"id":64,"m":"Honestly, sir, I don't think you'll win her easily."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Why? Couldst thou perceive so much from her?"],"id":65,"m":"Why? Could you tell that much from her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Sir, I could perceive nothing at all from her; no, not so much as a","ducat for delivering your letter. And being so hard to me that brought","your mind, I fear she'll prove as hard to you in telling your mind.","Give her no token but stones, for she's as hard as steel."],"id":66,"m":"Sir, I couldn't get anything from her at all, not even a ducat for delivering your letter. And since she was so hard on me for bringing your feelings to her, I'm afraid she'll be just as hard on you when you tell her them in person. Don't give her any gifts but stones, because she's as hard as steel."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["What said she, nothing?"],"id":67,"m":"What, she said nothing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["No, not so much as \"Take this for thy pains.\" To testify your bounty, I","thank you, you have testerned me; in requital whereof, henceforth carry","your letters yourself. And so, sir, I'll commend you to my master."],"id":68,"m":"No, not even \"Take this for your trouble.\" To show how generous you are, thank you for tipping me sixpence. In return, from now on carry your letters yourself. And so, sir, I'll go and give my master your regards."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Go, go, begone, to save your ship from wrack,","Which cannot perish having thee aboard,","Being destined to a drier death on shore."],"id":69,"m":"Go on, go, be off and save your ship from being wrecked. It can't sink with you on board, because you're destined to die a drier death on land, by hanging."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Speed.","id":70},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I must go send some better messenger.","I fear my Julia would not deign my lines,","Receiving them from such a worthless post."],"cont":1,"id":71,"m":"I'll have to send a better messenger. I'm afraid my Julia wouldn't accept my letters from such a worthless postman."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":72},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. The garden of Julia's house.","id":73},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Julia and Lucetta.","id":74},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["But say, Lucetta, now we are alone,","Wouldst thou then counsel me to fall in love?"],"id":75,"m":"But tell me, Lucetta, now that we're alone, would you advise me to fall in love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Ay, madam, so you stumble not unheedfully."],"id":76,"m":"Yes, madam, as long as you don't stumble into it carelessly."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Of all the fair resort of gentlemen","That every day with parle encounter me,","In thy opinion which is worthiest love?"],"id":77,"m":"Of all the fine gentlemen who come every day to talk with me, which do you think most deserves my love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Please you, repeat their names, I'll show my mind","According to my shallow simple skill."],"id":78,"m":"If you'll repeat their names, I'll give you my opinion, as far as my small, simple judgement goes."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["What think'st thou of the fair Sir Eglamour?"],"id":79,"m":"What do you think of the handsome Sir Eglamour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["As of a knight well-spoken, neat, and fine;","But, were I you, he never should be mine."],"id":80,"m":"That he's a well-spoken knight, neat and elegant; but if I were you, he'd never be mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["What think'st thou of the rich Mercatio?"],"id":81,"m":"What do you think of the rich Mercatio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Well of his wealth; but of himself, so-so."],"id":82,"m":"I think well of his money, but of the man himself, so-so."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["What think'st thou of the gentle Proteus?"],"id":83,"m":"What do you think of the gentle Proteus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Lord, Lord, to see what folly reigns in us!"],"id":84,"m":"Lord, Lord, what fools we all are!"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["How now? What means this passion at his name?"],"id":85,"m":"What's this? Why such a reaction at his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Pardon, dear madam, 'tis a passing shame","That I, unworthy body as I am,","Should censure thus on lovely gentlemen."],"id":86,"m":"Forgive me, dear madam. It's a real shame that someone as lowly as I am should pass judgement like this on fine gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Why not on Proteus, as of all the rest?"],"id":87,"m":"Why not on Proteus, as well as all the others?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Then thus: of many good I think him best."],"id":88,"m":"Then here it is: of many good men, I think he's the best."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Your reason?"],"id":89,"m":"Your reason?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["I have no other but a woman's reason:","I think him so because I think him so."],"id":90,"m":"I have no reason but a woman's reason: I think so because I think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And wouldst thou have me cast my love on him?"],"id":91,"m":"And would you have me give my love to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Ay, if you thought your love not cast away."],"id":92,"m":"Yes, as long as you didn't think your love was being thrown away."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Why, he of all the rest hath never moved me."],"id":93,"m":"But of all of them, he's the only one who has never tried to win me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Yet he of all the rest I think best loves ye."],"id":94,"m":"Yet of all of them, I think he loves you best."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["His little speaking shows his love but small."],"id":95,"m":"He says so little, which shows his love is small."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Fire that's closest kept burns most of all."],"id":96,"m":"The fire that's most shut in burns hottest."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["They do not love that do not show their love."],"id":97,"m":"People who don't show their love don't love."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["O, they love least that let men know their love."],"id":98,"m":"Oh, the ones who let everyone know they're in love love least."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["I would I knew his mind."],"id":99,"m":"I wish I knew what he thought."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Peruse this paper, madam."],"id":100,"m":"Read this letter, madam."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives her a letter.","id":101},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["To Julia—Say, from whom?"],"id":102,"m":"\"To Julia.\" Tell me, who is it from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["That the contents will show."],"id":103,"m":"The letter will tell you that."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Say, say, who gave it thee?"],"id":104,"m":"Tell me, tell me, who gave it to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Sir Valentine's page, and sent, I think, from Proteus.","He would have given it you, but I, being in the way,","Did in your name receive it. Pardon the fault, I pray."],"id":105,"m":"Sir Valentine's servant, and sent, I think, by Proteus. He would have given it to you, but I happened to meet him and took it in your name. Please forgive me for doing so."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Now, by my modesty, a goodly broker!","Dare you presume to harbour wanton lines?","To whisper and conspire against my youth?","Now trust me, 'tis an office of great worth,","And you an officer fit for the place.","There, take the paper; see it be returned,","Or else return no more into my sight."],"id":106,"m":"Upon my honour, what a fine go-between you are! How dare you take in love letters? Whispering and plotting against my innocence? Believe me, that's a very respectable job, and you're just the right person for it. There, take the letter back and see it's returned, or never come into my sight again."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["To plead for love deserves more fee than hate."],"id":107,"m":"Pleading for love deserves a better reward than hate."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Will ye be gone?"],"id":108,"m":"Will you leave?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["That you may ruminate."],"id":109,"m":"Yes, so you can think it over."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":110},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And yet I would I had o'erlooked the letter.","It were a shame to call her back again","And pray her to a fault for which I chid her.","What fool is she, that knows I am a maid","And would not force the letter to my view,","Since maids in modesty say \"No\" to that","Which they would have the profferer construe \"Ay\".","Fie, fie, how wayward is this foolish love","That like a testy babe will scratch the nurse","And presently, all humbled, kiss the rod!","How churlishly I chid Lucetta hence,","When willingly I would have had her here!","How angerly I taught my brow to frown,","When inward joy enforced my heart to smile!","My penance is to call Lucetta back","And ask remission for my folly past.","What ho! Lucetta!"],"id":111,"m":"And yet I wish I'd read the letter. It would be embarrassing to call her back and beg her to do the very thing I scolded her for. What a fool she is! She knows I'm a girl, so why didn't she force me to read it? Girls say \"no\", out of modesty, to things they want the person offering to take as \"yes\". Honestly, how contrary this foolish love is! Like a bad-tempered baby, it scratches the nurse and then, all meek, kisses the stick it was smacked with. How rudely I drove Lucetta away, when I really wanted her to stay! How angrily I made myself frown, when inside I was so happy I wanted to smile! My punishment will be to call Lucetta back and ask forgiveness for my silliness. Hello there! Lucetta!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucetta.","id":112},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["What would your ladyship?"],"id":113,"m":"What does your ladyship want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Is 't near dinner time?"],"id":114,"m":"Is it nearly dinner time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["I would it were,","That you might kill your stomach on your meat","And not upon your maid."],"id":115,"m":"I wish it were, so you could take out your temper on your food and not on your maid."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drops and picks up the letter.","id":116},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["What is't that you took up so gingerly?"],"id":117,"m":"What's that you picked up so carefully?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Nothing."],"id":118,"m":"Nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Why didst thou stoop, then?"],"id":119,"m":"Then why did you bend down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["To take a paper up that I let fall."],"id":120,"m":"To pick up a piece of paper I dropped."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And is that paper nothing?"],"id":121,"m":"And is that paper nothing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Nothing concerning me."],"id":122,"m":"Nothing to do with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Then let it lie for those that it concerns."],"id":123,"m":"Then leave it lying there for the people it does concern."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Madam, it will not lie where it concerns,","Unless it have a false interpreter."],"id":124,"m":"Madam, it won't lie about the person it concerns, unless someone misreads it."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Some love of yours hath writ to you in rhyme."],"id":125,"m":"Some lover of yours has written to you in verse."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["That I might sing it, madam, to a tune.","Give me a note. Your ladyship can set—"],"id":126,"m":"So that I could sing it to a tune, madam. Give me a note. Your ladyship can set—"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["As little by such toys as may be possible.","Best sing it to the tune of \"Light o' Love\"."],"id":127,"m":"As little store by such trifles as possible. You'd better sing it to the tune of \"Light o' Love\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["It is too heavy for so light a tune."],"id":128,"m":"It's too heavy for such a light tune."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Heavy? Belike it hath some burden then?"],"id":129,"m":"Heavy? Then I suppose it has a weighty refrain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Ay, and melodious were it, would you sing it."],"id":130,"m":"Yes, and it would sound lovely if you sang it."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And why not you?"],"id":131,"m":"Then why don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["I cannot reach so high."],"id":132,"m":"I can't reach so high."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Let's see your song. [Taking the letter]","How now, minion!"],"id":133,"m":"Let me see your song. [Taking the letter] What's this, you hussy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Keep tune there still, so you will sing it out.","And yet methinks I do not like this tune."],"id":134,"m":"Keep to that tune and you'll sing it to the end. And yet I don't think I like this tune."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["You do not?"],"id":135,"m":"You don't?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["No, madam, it is too sharp."],"id":136,"m":"No, madam, it's too sharp."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["You, minion, are too saucy."],"id":137,"m":"And you, girl, are too cheeky."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Nay, now you are too flat","And mar the concord with too harsh a descant.","There wanteth but a mean to fill your song."],"id":138,"m":"No, now you're too flat, and you spoil the harmony with such a harsh line. All your song needs is a middle part, a tenor, to fill it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["The mean is drowned with your unruly bass."],"id":139,"m":"The middle part is drowned out by your unruly bass."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Indeed, I bid the base for Proteus."],"id":140,"m":"Well, I'm speaking up for Proteus."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["This babble shall not henceforth trouble me.","Here is a coil with protestation! [Tears the letter]","Go, get you gone, and let the papers lie.","You would be fingering them to anger me."],"id":141,"m":"This chatter won't bother me any more. What a fuss about a love letter! [Tears the letter] Go on, get out, and leave the papers lying there. You'd fiddle with them just to annoy me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["She makes it strange, but she would be best pleased","To be so angered with another letter."],"id":142,"m":"She's acting distant, but she'd be delighted to be made this angry by another letter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":143},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Nay, would I were so angered with the same!","O hateful hands, to tear such loving words!","Injurious wasps, to feed on such sweet honey","And kill the bees that yield it with your stings!","I'll kiss each several paper for amends.","Look, here is writ kind Julia. Unkind Julia!","As in revenge of thy ingratitude,","I throw thy name against the bruising stones,","Trampling contemptuously on thy disdain.","And here is writ love-wounded Proteus.","Poor wounded name, my bosom as a bed","Shall lodge thee till thy wound be throughly healed;","And thus I search it with a sovereign kiss.","But twice or thrice was Proteus written down.","Be calm, good wind, blow not a word away","Till I have found each letter in the letter","Except mine own name. That some whirlwind bear","Unto a ragged, fearful, hanging rock,","And throw it thence into the raging sea.","Lo, here in one line is his name twice writ:","Poor forlorn Proteus, passionate Proteus,","To the sweet Julia. That I'll tear away;","And yet I will not, sith so prettily","He couples it to his complaining names.","Thus will I fold them one upon another.","Now kiss, embrace, contend, do what you will."],"id":144,"m":"No, I wish I were angry with this same one! Oh, hateful hands, to tear up such loving words! Spiteful wasps, to feed on such sweet honey and kill the bees that make it with your stings! I'll kiss each scrap of paper to make up for it. Look, here it says \"kind Julia\". Unkind Julia! To punish you for being so ungrateful, I'll throw your name against the hard stones and trample on your scorn. And here it says \"love-wounded Proteus\". Poor wounded name, my breast will be your bed until your wound is fully healed, and I'll treat it with a healing kiss. But \"Proteus\" was written two or three times. Be calm, good wind, don't blow away a single word until I've found every letter in the letter, except my own name. Let a whirlwind carry that off to a jagged, terrifying, overhanging rock and throw it from there into the raging sea. Look, here's his name twice in one line: \"Poor forlorn Proteus, passionate Proteus, to the sweet Julia.\" I'll tear that part off. And yet I won't, since he joins it so prettily to his own sad names. I'll fold them one on top of the other like this. Now kiss, embrace, wrestle, do whatever you like."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucetta.","id":145},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Madam, dinner is ready, and your father stays."],"id":146,"m":"Madam, dinner is ready, and your father is waiting."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Well, let us go."],"id":147,"m":"All right, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["What, shall these papers lie like tell-tales here?"],"id":148,"m":"What, are you going to leave these papers lying here to tell tales?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["If you respect them, best to take them up."],"id":149,"m":"If you care about them, you'd better pick them up."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Nay, I was taken up for laying them down.","Yet here they shall not lie, for catching cold."],"id":150,"m":"No, I was told off for putting them down. But they mustn't lie here and catch cold."},{"t":"sd","text":"Picks up pieces of the letter.","id":151},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["I see you have a month's mind to them."],"id":152,"m":"I can see you have a real hankering for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Ay, madam, you may say what sights you see;","I see things too, although you judge I wink."],"id":153,"m":"Yes, madam, you can say whatever you think you see. I see things too, though you think I'm turning a blind eye."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Come, come, will't please you go?"],"id":154,"m":"Come on, come on, will you come?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":155},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A room in Antonio's house.","id":156},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antonio and Pantino.","id":157},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Tell me, Pantino, what sad talk was that","Wherewith my brother held you in the cloister?"],"id":158,"m":"Tell me, Pantino, what was that serious talk my brother was having with you in the cloister?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["'Twas of his nephew Proteus, your son."],"id":159,"m":"It was about his nephew Proteus, your son."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Why, what of him?"],"id":160,"m":"Why, what about him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["He wondered that your lordship","Would suffer him to spend his youth at home","While other men, of slender reputation,","Put forth their sons to seek preferment out:","Some to the wars to try their fortune there;","Some to discover islands far away;","Some to the studious universities.","For any or for all these exercises","He said that Proteus your son was meet,","And did request me to importune you","To let him spend his time no more at home,","Which would be great impeachment to his age","In having known no travel in his youth."],"id":161,"m":"He wondered why your lordship lets him spend his youth at home, while other men of much lower standing send their sons out to make their way in the world: some to the wars, to try their luck there; some to discover islands far away; some to the universities to study. He said your son Proteus was fit for any or all of these, and he asked me to urge you not to let him stay at home any longer. It would be a great disadvantage to him when he's older if he'd never travelled when young."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Nor need'st thou much importune me to that","Whereon this month I have been hammering.","I have considered well his loss of time,","And how he cannot be a perfect man,","Not being tried and tutored in the world.","Experience is by industry achieved","And perfected by the swift course of time.","Then tell me whither were I best to send him?"],"id":162,"m":"You don't need to urge me much about that. I've been turning it over all month. I've thought hard about how he's wasting his time, and how he can't become a complete man without being tested and taught by the world. Experience is gained by hard work and perfected by the passing of time. So tell me, where would be best to send him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["I think your lordship is not ignorant","How his companion, youthful Valentine,","Attends the Emperor in his royal court."],"id":163,"m":"I think your lordship knows that his friend, young Valentine, is serving the Emperor at his royal court."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I know it well."],"id":164,"m":"I know that well."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["'Twere good, I think, your lordship sent him thither.","There shall he practise tilts and tournaments,","Hear sweet discourse, converse with noblemen,","And be in eye of every exercise","Worthy his youth and nobleness of birth."],"id":165,"m":"I think it would be good if your lordship sent him there. He could practise jousting and tournaments, hear fine conversation, mix with noblemen, and see every activity fit for a young man of his noble birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I like thy counsel; well hast thou advised,","And that thou mayst perceive how well I like it,","The execution of it shall make known.","Even with the speediest expedition","I will dispatch him to the Emperor's court."],"id":166,"m":"I like your advice; you've advised me well. And to show you how much I like it, I'll act on it. I'll send him to the Emperor's court as fast as possible."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Tomorrow, may it please you, Don Alphonso","With other gentlemen of good esteem","Are journeying to salute the Emperor","And to commend their service to his will."],"id":167,"m":"Tomorrow, if it suits you, Don Alphonso and some other well-respected gentlemen are travelling to pay their respects to the Emperor and offer him their service."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Good company. With them shall Proteus go."],"id":168,"m":"Good company. Proteus will go with them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus reading a letter.","id":169},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["And in good time! Now will we break with him."],"cont":1,"id":170,"m":"And just at the right moment! Now we'll tell him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sweet love, sweet lines, sweet life!","Here is her hand, the agent of her heart;","Here is her oath for love, her honour's pawn.","O, that our fathers would applaud our loves","To seal our happiness with their consents.","O heavenly Julia!"],"id":171,"m":"Sweet love, sweet words, sweet life! Here is her handwriting, which speaks for her heart; here is her vow of love, which she pledges on her honour. Oh, if only our fathers would approve of our love and seal our happiness with their consent. Oh, heavenly Julia!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["How now? What letter are you reading there?"],"id":172,"m":"What's this? What letter are you reading there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["May't please your lordship, 'tis a word or two","Of commendations sent from Valentine,","Delivered by a friend that came from him."],"id":173,"m":"If it please your lordship, it's a few lines of greetings from Valentine, brought by a friend who came from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Lend me the letter. Let me see what news."],"id":174,"m":"Let me have the letter. Let me see the news."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["There is no news, my lord, but that he writes","How happily he lives, how well beloved","And daily graced by the Emperor,","Wishing me with him, partner of his fortune."],"id":175,"m":"There's no news, my lord, except that he writes about how happily he's living, how well loved he is and how the Emperor favours him every day. He wishes I were with him, to share his good fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["And how stand you affected to his wish?"],"id":176,"m":"And how do you feel about his wish?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["As one relying on your lordship's will,","And not depending on his friendly wish."],"id":177,"m":"As someone who depends on your lordship's wishes, not on his friendly one."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["My will is something sorted with his wish.","Muse not that I thus suddenly proceed,","For what I will, I will, and there an end.","I am resolved that thou shalt spend some time","With Valentinus in the Emperor's court.","What maintenance he from his friends receives,","Like exhibition thou shalt have from me.","Tomorrow be in readiness to go.","Excuse it not, for I am peremptory."],"id":178,"m":"My wishes rather agree with his. Don't be surprised that I'm acting so suddenly, because what I want, I want, and that's the end of it. I've decided that you'll spend some time with Valentine at the Emperor's court. Whatever allowance his family gives him, you'll have the same from me. Be ready to leave tomorrow. Don't make excuses, because my mind is made up."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["My lord, I cannot be so soon provided;","Please you deliberate a day or two."],"id":179,"m":"My lord, I can't get ready so soon. Please think about it for a day or two."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Look what thou want'st shall be sent after thee.","No more of stay. Tomorrow thou must go.","Come on, Pantino, you shall be employed","To hasten on his expedition."],"id":180,"m":"Whatever you need will be sent after you. No more delay. You must go tomorrow. Come on, Pantino, you'll be put to work hurrying on his departure."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Antonio and Pantino.","id":181},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Thus have I shunned the fire for fear of burning","And drenched me in the sea, where I am drowned.","I feared to show my father Julia's letter","Lest he should take exceptions to my love,","And with the vantage of mine own excuse","Hath he excepted most against my love.","O, how this spring of love resembleth","The uncertain glory of an April day,","Which now shows all the beauty of the sun,","And by an by a cloud takes all away."],"id":182,"m":"So I've avoided the fire for fear of being burned, and jumped into the sea, where I'm drowning. I was afraid to show my father Julia's letter in case he objected to my love, and he has used my own excuse against me and done the most damage to my love. Oh, how this springtime of love is like the changeable glory of an April day, which one moment shows all the beauty of the sun, and the next a cloud takes it all away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pantino.","id":183},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Sir Proteus, your father calls for you.","He is in haste. Therefore, I pray you, go."],"id":184,"m":"Sir Proteus, your father is calling for you. He's in a hurry, so please go."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Why, this it is: my heart accords thereto,","And yet a thousand times it answers \"No\"."],"id":185,"m":"Well, that's how it is: my heart agrees to go, and yet a thousand times it says \"No\"."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":186},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Milan. A room in the Duke's palace.","id":187},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine and Speed.","id":188},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Sir, your glove."],"id":189,"m":"Sir, your glove."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Not mine. My gloves are on."],"id":190,"m":"Not mine. My gloves are on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why, then, this may be yours, for this is but one."],"id":191,"m":"Well then, this could be yours, because it's only one."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Ha? Let me see. Ay, give it me, it's mine.","Sweet ornament that decks a thing divine!","Ah, Silvia, Silvia!"],"id":192,"m":"What? Let me see. Yes, give it to me, it's mine. Sweet ornament that adorns a heavenly thing! Ah, Silvia, Silvia!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["[Calling] Madam Silvia! Madam Silvia!"],"id":193,"m":"[Calling] Madam Silvia! Madam Silvia!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["How now, sirrah?"],"id":194,"m":"What are you doing, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["She is not within hearing, sir."],"id":195,"m":"She's out of earshot, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why, sir, who bade you call her?"],"id":196,"m":"Why, sir, who told you to call her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Your worship, sir, or else I mistook."],"id":197,"m":"Your worship did, sir, unless I misunderstood."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Well, you'll still be too forward."],"id":198,"m":"Well, you're always too pushy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["And yet I was last chidden for being too slow."],"id":199,"m":"And yet last time I was told off for being too slow."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Go to, sir. Tell me, do you know Madam Silvia?"],"id":200,"m":"That's enough, sir. Tell me, do you know Madam Silvia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["She that your worship loves?"],"id":201,"m":"The one your worship loves?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why, how know you that I am in love?"],"id":202,"m":"Why, how do you know I'm in love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Marry, by these special marks: first, you have learned, like Sir","Proteus, to wreathe your arms like a malcontent; to relish a love-song,","like a robin redbreast; to walk alone, like one that had the","pestilence; to sigh, like a schoolboy that had lost his ABC; to weep,","like a young wench that had buried her grandam; to fast, like one that","takes diet; to watch, like one that fears robbing; to speak puling,","like a beggar at Hallowmas. You were wont, when you laughed, to crow","like a cock; when you walked, to walk like one of the lions; when you","fasted, it was presently after dinner; when you looked sadly, it was","for want of money. And now you are metamorphosed with a mistress, that,","when I look on you, I can hardly think you my master."],"id":203,"m":"Well, by these special signs. First, you've learned, like Sir Proteus, to fold your arms like a gloomy malcontent; to enjoy a love song, like a robin redbreast; to walk alone, like someone with the plague; to sigh, like a schoolboy who's lost his alphabet book; to weep, like a young girl who's buried her grandmother; to fast, like someone on a diet; to stay up all night, like someone afraid of burglars; to whine, like a beggar on All Saints' Day. You used to crow like a cockerel when you laughed, and swagger like a lion when you walked. When you fasted, it was right after dinner; when you looked sad, it was because you were short of money. And now a mistress has transformed you so much that when I look at you, I can hardly believe you're my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Are all these things perceived in me?"],"id":204,"m":"Can people see all these things in me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["They are all perceived without ye."],"id":205,"m":"They can all be seen from outside you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Without me? They cannot."],"id":206,"m":"Outside me? They can't."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Without you? Nay, that's certain, for without you were so simple, none","else would. But you are so without these follies, that these follies","are within you, and shine through you like the water in an urinal, that","not an eye that sees you but is a physician to comment on your malady."],"id":207,"m":"Outside you? No, that's certain, because no one but you would be so foolish. But you are so wrapped in these follies that they're inside you and shine through you, like urine in a doctor's glass, so that everyone who sees you becomes a doctor and diagnoses your illness."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["But tell me, dost thou know my lady Silvia?"],"id":208,"m":"But tell me, do you know my lady Silvia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["She that you gaze on so as she sits at supper?"],"id":209,"m":"The one you stare at like that while she's sitting at supper?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Hast thou observed that? Even she I mean."],"id":210,"m":"Have you noticed that? Yes, she's the one I mean."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why, sir, I know her not."],"id":211,"m":"Why, sir, I don't know her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Dost thou know her by my gazing on her, and yet know'st her not?"],"id":212,"m":"You know her from my staring at her, and yet you don't know her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Is she not hard-favoured, sir?"],"id":213,"m":"Isn't she rather ugly, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Not so fair, boy, as well-favoured."],"id":214,"m":"She's not so much beautiful, boy, as gracious."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Sir, I know that well enough."],"id":215,"m":"Sir, I know that well enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["What dost thou know?"],"id":216,"m":"What do you know?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["That she is not so fair as, of you, well-favoured."],"id":217,"m":"That she's not as beautiful as you think she's gracious."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I mean that her beauty is exquisite but her favour infinite."],"id":218,"m":"I mean her beauty is exquisite, but her grace is infinite."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["That's because the one is painted, and the other out of all count."],"id":219,"m":"That's because one is painted on, and the other is beyond all counting."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["How painted? And how out of count?"],"id":220,"m":"What do you mean, painted? And beyond counting?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Marry, sir, so painted to make her fair, that no man counts of her","beauty."],"id":221,"m":"Well, sir, she's so painted with make-up to make her beautiful that no man takes her beauty into account."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["How esteem'st thou me? I account of her beauty."],"id":222,"m":"What do you take me for? I value her beauty."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["You never saw her since she was deformed."],"id":223,"m":"You've never seen her since she was disfigured."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["How long hath she been deformed?"],"id":224,"m":"How long has she been disfigured?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Ever since you loved her."],"id":225,"m":"Ever since you fell in love with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I have loved her ever since I saw her, and still I see her beautiful."],"id":226,"m":"I've loved her ever since I first saw her, and I still see her as beautiful."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["If you love her, you cannot see her."],"id":227,"m":"If you love her, you can't see her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why?"],"id":228,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Because Love is blind. O, that you had mine eyes, or your own eyes had","the lights they were wont to have when you chid at Sir Proteus for","going ungartered!"],"id":229,"m":"Because Love is blind. Oh, I wish you had my eyes, or that your own eyes were as clear as they were when you used to tell Sir Proteus off for going around with his stockings ungartered!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["What should I see then?"],"id":230,"m":"What would I see then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Your own present folly and her passing deformity; for he, being in","love, could not see to garter his hose; and you, being in love, cannot","see to put on your hose."],"id":231,"m":"Your own present foolishness and her extreme ugliness. Because he, being in love, couldn't see to tie up his stockings; and you, being in love, can't see to put yours on."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Belike, boy, then you are in love, for last morning you could not see","to wipe my shoes."],"id":232,"m":"Then I suppose you're in love, boy, because yesterday morning you couldn't see to clean my shoes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["True, sir, I was in love with my bed. I thank you, you swinged me for","my love, which makes me the bolder to chide you for yours."],"id":233,"m":"True, sir, I was in love with my bed. Thank you for beating me for my love; it makes me bolder about telling you off for yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["In conclusion, I stand affected to her."],"id":234,"m":"In short, I am in love with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["I would you were set, so your affection would cease."],"id":235,"m":"I wish you'd sit down, so your affection would stop."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Last night she enjoined me to write some lines to one she loves."],"id":236,"m":"Last night she asked me to write some lines to someone she loves."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["And have you?"],"id":237,"m":"And have you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I have."],"id":238,"m":"I have."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Are they not lamely writ?"],"id":239,"m":"Aren't they badly written?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No, boy, but as well as I can do them.","Peace, here she comes."],"id":240,"m":"No, boy, they're as good as I can make them. Quiet, here she comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Silvia.","id":241},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["[Aside] O excellent motion! O exceeding puppet!","Now will he interpret to her."],"id":242,"m":"[Aside] Oh, what a great puppet show! Oh, what a lovely puppet! Now he'll speak her lines for her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Madam and mistress, a thousand good-morrows."],"id":243,"m":"Madam and mistress, a thousand good mornings."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["[Aside] O, give ye good e'en! Here's a million of manners."],"id":244,"m":"[Aside] Oh, why not good evening too! Here's a million manners."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Sir Valentine and servant, to you two thousand."],"id":245,"m":"Sir Valentine, my servant, two thousand to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["[Aside] He should give her interest, and she gives it him."],"id":246,"m":"[Aside] He ought to pay her the interest, and she's paying it to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["As you enjoined me, I have writ your letter","Unto the secret nameless friend of yours,","Which I was much unwilling to proceed in","But for my duty to your ladyship."],"id":247,"m":"As you asked me, I've written your letter to your secret, nameless friend. I was very unwilling to do it, except for my duty to your ladyship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives her a letter.","id":248},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["I thank you, gentle servant, 'tis very clerkly done."],"id":249,"m":"Thank you, gentle servant. It's very skilfully done."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Now trust me, madam, it came hardly off,","For, being ignorant to whom it goes,","I writ at random, very doubtfully."],"id":250,"m":"Believe me, madam, it was hard to do, because not knowing who it's going to, I wrote at random, very uncertainly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Perchance you think too much of so much pains?"],"id":251,"m":"Perhaps you think it was too much trouble?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No, madam; so it stead you, I will write,","Please you command, a thousand times as much.","And yet—"],"id":252,"m":"No, madam. If it helps you, I'll write a thousand times as much, if you order me to. And yet—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["A pretty period. Well, I guess the sequel;","And yet I will not name it. And yet I care not.","And yet take this again."],"id":253,"m":"A nice place to stop. Well, I can guess what follows, and yet I won't say it. And yet I don't care. And yet, take this back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Offers him the letter.","id":254},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["And yet I thank you,","Meaning henceforth to trouble you no more."],"cont":1,"id":255,"m":"And yet I thank you, though I mean not to trouble you again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["[Aside] And yet you will; and yet another \"yet\"."],"id":256,"m":"[Aside] And yet you will; and there's another \"yet\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["What means your ladyship? Do you not like it?"],"id":257,"m":"What does your ladyship mean? Don't you like it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Yes, yes, the lines are very quaintly writ,","But, since unwillingly, take them again.","Nay, take them."],"id":258,"m":"Yes, yes, the lines are very cleverly written, but since you wrote them unwillingly, take them back. No, take them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Offers the letter again.","id":259},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Madam, they are for you."],"id":260,"m":"Madam, they're for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Ay, ay, you writ them, sir, at my request,","But I will none of them. They are for you.","I would have had them writ more movingly."],"id":261,"m":"Yes, yes, you wrote them, sir, because I asked you to, but I don't want them. They're for you. I would have liked them written with more feeling."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Please you, I'll write your ladyship another."],"id":262,"m":"If you like, I'll write your ladyship another."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["And when it's writ, for my sake read it over,","And if it please you, so; if not, why, so."],"id":263,"m":"And when it's written, read it through for my sake. If you like it, good; if not, well, never mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["If it please me, madam? What then?"],"id":264,"m":"If I like it, madam? What then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Why, if it please you, take it for your labour.","And so good morrow, servant."],"id":265,"m":"Why, if you like it, keep it as payment for your trouble. And so good morning, servant."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":266},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["[Aside] O jest unseen, inscrutable, invisible,","As a nose on a man's face, or a weathercock on a steeple!","My master sues to her, and she hath taught her suitor,","He being her pupil, to become her tutor.","O excellent device! Was there ever heard a better?","That my master, being scribe, to himself should write the letter?"],"id":267,"m":"[Aside] Oh, what a joke, hidden, mysterious, invisible, as plain as the nose on a man's face or a weathervane on a steeple! My master is wooing her, and she has taught her suitor, her own pupil, to become her teacher. Oh, what a brilliant trick! Was there ever a better one? My master is the secretary, and he has to write the letter to himself!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["How now, sir? What are you reasoning with yourself?"],"id":268,"m":"What's this, sir? What are you arguing about with yourself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Nay, I was rhyming. 'Tis you that have the reason."],"id":269,"m":"No, I was rhyming. It's you who have the reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["To do what?"],"id":270,"m":"To do what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["To be a spokesman from Madam Silvia."],"id":271,"m":"To be Madam Silvia's spokesman."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["To whom?"],"id":272,"m":"To whom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["To yourself. Why, she woos you by a figure."],"id":273,"m":"To yourself. Why, she's wooing you in a roundabout way."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["What figure?"],"id":274,"m":"What way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["By a letter, I should say."],"id":275,"m":"By letter, I should say."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why, she hath not writ to me."],"id":276,"m":"But she hasn't written to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["What need she, when she hath made you write to yourself? Why, do you","not perceive the jest?"],"id":277,"m":"Why should she, when she's made you write to yourself? Why, don't you see the joke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No, believe me."],"id":278,"m":"No, believe me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["No believing you indeed, sir. But did you perceive her earnest?"],"id":279,"m":"There's no believing you, sir. But did you see she was in earnest?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["She gave me none, except an angry word."],"id":280,"m":"She gave me nothing but an angry word."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why, she hath given you a letter."],"id":281,"m":"Why, she's given you a letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["That's the letter I writ to her friend."],"id":282,"m":"That's the letter I wrote to her friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["And that letter hath she delivered, and there an end."],"id":283,"m":"And she's delivered that letter, and that's that."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I would it were no worse."],"id":284,"m":"I wish that was all it meant."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["I'll warrant you, 'tis as well.","For often have you writ to her, and she, in modesty","Or else for want of idle time, could not again reply,","Or fearing else some messenger that might her mind discover,","Herself hath taught her love himself to write unto her lover.","All this I speak in print, for in print I found it. Why muse you, sir?","'Tis dinner time."],"id":285,"m":"I promise you, it's as good as that. Because you've often written to her, and she, out of modesty or for lack of spare time, couldn't reply; or else, afraid that a messenger might give away her feelings, she has taught her lover to write to her lover himself. I'm telling you this word for word, as if I'd read it in a book. Why are you brooding, sir? It's dinner time."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I have dined."],"id":286,"m":"I've already eaten."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Ay, but hearken, sir, though the chameleon Love can feed on the air, I","am one that am nourished by my victuals, and would fain have meat. O,","be not like your mistress! Be moved, be moved."],"id":287,"m":"Yes, but listen, sir. Love may be like the chameleon and live on air, but I'm someone who's fed by food, and I'd like my dinner. Oh, don't be like your mistress! Be moved, be moved."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":288},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Verona. A room in Julia's house.","id":289},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus and Julia.","id":290},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Have patience, gentle Julia."],"id":291,"m":"Be patient, gentle Julia."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["I must, where is no remedy."],"id":292,"m":"I have to be, when nothing can be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["When possibly I can, I will return."],"id":293,"m":"I'll come back as soon as I possibly can."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["If you turn not, you will return the sooner.","Keep this remembrance for thy Julia's sake."],"id":294,"m":"If you don't change, you'll come back sooner. Keep this as a reminder of your Julia."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives him a ring.","id":295},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Why, then we'll make exchange. Here, take you this."],"id":296,"m":"Why, then let's exchange. Here, you take this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives her a ring.","id":297},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And seal the bargain with a holy kiss."],"id":298,"m":"And seal the bargain with a holy kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Here is my hand for my true constancy.","And when that hour o'erslips me in the day","Wherein I sigh not, Julia, for thy sake,","The next ensuing hour some foul mischance","Torment me for my love's forgetfulness.","My father stays my coming; answer not.","The tide is now—nay, not thy tide of tears,","That tide will stay me longer than I should.","Julia, farewell."],"id":299,"m":"Here's my hand as a pledge that I'll stay true. And if an hour of any day slips past in which I don't sigh for you, Julia, let some terrible misfortune torment me the very next hour for forgetting my love. My father is waiting for me; don't answer. The tide is turning, no, not your tide of tears; that tide would keep me here longer than I should stay. Julia, farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Julia.","id":300},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["What, gone without a word?","Ay, so true love should do. It cannot speak,","For truth hath better deeds than words to grace it."],"cont":1,"id":301,"m":"What, gone without a word? Yes, that's what true love should do. It can't speak, because truth is better honoured by actions than by words."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pantino.","id":302},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Sir Proteus, you are stayed for."],"id":303,"m":"Sir Proteus, they're waiting for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Go, I come, I come.","Alas, this parting strikes poor lovers dumb."],"id":304,"m":"Go on, I'm coming, I'm coming. Oh, this parting leaves poor lovers speechless."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":305},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A street.","id":306},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lance with his dog Crab.","id":307},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Nay, 'twill be this hour ere I have done weeping; all the kind of the","Lances have this very fault. I have received my proportion, like the","prodigious son, and am going with Sir Proteus to the Imperial's court.","I think Crab my dog be the sourest-natured dog that lives: my mother","weeping, my father wailing, my sister crying, our maid howling, our cat","wringing her hands, and all our house in a great perplexity, yet did","not this cruel-hearted cur shed one tear. He is a stone, a very","pebblestone, and has no more pity in him than a dog. A Jew would have","wept to have seen our parting. Why, my grandam, having no eyes, look","you, wept herself blind at my parting. Nay, I'll show you the manner of","it. This shoe is my father. No, this left shoe is my father; no, no,","this left shoe is my mother. Nay, that cannot be so neither. Yes, it is","so, it is so; it hath the worser sole. This shoe with the hole in it is","my mother, and this my father. A vengeance on 't, there 'tis. Now, sir,","this staff is my sister, for, look you, she is as white as a lily and","as small as a wand. This hat is Nan, our maid. I am the dog. No, the","dog is himself, and I am the dog. O, the dog is me, and I am myself.","Ay, so, so. Now come I to my father: \"Father, your blessing.\" Now","should not the shoe speak a word for weeping. Now should I kiss my","father. Well, he weeps on. Now come I to my mother. O, that she could","speak now like a wood woman! Well, I kiss her. Why there 'tis; here's","my mother's breath up and down. Now come I to my sister. Mark the moan","she makes. Now the dog all this while sheds not a tear nor speaks a","word; but see how I lay the dust with my tears."],"id":308,"m":"No, it'll be an hour before I've finished crying. All the Lance family have this same fault. I've had my share of the inheritance, like the prodigal son, and I'm going with Sir Proteus to the Emperor's court. I think my dog Crab is the hardest-hearted dog alive. My mother was weeping, my father wailing, my sister crying, our maid howling, our cat wringing her hands and our whole house in a terrible state, and yet this cruel-hearted mutt didn't shed one tear. He's a stone, a real pebble, and he's got no more pity in him than a dog. Even a Jew would have wept to see us part. Why, my grandmother, who's got no eyes, look, cried herself blind when I left. No, I'll show you how it went. This shoe is my father. No, this left shoe is my father; no, no, this left shoe is my mother. No, that can't be right either. Yes, it is, it is: it's got the worse sole. This shoe with the hole in it is my mother, and this one is my father. Curse it, there it is. Now, sir, this stick is my sister, because, look, she's as white as a lily and as thin as a twig. This hat is Nan, our maid. I'm the dog. No, the dog is himself, and I'm the dog. Oh, the dog is me, and I'm myself. Yes, that's it, that's it. Now I come to my father: \"Father, your blessing.\" Now the shoe should be too choked with tears to say a word. Now I should kiss my father. Well, he keeps on weeping. Now I come to my mother. Oh, if only she could talk now like a woman out of her mind! Well, I kiss her. Why, there it is: that's my mother's breath exactly. Now I come to my sister. Listen to her moaning. And all this time the dog doesn't shed a tear or say a word; but look how I'm settling the dust with my tears."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pantino.","id":309},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Lance, away, away! Aboard! Thy master is shipped, and thou art to post","after with oars. What's the matter? Why weep'st thou, man? Away, ass.","You'll lose the tide if you tarry any longer."],"id":310,"m":"Lance, go, go! Get on board! Your master is on the ship, and you'll have to row after him. What's the matter? Why are you crying, man? Off with you, you ass. You'll miss the tide if you wait any longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["It is no matter if the tied were lost, for it is the unkindest tied","that ever any man tied."],"id":311,"m":"It wouldn't matter if the tied one were lost, because he's the cruellest tied one that anyone ever tied."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["What's the unkindest tide?"],"id":312,"m":"What's the cruellest tide?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, he that's tied here, Crab, my dog."],"id":313,"m":"Why, the one that's tied up here: Crab, my dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Tut, man, I mean thou'lt lose the flood, and, in losing the flood, lose","thy voyage, and, in losing thy voyage, lose thy master, and, in losing","thy master, lose thy service, and, in losing thy service—why dost thou","stop my mouth?"],"id":314,"m":"Nonsense, man, I mean you'll miss the high tide, and by missing the tide, miss your voyage, and by missing your voyage, lose your master, and by losing your master, lose your job, and by losing your job— Why are you covering my mouth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["For fear thou shouldst lose thy tongue."],"id":315,"m":"In case you lose your tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Where should I lose my tongue?"],"id":316,"m":"Where would I lose my tongue?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["In thy tale."],"id":317,"m":"In your tale."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["In thy tail!"],"id":318,"m":"In your tail, you mean!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Lose the tide, and the voyage, and the master, and the service, and the","tied? Why, man, if the river were dry, I am able to fill it with my","tears; if the wind were down, I could drive the boat with my sighs."],"id":319,"m":"Lose the tide, and the voyage, and the master, and the job, and the tied one? Why, man, if the river were dry, I could fill it with my tears; if the wind dropped, I could drive the boat with my sighs."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Come, come away, man. I was sent to call thee."],"id":320,"m":"Come on, come away, man. I was sent to call you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Sir, call me what thou dar'st."],"id":321,"m":"Sir, call me whatever you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANTINO","lines":["Will thou go?"],"id":322,"m":"Will you go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Well, I will go."],"id":323,"m":"All right, I'll go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":324},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"Milan. A room in the Duke's palace.","id":325},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine, Silvia, Thurio and Speed.","id":326},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Servant!"],"id":327,"m":"Servant!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Mistress?"],"id":328,"m":"Mistress?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Master, Sir Thurio frowns on you."],"id":329,"m":"Master, Sir Thurio is frowning at you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Ay, boy, it's for love."],"id":330,"m":"Yes, boy, it's because of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Not of you."],"id":331,"m":"Not love of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Of my mistress, then."],"id":332,"m":"Of my mistress, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["'Twere good you knocked him."],"id":333,"m":"It would be a good idea to knock him down."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Servant, you are sad."],"id":334,"m":"Servant, you look sad."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Indeed, madam, I seem so."],"id":335,"m":"Indeed, madam, I seem so."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Seem you that you are not?"],"id":336,"m":"Do you seem to be what you're not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Haply I do."],"id":337,"m":"Perhaps I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["So do counterfeits."],"id":338,"m":"So do fakes."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["So do you."],"id":339,"m":"So do you."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["What seem I that I am not?"],"id":340,"m":"What do I seem to be that I'm not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Wise."],"id":341,"m":"Wise."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["What instance of the contrary?"],"id":342,"m":"What proof is there of the opposite?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Your folly."],"id":343,"m":"Your foolishness."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["And how quote you my folly?"],"id":344,"m":"And where do you see my foolishness?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I quote it in your jerkin."],"id":345,"m":"I see it in your jacket."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["My jerkin is a doublet."],"id":346,"m":"My jacket is a doublet."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Well, then, I'll double your folly."],"id":347,"m":"Well then, I'll double your foolishness."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["How!"],"id":348,"m":"What!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["What, angry, Sir Thurio? Do you change colour?"],"id":349,"m":"What, angry, Sir Thurio? Are you changing colour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Give him leave, madam, he is a kind of chameleon."],"id":350,"m":"Let him be, madam. He's a sort of chameleon."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["That hath more mind to feed on your blood than live in your air."],"id":351,"m":"One that would rather feed on your blood than live on your air."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["You have said, sir."],"id":352,"m":"You've had your say, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Ay, sir, and done too, for this time."],"id":353,"m":"Yes, sir, and I've done, for now."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I know it well, sir. You always end ere you begin."],"id":354,"m":"I know that well, sir. You always finish before you start."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["A fine volley of words, gentlemen, and quickly shot off."],"id":355,"m":"A fine volley of words, gentlemen, and quickly fired."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["'Tis indeed, madam, we thank the giver."],"id":356,"m":"It is indeed, madam, and we thank the person who supplied it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Who is that, servant?"],"id":357,"m":"Who's that, servant?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Yourself, sweet lady, for you gave the fire. Sir Thurio borrows his wit","from your ladyship's looks, and spends what he borrows kindly in your","company."],"id":358,"m":"You yourself, sweet lady, because you lit the fuse. Sir Thurio borrows his wit from your ladyship's looks and generously spends what he borrows in your company."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Sir, if you spend word for word with me, I shall make your wit","bankrupt."],"id":359,"m":"Sir, if you trade word for word with me, I'll bankrupt your wit."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I know it well, sir. You have an exchequer of words and, I think, no","other treasure to give your followers, for it appears by their bare","liveries that they live by your bare words."],"id":360,"m":"I know that well, sir. You've got a treasury of words and, I think, no other wealth to give your servants, because you can tell from their threadbare uniforms that they live on your empty words."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["No more, gentlemen, no more. Here comes my father."],"id":361,"m":"That's enough, gentlemen, that's enough. Here comes my father."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke.","id":362},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Now, daughter Silvia, you are hard beset.","Sir Valentine, your father is in good health.","What say you to a letter from your friends","Of much good news?"],"id":363,"m":"Now, daughter Silvia, you're under siege. Sir Valentine, your father is in good health. What would you say to a letter from your family with lots of good news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["My lord, I will be thankful","To any happy messenger from thence."],"id":364,"m":"My lord, I'd be grateful to any messenger bringing good news from there."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Know ye Don Antonio, your countryman?"],"id":365,"m":"Do you know Don Antonio, from your home town?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Ay, my good lord, I know the gentleman","To be of worth and worthy estimation,","And not without desert so well reputed."],"id":366,"m":"Yes, my good lord. I know the gentleman to be a man of worth and high standing, and he deserves his good reputation."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Hath he not a son?"],"id":367,"m":"Doesn't he have a son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Ay, my good lord, a son that well deserves","The honour and regard of such a father."],"id":368,"m":"Yes, my good lord, a son who well deserves the respect and love of such a father."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["You know him well?"],"id":369,"m":"You know him well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I knew him as myself, for from our infancy","We have conversed and spent our hours together.","And though myself have been an idle truant,","Omitting the sweet benefit of time","To clothe mine age with angel-like perfection,","Yet hath Sir Proteus, for that's his name,","Made use and fair advantage of his days:","His years but young, but his experience old;","His head unmellowed, but his judgement ripe;","And in a word, for far behind his worth","Comes all the praises that I now bestow,","He is complete in feature and in mind,","With all good grace to grace a gentleman."],"id":370,"m":"I knew him like my own self, because we've talked and spent our time together since we were children. And though I've been a lazy truant, wasting the precious chance to use my time to make my older self perfect, Sir Proteus, for that's his name, has used his days well and to good advantage. He's young in years but old in experience; his head isn't yet grey, but his judgement is mature. In short, since all the praise I'm giving him falls far short of what he's worth, he's perfect in body and in mind, with every good quality that can grace a gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Beshrew me, sir, but if he make this good,","He is as worthy for an empress' love","As meet to be an emperor's counsellor.","Well, sir, this gentleman is come to me","With commendation from great potentates,","And here he means to spend his time awhile.","I think 'tis no unwelcome news to you."],"id":371,"m":"Upon my word, sir, if he lives up to that, he's worthy of an empress's love and fit to advise an emperor. Well, sir, this gentleman has come to me with recommendations from great rulers, and he intends to stay here for a while. I don't think that's unwelcome news to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Should I have wished a thing, it had been he."],"id":372,"m":"If I'd wished for anything, it would have been for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Welcome him then according to his worth.","Silvia, I speak to you, and you, Sir Thurio.","For Valentine, I need not cite him to it.","I will send him hither to you presently."],"id":373,"m":"Then welcome him as he deserves. I'm speaking to you, Silvia, and to you, Sir Thurio. Valentine doesn't need telling. I'll send him to you straight away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":374},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["This is the gentleman I told your ladyship","Had come along with me but that his mistresss","Did hold his eyes locked in her crystal looks."],"id":375,"m":"This is the gentleman I told your ladyship about, who would have come with me except that his mistress kept his eyes locked in her crystal gaze."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Belike that now she hath enfranchised them","Upon some other pawn for fealty."],"id":376,"m":"I suppose she has freed them now, in return for some other pledge of loyalty."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Nay, sure, I think she holds them prisoners still."],"id":377,"m":"No, I'm sure she still holds them prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Nay, then, he should be blind, and being blind","How could he see his way to seek out you?"],"id":378,"m":"No? Then he must be blind, and if he's blind, how could he see his way to come and find you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why, lady, Love hath twenty pair of eyes."],"id":379,"m":"Why, lady, Love has twenty pairs of eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["They say that Love hath not an eye at all."],"id":380,"m":"People say Love has no eyes at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["To see such lovers, Thurio, as yourself.","Upon a homely object, Love can wink."],"id":381,"m":"To see lovers like you, Thurio. When it comes to an ugly thing, Love can shut its eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Have done, have done. Here comes the gentleman."],"id":382,"m":"Stop, stop. Here comes the gentleman."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus.","id":383},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Welcome, dear Proteus! Mistress, I beseech you","Confirm his welcome with some special favour."],"id":384,"m":"Welcome, dear Proteus! Mistress, please, confirm his welcome with some special favour."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["His worth is warrant for his welcome hither,","If this be he you oft have wished to hear from."],"id":385,"m":"His worth guarantees him a welcome here, if this is the man you've often wished to hear from."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Mistress, it is. Sweet lady, entertain him","To be my fellow-servant to your ladyship."],"id":386,"m":"Mistress, it is. Sweet lady, take him on as my fellow servant to your ladyship."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Too low a mistress for so high a servant."],"id":387,"m":"Too humble a mistress for so fine a servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Not so, sweet lady, but too mean a servant","To have a look of such a worthy mistress."],"id":388,"m":"Not so, sweet lady; too lowly a servant to be looked at by such a worthy mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Leave off discourse of disability.","Sweet lady, entertain him for your servant."],"id":389,"m":"Stop talking about how unworthy you are. Sweet lady, take him on as your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["My duty will I boast of, nothing else."],"id":390,"m":"I'll boast of my duty to you, and nothing else."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["And duty never yet did want his meed.","Servant, you are welcome to a worthless mistress."],"id":391,"m":"And duty has never gone without its reward. Servant, you're welcome to a worthless mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I'll die on him that says so but yourself."],"id":392,"m":"I'd fight to the death anyone but you who said that."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["That you are welcome?"],"id":393,"m":"That you're welcome?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["That you are worthless."],"id":394,"m":"That you're worthless."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Servant.","id":395},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Madam, my lord your father would speak with you."],"id":396,"m":"Madam, my lord your father wants to speak with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["I wait upon his pleasure."],"id":397,"m":"I'll come when he pleases."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":398},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Come, Sir Thurio,","Go with me.—Once more, new servant, welcome.","I'll leave you to confer of home affairs;","When you have done, we look to hear from you."],"cont":1,"id":399,"m":"Come, Sir Thurio, come with me. Once more, new servant, welcome. I'll leave you to talk about news from home. When you've finished, we expect to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["We'll both attend upon your ladyship."],"id":400,"m":"We'll both come and wait on your ladyship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Silvia and Thurio.","id":401},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Now, tell me, how do all from whence you came?"],"id":402,"m":"Now tell me, how is everyone back home?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Your friends are well and have them much commended."],"id":403,"m":"Your family are well and send you their warm regards."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["And how do yours?"],"id":404,"m":"And how are yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I left them all in health."],"id":405,"m":"I left them all in good health."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["How does your lady? And how thrives your love?"],"id":406,"m":"How's your lady? And how's your love going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["My tales of love were wont to weary you;","I know you joy not in a love-discourse."],"id":407,"m":"My love stories used to bore you. I know you don't enjoy talk about love."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Ay, Proteus, but that life is altered now.","I have done penance for contemning Love,","Whose high imperious thoughts have punished me","With bitter fasts, with penitential groans,","With nightly tears, and daily heart-sore sighs;","For in revenge of my contempt of love,","Love hath chased sleep from my enthralled eyes","And made them watchers of mine own heart's sorrow.","O gentle Proteus, Love's a mighty lord,","And hath so humbled me as I confess","There is no woe to his correction,","Nor to his service no such joy on earth.","Now, no discourse, except it be of love;","Now can I break my fast, dine, sup, and sleep","Upon the very naked name of love."],"id":408,"m":"Yes, Proteus, but my life has changed now. I've been punished for despising Love. His proud, commanding mind has made me suffer bitter fasting, groans of repentance, tears at night and heartsick sighs by day. To pay me back for scorning love, Love has driven sleep from my captive eyes and made them stay awake to watch my own heart's sorrow. Oh, gentle Proteus, Love is a mighty lord, and he's humbled me so much that I admit there's no pain like his punishment, and no joy on earth like serving him. Now I want no conversation except about love. Now I can eat breakfast, dinner and supper, and sleep, on nothing but the bare word \"love\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Enough; I read your fortune in your eye.","Was this the idol that you worship so?"],"id":409,"m":"Enough. I can read your fortune in your eyes. Was that the idol you worship so much?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Even she; and is she not a heavenly saint?"],"id":410,"m":"That's her. Isn't she a heavenly saint?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["No, but she is an earthly paragon."],"id":411,"m":"No, but she's an earthly model of perfection."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Call her divine."],"id":412,"m":"Call her divine."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I will not flatter her."],"id":413,"m":"I won't flatter her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["O, flatter me, for love delights in praises."],"id":414,"m":"Oh, flatter me, then, because love delights in praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["When I was sick, you gave me bitter pills,","And I must minister the like to you."],"id":415,"m":"When I was lovesick, you gave me bitter medicine, and I must give you the same."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Then speak the truth by her; if not divine,","Yet let her be a principality,","Sovereign to all the creatures on the earth."],"id":416,"m":"Then tell the truth about her. If she's not divine, let her at least be an angel, ruling over every creature on earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Except my mistress."],"id":417,"m":"Except my mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Sweet, except not any,","Except thou wilt except against my love."],"id":418,"m":"My dear friend, except no one, unless you mean to insult my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Have I not reason to prefer mine own?"],"id":419,"m":"Haven't I got reason to prefer my own?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["And I will help thee to prefer her too:","She shall be dignified with this high honour,","To bear my lady's train, lest the base earth","Should from her vesture chance to steal a kiss,","And, of so great a favour growing proud,","Disdain to root the summer-swelling flower","And make rough winter everlastingly."],"id":420,"m":"And I'll help you to promote her too: she'll be given the high honour of carrying my lady's train, in case the lowly earth steals a kiss from her dress and, grown proud of such a great favour, refuses to feed the roots of summer flowers and makes rough winter last for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Why, Valentine, what braggartism is this?"],"id":421,"m":"Why, Valentine, what bragging is this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Pardon me, Proteus, all I can is nothing","To her, whose worth makes other worthies nothing;","She is alone."],"id":422,"m":"Forgive me, Proteus. Everything I can say is nothing compared to her, whose worth makes every other worthy person nothing. She's unique."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Then let her alone."],"id":423,"m":"Then leave her alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Not for the world! Why, man, she is mine own,","And I as rich in having such a jewel","As twenty seas, if all their sand were pearl,","The water nectar, and the rocks pure gold.","Forgive me that I do not dream on thee,","Because thou seest me dote upon my love.","My foolish rival, that her father likes","Only for his possessions are so huge,","Is gone with her along, and I must after,","For love, thou know'st, is full of jealousy."],"id":424,"m":"Not for the world! Why, man, she's mine, and I'm as rich in having such a jewel as twenty seas would be if all their sand were pearls, their water nectar and their rocks pure gold. Forgive me for not thinking about you, because you can see I'm infatuated with my love. My foolish rival, whom her father likes only because his estates are so huge, has gone with her, and I must follow, because love, as you know, is full of jealousy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["But she loves you?"],"id":425,"m":"But does she love you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Ay, and we are betrothed; nay more, our marriage hour,","With all the cunning manner of our flight,","Determined of: how I must climb her window,","The ladder made of cords, and all the means","Plotted and 'greed on for my happiness.","Good Proteus, go with me to my chamber,","In these affairs to aid me with thy counsel."],"id":426,"m":"Yes, and we're engaged. More than that, we've settled the hour of our marriage and the clever way we'll run away: how I'll climb up to her window by a ladder made of ropes, and every step of the plan for my happiness is plotted and agreed. Good Proteus, come with me to my room and help me with your advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Go on before; I shall enquire you forth.","I must unto the road to disembark","Some necessaries that I needs must use,","And then I'll presently attend you."],"id":427,"m":"You go ahead; I'll find you. I must go down to the harbour to unload some things I need, and then I'll come straight to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Will you make haste?"],"id":428,"m":"Will you hurry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I will."],"id":429,"m":"I will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Valentine.","id":430},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Even as one heat another heat expels,","Or as one nail by strength drives out another,","So the remembrance of my former love","Is by a newer object quite forgotten.","Is it mine eye, or Valentine's praise,","Her true perfection, or my false transgression,","That makes me reasonless to reason thus?","She is fair; and so is Julia that I love—","That I did love, for now my love is thawed,","Which like a waxen image 'gainst a fire","Bears no impression of the thing it was.","Methinks my zeal to Valentine is cold,","And that I love him not as I was wont.","O, but I love his lady too too much,","And that's the reason I love him so little.","How shall I dote on her with more advice","That thus without advice begin to love her?","'Tis but her picture I have yet beheld,","And that hath dazzled my reason's light;","But when I look on her perfections,","There is no reason but I shall be blind.","If I can check my erring love, I will;","If not, to compass her I'll use my skill."],"cont":1,"id":431,"m":"Just as one heat drives out another, or as one nail is forced out by another, so the memory of my old love has been completely forgotten for a newer one. Is it my eyes, or Valentine's praise, her real perfection or my false wrongdoing, that makes me reason so unreasonably? She's beautiful; and so is Julia, whom I love— whom I did love, because now my love has melted, and like a wax figure in front of a fire it keeps no trace of what it was. I think my devotion to Valentine has cooled, and I don't love him as I used to. Oh, but I love his lady far too much, and that's why I love him so little. How much more will I dote on her when I've thought it over, if I begin to love her like this without thinking? I've only seen her outward appearance so far, and that's dazzled the light of my reason; but when I see her inner perfections, I'll certainly be blinded. If I can stop my straying love, I will; if not, I'll use all my skill to win her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":432},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"V","loc":"The same. A street.","id":433},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Speed and Lance with his dog Crab.","id":434},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Lance, by mine honesty, welcome to Milan!"],"id":435,"m":"Lance, on my honour, welcome to Milan!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Forswear not thyself, sweet youth, for I am not welcome. I reckon this","always, that a man is never undone till he be hanged, nor never welcome","to a place till some certain shot be paid and the hostess say","\"Welcome\"."],"id":436,"m":"Don't perjure yourself, sweet boy, because I'm not welcome. I always reckon a man is never ruined until he's hanged, and never welcome anywhere until the bill is paid and the landlady says \"Welcome\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Come on, you madcap. I'll to the alehouse with you presently, where,","for one shot of five pence, thou shalt have five thousand welcomes.","But, sirrah, how did thy master part with Madam Julia?"],"id":437,"m":"Come on, you madman. I'll go to the pub with you right now, where for one round costing five pence you'll get five thousand welcomes. But tell me, how did your master part from Madam Julia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Marry, after they closed in earnest, they parted very fairly in jest."],"id":438,"m":"Well, after they'd embraced in earnest, they parted very nicely in jest."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["But shall she marry him?"],"id":439,"m":"But will she marry him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["No."],"id":440,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["How then? Shall he marry her?"],"id":441,"m":"What then? Will he marry her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["No, neither."],"id":442,"m":"No, not that either."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["What, are they broken?"],"id":443,"m":"What, have they broken it off?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["No, they are both as whole as a fish."],"id":444,"m":"No, they're both as whole as a fish."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why then, how stands the matter with them?"],"id":445,"m":"Well then, how do things stand between them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Marry, thus: when it stands well with him, it stands well with her."],"id":446,"m":"Well, like this: when things stand well with him, they stand well with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["What an ass art thou! I understand thee not."],"id":447,"m":"What an ass you are! I don't understand you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["What a block art thou that thou canst not! My staff understands me."],"id":448,"m":"What a blockhead you are, that you can't! My stick understands me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["What thou sayst?"],"id":449,"m":"Understands what you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Ay, and what I do too. Look thee, I'll but lean, and my staff","understands me."],"id":450,"m":"Yes, and what I do too. Look, I'll just lean on it, and my stick stands under me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["It stands under thee indeed."],"id":451,"m":"It does stand under you, that's true."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, stand-under and under-stand is all one."],"id":452,"m":"Why, standing under and understanding are the same thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["But tell me true, will't be a match?"],"id":453,"m":"But tell me honestly, will it be a marriage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Ask my dog. If he say \"Ay\", it will; if he say \"No\", it will; if he","shake his tail and say nothing, it will."],"id":454,"m":"Ask my dog. If he says \"Yes\", it will; if he says \"No\", it will; if he wags his tail and says nothing, it will."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["The conclusion is, then, that it will."],"id":455,"m":"So the answer is that it will."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Thou shalt never get such a secret from me but by a parable."],"id":456,"m":"You'll never get a secret like that out of me except in a riddle."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["'Tis well that I get it so. But, Lance, how sayst thou that my master","is become a notable lover?"],"id":457,"m":"Fine, I'll take it that way. But Lance, what do you say to my master becoming a famous lover?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["I never knew him otherwise."],"id":458,"m":"I never knew him to be anything else."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Than how?"],"id":459,"m":"Anything else than what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["A notable lubber, as thou reportest him to be."],"id":460,"m":"A famous lubber, a big lump, as you say he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why, thou whoreson ass, thou mistak'st me."],"id":461,"m":"Why, you son-of-a-whore ass, you've got me wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, fool, I meant not thee, I meant thy master."],"id":462,"m":"Why, you fool, I didn't mean you, I meant your master."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["I tell thee, my master is become a hot lover."],"id":463,"m":"I'm telling you, my master has become a passionate lover."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, I tell thee, I care not though he burn himself in love. If thou","wilt, go with me to the alehouse; if not, thou art an Hebrew, a Jew,","and not worth the name of a Christian."],"id":464,"m":"Well, I'm telling you, I don't care if he burns himself up with love. If you like, come with me to the pub; if not, you're a Hebrew, a Jew, and not worth the name of a Christian."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why?"],"id":465,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Because thou hast not so much charity in thee as to go to the ale with","a Christian. Wilt thou go?"],"id":466,"m":"Because you haven't got enough Christian charity in you to go for a drink with a fellow Christian. Will you come?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["At thy service."],"id":467,"m":"At your service."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":468},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"VI","loc":"The same. The Duke's palace.","id":469},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus alone.","id":470},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["To leave my Julia, shall I be forsworn;","To love fair Silvia, shall I be forsworn;","To wrong my friend, I shall be much forsworn.","And e'en that power which gave me first my oath","Provokes me to this threefold perjury.","Love bade me swear, and Love bids me forswear.","O sweet-suggesting Love, if thou hast sinned,","Teach me, thy tempted subject, to excuse it.","At first I did adore a twinkling star,","But now I worship a celestial sun.","Unheedful vows may heedfully be broken,","And he wants wit that wants resolved will","To learn his wit t' exchange the bad for better.","Fie, fie, unreverend tongue, to call her bad","Whose sovereignty so oft thou hast preferred","With twenty thousand soul-confirming oaths.","I cannot leave to love, and yet I do;","But there I leave to love where I should love.","Julia I lose, and Valentine I lose;","If I keep them, I needs must lose myself;","If I lose them, thus find I by their loss,","For Valentine, myself; for Julia, Silvia.","I to myself am dearer than a friend,","For love is still most precious in itself,","And Silvia—witness heaven that made her fair—","Shows Julia but a swarthy Ethiope.","I will forget that Julia is alive,","Remembering that my love to her is dead;","And Valentine I'll hold an enemy,","Aiming at Silvia as a sweeter friend.","I cannot now prove constant to myself","Without some treachery used to Valentine.","This night he meaneth with a corded ladder","To climb celestial Silvia's chamber window,","Myself in counsel, his competitor.","Now presently I'll give her father notice","Of their disguising and pretended flight,","Who, all enraged, will banish Valentine,","For Thurio he intends shall wed his daughter.","But Valentine being gone, I'll quickly cross","By some sly trick blunt Thurio's dull proceeding.","Love, lend me wings to make my purpose swift,","As thou hast lent me wit to plot this drift."],"id":471,"m":"If I leave my Julia, I'll break my vow; if I love beautiful Silvia, I'll break my vow; if I wrong my friend, I'll break my vow worst of all. And the very same power that made me swear in the first place drives me to break three oaths at once. Love told me to swear, and Love tells me to break my word. Oh, sweetly tempting Love, if you've sinned, teach me, your tempted subject, how to excuse it. At first I adored a twinkling star, but now I worship a heavenly sun. Thoughtless vows may be broken thoughtfully, and a man lacks sense if he lacks the willpower to teach his mind to swap bad for better. Shame, shame, irreverent tongue, to call her bad, when you've so often declared she ruled over all, with twenty thousand oaths sworn on your soul. I can't stop loving, and yet I do; but I stop loving where I should love. I lose Julia, and I lose Valentine. If I keep them, I'll have to lose myself. If I lose them, then by losing them I find myself in place of Valentine, and Silvia in place of Julia. I'm dearer to myself than any friend, because love always values itself most; and Silvia, as heaven that made her beautiful is my witness, makes Julia look like a sunburnt African. I'll forget that Julia is alive, remembering that my love for her is dead; and I'll treat Valentine as an enemy, aiming at Silvia as a sweeter friend. I can't be true to myself now without using some treachery against Valentine. Tonight he plans to climb up to heavenly Silvia's bedroom window with a rope ladder, and I, his rival, am in on the secret. I'll tell her father straight away about their disguises and planned escape. He'll be furious and banish Valentine, because he means Thurio to marry his daughter. But once Valentine is gone, I'll quickly use some sly trick to block dull Thurio's slow courtship. Love, lend me wings to make my plan move fast, as you've lent me the wit to plot it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":472},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"VII","loc":"Verona. A room in Julia's house.","id":473},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Julia and Lucetta.","id":474},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Counsel, Lucetta; gentle girl, assist me,","And ev'n in kind love I do conjure thee,","Who art the table wherein all my thoughts","Are visibly charactered and engraved,","To lesson me and tell me some good mean","How with my honour I may undertake","A journey to my loving Proteus."],"id":475,"m":"Advise me, Lucetta; help me, gentle girl. I beg you, out of love, since you are the tablet on which all my thoughts are plainly written and engraved, teach me and tell me some good way that I can make a journey to my loving Proteus without losing my honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Alas, the way is wearisome and long."],"id":476,"m":"Oh dear, the road is long and tiring."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["A true-devoted pilgrim is not weary","To measure kingdoms with his feeble steps;","Much less shall she that hath Love's wings to fly,","And when the flight is made to one so dear,","Of such divine perfection, as Sir Proteus."],"id":477,"m":"A truly devoted pilgrim isn't tired by crossing kingdoms on foot; still less will someone be who has Love's wings to fly with, especially when she's flying to someone as dear and as divinely perfect as Sir Proteus."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Better forbear till Proteus make return."],"id":478,"m":"Better wait till Proteus comes back."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["O, know'st thou not his looks are my soul's food?","Pity the dearth that I have pined in","By longing for that food so long a time.","Didst thou but know the inly touch of love","Thou wouldst as soon go kindle fire with snow","As seek to quench the fire of love with words."],"id":479,"m":"Oh, don't you know his looks are food for my soul? Pity the famine I've been wasting away in, longing for that food for so long. If you only knew how love feels inside, you'd as soon try to light a fire with snow as try to put out the fire of love with words."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["I do not seek to quench your love's hot fire,","But qualify the fire's extreme rage,","Lest it should burn above the bounds of reason."],"id":480,"m":"I'm not trying to put out the hot fire of your love, only to calm its wild rage, in case it burns past the limits of reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["The more thou damm'st it up, the more it burns.","The current that with gentle murmur glides,","Thou know'st, being stopped, impatiently doth rage;","But when his fair course is not hindered,","He makes sweet music with th' enamelled stones,","Giving a gentle kiss to every sedge","He overtaketh in his pilgrimage;","And so by many winding nooks he strays","With willing sport to the wild ocean.","Then let me go and hinder not my course.","I'll be as patient as a gentle stream","And make a pastime of each weary step","Till the last step have brought me to my love,","And there I'll rest as after much turmoil","A blessed soul doth in Elysium."],"id":481,"m":"The more you dam it up, the more it burns. You know that a stream that glides along with a gentle murmur rages impatiently when it's blocked; but when its smooth course isn't held back, it makes sweet music over the shining pebbles and gives a gentle kiss to every reed it passes on its pilgrimage. And so it wanders through many winding bends, playing happily, to the wild ocean. So let me go, and don't block my course. I'll be as patient as a gentle stream and make a game of every tiring step, until the last step brings me to my love. And there I'll rest, as a blessed soul rests in heaven after much trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["But in what habit will you go along?"],"id":482,"m":"But what clothes will you wear for the journey?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Not like a woman, for I would prevent","The loose encounters of lascivious men.","Gentle Lucetta, fit me with such weeds","As may beseem some well-reputed page."],"id":483,"m":"Not a woman's, because I want to avoid being pestered by lustful men. Gentle Lucetta, get me the kind of clothes a respectable page would wear."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Why then, your ladyship must cut your hair."],"id":484,"m":"Why then, your ladyship will have to cut your hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["No, girl, I'll knit it up in silken strings","With twenty odd-conceited true-love knots.","To be fantastic may become a youth","Of greater time than I shall show to be."],"id":485,"m":"No, girl, I'll tie it up with silk ribbons in twenty fancy love knots. Looking showy can suit a young man even older than I'll appear to be."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["What fashion, madam, shall I make your breeches?"],"id":486,"m":"What style shall I make your breeches, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["That fits as well as \"Tell me, good my lord,","What compass will you wear your farthingale?\"","Why e'en what fashion thou best likes, Lucetta."],"id":487,"m":"That's as sensible as saying, \"Tell me, my good lord, how wide will you wear your hooped skirt?\" Why, whatever style you like best, Lucetta."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["You must needs have them with a codpiece, madam."],"id":488,"m":"You'll have to have them with a codpiece, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Out, out, Lucetta, that will be ill-favoured."],"id":489,"m":"Stop it, stop it, Lucetta, that would look ugly."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["A round hose, madam, now's not worth a pin","Unless you have a codpiece to stick pins on."],"id":490,"m":"Round breeches aren't worth a pin these days, madam, unless you've got a codpiece to stick pins in."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Lucetta, as thou lov'st me, let me have","What thou think'st meet and is most mannerly.","But tell me, wench, how will the world repute me","For undertaking so unstaid a journey?","I fear me it will make me scandalized."],"id":491,"m":"Lucetta, if you love me, get me whatever you think suitable and most decent. But tell me, girl, what will people think of me for making such a reckless journey? I'm afraid it will cause a scandal."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["If you think so, then stay at home and go not."],"id":492,"m":"If you think so, then stay at home and don't go."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Nay, that I will not."],"id":493,"m":"No, I won't do that."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Then never dream on infamy, but go.","If Proteus like your journey when you come,","No matter who's displeased when you are gone.","I fear me he will scarce be pleased withal."],"id":494,"m":"Then don't worry about your reputation, but go. If Proteus is pleased with your journey when you arrive, it doesn't matter who's displeased that you've gone. I'm afraid he won't be very pleased with it, though."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["That is the least, Lucetta, of my fear.","A thousand oaths, an ocean of his tears,","And instances of infinite of love,","Warrant me welcome to my Proteus."],"id":495,"m":"That's the least of my fears, Lucetta. A thousand vows, an ocean of his tears and endless proofs of his love guarantee that my Proteus will welcome me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["All these are servants to deceitful men."],"id":496,"m":"Deceitful men use all those things."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Base men that use them to so base effect!","But truer stars did govern Proteus' birth.","His words are bonds, his oaths are oracles,","His love sincere, his thoughts immaculate,","His tears pure messengers sent from his heart,","His heart as far from fraud as heaven from earth."],"id":497,"m":"Low men, who use them for such low purposes! But truer stars ruled Proteus's birth. His words are binding contracts, his oaths are sacred truths, his love is sincere, his thoughts are pure, his tears are honest messengers from his heart, and his heart is as far from deceit as heaven is from earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCETTA","lines":["Pray heav'n he prove so when you come to him."],"id":498,"m":"I pray heaven he turns out like that when you get to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Now, as thou lov'st me, do him not that wrong","To bear a hard opinion of his truth.","Only deserve my love by loving him.","And presently go with me to my chamber","To take a note of what I stand in need of","To furnish me upon my longing journey.","All that is mine I leave at thy dispose,","My goods, my lands, my reputation;","Only, in lieu thereof, dispatch me hence.","Come, answer not, but to it presently.","I am impatient of my tarriance."],"id":499,"m":"Now, if you love me, don't wrong him by thinking badly of his faithfulness. Just earn my love by loving him. Come with me to my room right now and make a list of everything I need for my longed-for journey. I leave everything I own in your hands: my goods, my lands, my good name. In return, just get me on my way. Come on, don't answer, just get to it at once. I can't bear waiting."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":500},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Milan. An anteroom in the Duke's palace.","id":501},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke, Thurio and Proteus.","id":502},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sir Thurio, give us leave, I pray, awhile;","We have some secrets to confer about."],"id":503,"m":"Sir Thurio, please excuse us for a while. We have some secret matters to discuss."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Thurio.","id":504},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Now tell me, Proteus, what's your will with me?"],"cont":1,"id":505,"m":"Now tell me, Proteus, what do you want with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["My gracious lord, that which I would discover","The law of friendship bids me to conceal,","But when I call to mind your gracious favours","Done to me, undeserving as I am,","My duty pricks me on to utter that","Which else no worldly good should draw from me.","Know, worthy prince, Sir Valentine my friend","This night intends to steal away your daughter;","Myself am one made privy to the plot.","I know you have determined to bestow her","On Thurio, whom your gentle daughter hates,","And should she thus be stol'n away from you,","It would be much vexation to your age.","Thus, for my duty's sake, I rather chose","To cross my friend in his intended drift","Than, by concealing it, heap on your head","A pack of sorrows which would press you down,","Being unprevented, to your timeless grave."],"id":506,"m":"My gracious lord, the law of friendship tells me to keep secret what I'm about to reveal. But when I remember the kindness you've shown me, undeserving as I am, my duty spurs me to say what nothing in the world could otherwise drag out of me. Know, worthy prince, that my friend Sir Valentine intends to steal your daughter away tonight. I've been let in on the plot. I know you've decided to give her to Thurio, whom your gentle daughter hates, and if she were stolen away from you like this, it would be a great grief to you in your old age. So, out of duty, I chose to block my friend's plan rather than keep quiet and pile on your head a load of sorrows that, if nothing prevented them, would weigh you down into an early grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Proteus, I thank thee for thine honest care,","Which to requite command me while I live.","This love of theirs myself have often seen,","Haply when they have judged me fast asleep,","And oftentimes have purposed to forbid","Sir Valentine her company and my court.","But fearing lest my jealous aim might err","And so, unworthily, disgrace the man—","A rashness that I ever yet have shunned—","I gave him gentle looks, thereby to find","That which thyself hast now disclosed to me.","And that thou mayst perceive my fear of this,","Knowing that tender youth is soon suggested,","I nightly lodge her in an upper tower,","The key whereof myself have ever kept;","And thence she cannot be conveyed away."],"id":507,"m":"Proteus, thank you for your honest concern. To repay it, call on me as long as I live. I've often seen this love of theirs myself, perhaps when they thought I was fast asleep, and I've often meant to ban Sir Valentine from her company and from my court. But I was afraid my suspicions might be wrong and so disgrace the man unfairly, a rash thing I've always avoided. So I was friendly to him, in order to find out what you've now told me. And so that you can see how I've feared this, knowing that young people are easily tempted, I make her sleep each night in an upper tower, and I always keep the key myself. She can't be carried off from there."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Know, noble lord, they have devised a mean","How he her chamber-window will ascend","And with a corded ladder fetch her down;","For which the youthful lover now is gone,","And this way comes he with it presently,","Where, if it please you, you may intercept him.","But, good my lord, do it so cunningly","That my discovery be not aimed at;","For love of you, not hate unto my friend,","Hath made me publisher of this pretence."],"id":508,"m":"Know, noble lord, they've worked out a way for him to climb up to her bedroom window and bring her down with a rope ladder. The young lover has gone to fetch it now, and he'll come this way with it soon. Here you can stop him, if you like. But, my good lord, do it so cleverly that no one guesses I told you. It's love for you, not hatred for my friend, that has made me reveal this plan."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Upon mine honour, he shall never know","That I had any light from thee of this."],"id":509,"m":"On my honour, he'll never know that you told me anything about this."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Adieu, my lord, Sir Valentine is coming."],"id":510,"m":"Goodbye, my lord. Sir Valentine is coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":511},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine.","id":512},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sir Valentine, whither away so fast?"],"id":513,"m":"Sir Valentine, where are you off to in such a hurry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Please it your Grace, there is a messenger","That stays to bear my letters to my friends,","And I am going to deliver them."],"id":514,"m":"If it please your grace, there's a messenger waiting to take my letters to my family, and I'm going to give them to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Be they of much import?"],"id":515,"m":"Are they very important?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["The tenor of them doth but signify","My health and happy being at your court."],"id":516,"m":"All they say is that I'm well and happy at your court."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Nay then, no matter. Stay with me awhile;","I am to break with thee of some affairs","That touch me near, wherein thou must be secret.","'Tis not unknown to thee that I have sought","To match my friend Sir Thurio to my daughter."],"id":517,"m":"No? Then it doesn't matter. Stay with me a while. I need to talk to you about some matters that concern me closely, and you must keep them secret. You know that I've been trying to marry my friend Sir Thurio to my daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I know it well, my lord, and sure the match","Were rich and honourable. Besides, the gentleman","Is full of virtue, bounty, worth, and qualities","Beseeming such a wife as your fair daughter.","Cannot your grace win her to fancy him?"],"id":518,"m":"I know it well, my lord, and surely the match would be rich and honourable. Besides, the gentleman is full of virtue, generosity, worth and qualities that suit a wife like your beautiful daughter. Can't your grace persuade her to like him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["No, trust me, she is peevish, sullen, froward,","Proud, disobedient, stubborn, lacking duty,","Neither regarding that she is my child","Nor fearing me as if I were her father;","And, may I say to thee, this pride of hers,","Upon advice, hath drawn my love from her,","And where I thought the remnant of mine age","Should have been cherished by her childlike duty,","I now am full resolved to take a wife","And turn her out to who will take her in.","Then let her beauty be her wedding dower,","For me and my possessions she esteems not."],"id":519,"m":"No, believe me, she's sulky, moody, contrary, proud, disobedient, stubborn and undutiful. She cares nothing that she's my child, and she isn't afraid of me as she should be of her father. And, I can tell you, this pride of hers, after some thought, has turned my love away from her. I thought the rest of my old age would be cared for by her duty as a daughter, but now I've firmly decided to take a wife and throw her out to whoever will take her in. Then let her beauty be her dowry, since she cares nothing for me or my wealth."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["What would your Grace have me to do in this?"],"id":520,"m":"What would your grace like me to do about this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["There is a lady of Verona here","Whom I affect; but she is nice, and coy,","And nought esteems my aged eloquence.","Now therefore would I have thee to my tutor—","For long agone I have forgot to court;","Besides, the fashion of the time is changed—","How and which way I may bestow myself","To be regarded in her sun-bright eye."],"id":521,"m":"There's a lady here from Verona whom I'm fond of, but she's fussy and shy, and thinks nothing of an old man's fine speeches. So I'd like you to be my teacher, because I forgot how to court long ago, and besides, fashions have changed. Teach me how to behave so that her sun-bright eyes will notice me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Win her with gifts if she respect not words;","Dumb jewels often in their silent kind","More than quick words do move a woman's mind."],"id":522,"m":"Win her with presents if she doesn't care for words. Silent jewels, quiet as they are, often move a woman's heart more than lively words."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["But she did scorn a present that I sent her."],"id":523,"m":"But she rejected a present I sent her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["A woman sometime scorns what best contents her.","Send her another; never give her o'er,","For scorn at first makes after-love the more.","If she do frown, 'tis not in hate of you,","But rather to beget more love in you.","If she do chide, 'tis not to have you gone,","Forwhy the fools are mad if left alone.","Take no repulse, whatever she doth say;","For \"Get you gone\" she doth not mean \"Away!\"","Flatter and praise, commend, extol their graces;","Though ne'er so black, say they have angels' faces.","That man that hath a tongue, I say, is no man","If with his tongue he cannot win a woman."],"id":524,"m":"A woman sometimes rejects what pleases her most. Send her another; never give up on her, because scorn at first makes love stronger later. If she frowns, it isn't because she hates you, but to make you love her more. If she scolds you, it isn't because she wants you gone, because the silly things go mad if they're left alone. Don't be put off, whatever she says, because when she says \"Get out\" she doesn't mean \"Go away!\" Flatter and praise them, admire and extol their charms; however dark-skinned they are, say they have angels' faces. I say the man who has a tongue is no man if he can't win a woman with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["But she I mean is promised by her friends","Unto a youthful gentleman of worth,","And kept severely from resort of men,","That no man hath access by day to her."],"id":525,"m":"But the lady I mean has been promised by her family to a fine young gentleman, and she's kept strictly away from men, so no man can see her by day."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why then, I would resort to her by night."],"id":526,"m":"Why then, I'd visit her by night."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Ay, but the doors be locked and keys kept safe,","That no man hath recourse to her by night."],"id":527,"m":"Yes, but the doors are locked and the keys kept safe, so no man can get to her by night."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["What lets but one may enter at her window?"],"id":528,"m":"What's to stop someone climbing in at her window?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Her chamber is aloft, far from the ground,","And built so shelving that one cannot climb it","Without apparent hazard of his life."],"id":529,"m":"Her room is high up, far from the ground, and the wall leans out so far that no one could climb it without obviously risking his life."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why, then a ladder quaintly made of cords","To cast up with a pair of anchoring hooks,","Would serve to scale another Hero's tower,","So bold Leander would adventure it."],"id":530,"m":"Why, then a cleverly made rope ladder, thrown up with a pair of grappling hooks, would do to climb another Hero's tower, if a bold Leander would risk it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Now, as thou art a gentleman of blood,","Advise me where I may have such a ladder."],"id":531,"m":"Now, as you're a well-born gentleman, tell me where I can get a ladder like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["When would you use it? Pray, sir, tell me that."],"id":532,"m":"When would you use it? Please, sir, tell me that."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This very night; for Love is like a child","That longs for everything that he can come by."],"id":533,"m":"This very night; because Love is like a child, wanting everything it can get hold of."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["By seven o'clock I'll get you such a ladder."],"id":534,"m":"I'll get you a ladder like that by seven o'clock."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["But, hark thee: I will go to her alone;","How shall I best convey the ladder thither?"],"id":535,"m":"But listen: I'll go to her alone. How can I best carry the ladder there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["It will be light, my lord, that you may bear it","Under a cloak that is of any length."],"id":536,"m":"It'll be light, my lord, so you can carry it under any reasonably long cloak."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["A cloak as long as thine will serve the turn?"],"id":537,"m":"Would a cloak as long as yours do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Ay, my good lord."],"id":538,"m":"Yes, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Then let me see thy cloak;","I'll get me one of such another length."],"id":539,"m":"Then let me see your cloak. I'll get myself one of the same length."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why, any cloak will serve the turn, my lord."],"id":540,"m":"Why, any cloak will do, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["How shall I fashion me to wear a cloak?","I pray thee, let me feel thy cloak upon me."],"id":541,"m":"How do I wear a cloak like this? Please, let me try your cloak on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Takes Valentine's cloak and finds a letter and a rope ladder concealed under it.","id":542},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["What letter is this same? What's here?—To Silvia?","And here an engine fit for my proceeding.","I'll be so bold to break the seal for once."],"cont":1,"id":543,"m":"What's this letter? What's this? \"To Silvia\"? And here's just the tool for my purpose. I'll be so bold as to break the seal for once."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["[Reads] My thoughts do harbour with my Silvia nightly,","And slaves they are to me that send them flying.","O, could their master come and go as lightly,","Himself would lodge where, senseless, they are lying.","My herald thoughts in thy pure bosom rest them,","While I, their king, that thither them importune,","Do curse the grace that with such grace hath blest them,","Because myself do want my servants' fortune.","I curse myself, for they are sent by me,","That they should harbour where their lord should be.","What's here?","[Reads] Silvia, this night I will enfranchise thee.","'Tis so; and here's the ladder for the purpose.","Why, Phaëthon—for thou art Merops' son—","Wilt thou aspire to guide the heavenly car,","And with thy daring folly burn the world?","Wilt thou reach stars because they shine on thee?","Go, base intruder, overweening slave,","Bestow thy fawning smiles on equal mates,","And think my patience, more than thy desert,","Is privilege for thy departure hence.","Thank me for this more than for all the favours","Which, all too much, I have bestowed on thee.","But if thou linger in my territories","Longer than swiftest expedition","Will give thee time to leave our royal court,","By heaven, my wrath shall far exceed the love","I ever bore my daughter or thyself.","Begone, I will not hear thy vain excuse,","But, as thou lov'st thy life, make speed from hence."],"id":544,"m":"[Reads] \"My thoughts stay with my Silvia every night, and they are slaves to me, who sends them flying to her. Oh, if their master could come and go as easily, he would lie himself where they lie, unfeeling. My messenger thoughts rest in your pure breast, while I, their king, who urges them to go there, curse the good luck that has blessed them so, because I lack my servants' fortune. I curse myself, because I send them, so that they lodge where their lord should be.\" What's this? [Reads] \"Silvia, tonight I will set you free.\" So that's it; and here's the ladder for the job. Why, Phaethon, for you're a nobody like Merops's son, will you try to drive the sun's chariot and burn up the world with your reckless folly? Will you reach for the stars just because they shine on you? Go, low intruder, presumptuous slave! Give your fawning smiles to your equals, and think that my patience, not what you deserve, is what allows you to leave. Thank me for this more than for all the favours I've given you, far too many. But if you stay in my lands longer than the fastest journey takes to leave our royal court, by heaven, my anger will far exceed any love I ever had for my daughter or for you. Get out. I won't listen to your useless excuses. If you value your life, leave here quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":545},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["And why not death, rather than living torment?","To die is to be banished from myself,","And Silvia is myself; banished from her","Is self from self—a deadly banishment.","What light is light, if Silvia be not seen?","What joy is joy, if Silvia be not by?","Unless it be to think that she is by","And feed upon the shadow of perfection.","Except I be by Silvia in the night,","There is no music in the nightingale.","Unless I look on Silvia in the day,","There is no day for me to look upon.","She is my essence, and I leave to be","If I be not by her fair influence","Fostered, illumined, cherished, kept alive.","I fly not death, to fly his deadly doom:","Tarry I here, I but attend on death,","But fly I hence, I fly away from life."],"id":546,"m":"And why not death, rather than this living torture? To die is to be banished from myself, and Silvia is myself. To be banished from her is to be cut off from my own self: a deadly banishment. What light is light, if I can't see Silvia? What joy is joy, if Silvia isn't near? Unless I imagine she's near and feed on the shadow of perfection. Unless I'm with Silvia at night, there's no music in the nightingale. Unless I see Silvia by day, there's no daylight for me to see. She's my very being, and I'll cease to exist if her lovely influence doesn't nourish, light, cherish and keep me alive. I don't escape death by escaping his death sentence: if I stay here, I'm only waiting for death, but if I go away from here, I'm running away from life."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus and Lance.","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Run, boy, run, run, seek him out."],"id":548,"m":"Run, boy, run, run, find him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["So-ho, so-ho!"],"id":549,"m":"Tally-ho, tally-ho!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["What seest thou?"],"id":550,"m":"What do you see?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Him we go to find. There's not a hair on 's head but 'tis a Valentine."],"id":551,"m":"The man we're looking for. There's not a hair on his head that isn't a Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Valentine?"],"id":552,"m":"Valentine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No."],"id":553,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Who then? His spirit?"],"id":554,"m":"Who, then? His ghost?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Neither."],"id":555,"m":"Not that either."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["What then?"],"id":556,"m":"What, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Nothing."],"id":557,"m":"Nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Can nothing speak? Master, shall I strike?"],"id":558,"m":"Can nothing speak? Master, shall I hit him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Who wouldst thou strike?"],"id":559,"m":"Who would you hit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Nothing."],"id":560,"m":"Nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Villain, forbear."],"id":561,"m":"Stop it, you villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, sir, I'll strike nothing. I pray you—"],"id":562,"m":"Why, sir, I'll hit nothing. Please let me—"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sirrah, I say, forbear.—Friend Valentine, a word."],"id":563,"m":"I said stop it, boy. Valentine, my friend, a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["My ears are stopped and cannot hear good news,","So much of bad already hath possessed them."],"id":564,"m":"My ears are blocked and can't hear good news, they're so full of bad already."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Then in dumb silence will I bury mine,","For they are harsh, untuneable, and bad."],"id":565,"m":"Then I'll bury my news in silence, because it's harsh, unpleasant and bad."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Is Silvia dead?"],"id":566,"m":"Is Silvia dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["No, Valentine."],"id":567,"m":"No, Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No Valentine indeed for sacred Silvia.","Hath she forsworn me?"],"id":568,"m":"No Valentine, indeed, for sacred Silvia. Has she rejected me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["No, Valentine."],"id":569,"m":"No, Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No Valentine, if Silvia have forsworn me.","What is your news?"],"id":570,"m":"No Valentine, if Silvia has rejected me. What's your news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Sir, there is a proclamation that you are vanished."],"id":571,"m":"Sir, there's a proclamation that you've vanished."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["That thou art banished—O, that's the news—","From hence, from Silvia, and from me thy friend."],"id":572,"m":"That you're banished, oh, that's the news, from here, from Silvia and from me, your friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["O, I have fed upon this woe already,","And now excess of it will make me surfeit.","Doth Silvia know that I am banished?"],"id":573,"m":"Oh, I've already fed on this sorrow, and now too much of it will make me sick. Does Silvia know I'm banished?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Ay, ay; and she hath offered to the doom—","Which unreversed stands in effectual force—","A sea of melting pearl, which some call tears;","Those at her father's churlish feet she tendered,","With them, upon her knees, her humble self,","Wringing her hands, whose whiteness so became them","As if but now they waxed pale for woe.","But neither bended knees, pure hands held up,","Sad sighs, deep groans, nor silver-shedding tears","Could penetrate her uncompassionate sire;","But Valentine, if he be ta'en, must die.","Besides, her intercession chafed him so,","When she for thy repeal was suppliant,","That to close prison he commanded her,","With many bitter threats of biding there."],"id":574,"m":"Yes, yes; and she has offered up against the sentence, which still stands, a sea of melting pearls that some call tears. She poured them at her father's harsh feet, and with them, on her knees, she offered her humble self, wringing her hands, whose whiteness suited them so well it was as if they had just turned pale with grief. But neither bended knees, pure hands raised up, sad sighs, deep groans nor silver tears could move her pitiless father: if Valentine is caught, he must die. Besides, her pleading for your pardon made him so angry that he ordered her locked up, with many bitter threats that she would stay there."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No more, unless the next word that thou speak'st","Have some malignant power upon my life.","If so, I pray thee breathe it in mine ear,","As ending anthem of my endless dolour."],"id":575,"m":"No more, unless the next word you say has power to kill me. If it does, please breathe it into my ear, as the last hymn of my endless sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Cease to lament for that thou canst not help,","And study help for that which thou lament'st.","Time is the nurse and breeder of all good.","Here if thou stay, thou canst not see thy love;","Besides, thy staying will abridge thy life.","Hope is a lover's staff; walk hence with that","And manage it against despairing thoughts.","Thy letters may be here, though thou art hence,","Which, being writ to me, shall be delivered","Even in the milk-white bosom of thy love.","The time now serves not to expostulate.","Come, I'll convey thee through the city-gate,","And, ere I part with thee, confer at large","Of all that may concern thy love affairs.","As thou lov'st Silvia, though not for thyself,","Regard thy danger, and along with me."],"id":576,"m":"Stop grieving over what you can't help, and work out how to help what you're grieving over. Time is the nurse and parent of everything good. If you stay here, you can't see your love; besides, staying will cut your life short. Hope is a lover's walking stick: walk away from here with it and use it to fight off despairing thoughts. Your letters can be here even if you're away. If you write them to me, I'll deliver them right into your love's milk-white bosom. Now isn't the time to argue. Come, I'll take you through the city gate, and before we part we'll talk at length about everything to do with your love. If you love Silvia, even if you don't care about yourself, think of the danger and come with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I pray thee, Lance, an if thou seest my boy,","Bid him make haste and meet me at the North Gate."],"id":577,"m":"Please, Lance, if you see my boy, tell him to hurry and meet me at the North Gate."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Go, sirrah, find him out. Come, Valentine."],"id":578,"m":"Go and find him, boy. Come, Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["O, my dear Silvia! Hapless Valentine!"],"id":579,"m":"Oh, my dear Silvia! Unlucky Valentine!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Valentine and Proteus.","id":580},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["I am but a fool, look you, and yet I have the wit to think my master is","a kind of a knave; but that's all one if he be but one knave. He lives","not now that knows me to be in love, yet I am in love, but a team of","horse shall not pluck that from me, nor who 'tis I love; and yet 'tis a","woman, but what woman I will not tell myself; and yet 'tis a milkmaid;","yet 'tis not a maid, for she hath had gossips; yet 'tis a maid, for she","is her master's maid and serves for wages. She hath more qualities than","a water-spaniel, which is much in a bare Christian. [Pulls out a paper]","Here is the cate-log of her condition. Imprimis, She can","fetch and carry. Why, a horse can do no more; nay, a horse cannot","fetch but only carry; therefore is she better than a jade. Item, She","can milk. Look you, a sweet virtue in a maid with clean hands."],"id":581,"m":"I'm only a fool, you see, and yet I've got the sense to think my master is a kind of knave; but that's all one, as long as he's only one knave. There's no one alive who knows I'm in love, and yet I'm in love, but a team of horses won't drag that out of me, nor who it is I love. And yet it's a woman, but which woman I won't tell even myself. And yet it's a milkmaid. And yet she's not a maid, because she's had a baby christened. And yet she is a maid, because she's her master's maid and works for wages. She has more good qualities than a water spaniel, which is a lot for a mere Christian. [Pulls out a paper] Here's the catalogue of her qualities. \"First, she can fetch and carry.\" Why, a horse can do no more than that; no, a horse can't fetch, only carry, so she's better than a nag. \"Next, she can milk.\" Look, that's a sweet virtue in a girl with clean hands."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Speed.","id":582},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["How now, Signior Lance? What news with your mastership?"],"id":583,"m":"Hello, Signior Lance! What news with your mastership?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["With my master's ship? Why, it is at sea."],"id":584,"m":"With my master's ship? Why, it's at sea."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Well, your old vice still: mistake the word. What news, then, in your","paper?"],"id":585,"m":"Well, your old fault again: getting the word wrong. What news is in your paper, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["The blackest news that ever thou heard'st."],"id":586,"m":"The blackest news you ever heard."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why, man? How black?"],"id":587,"m":"Why, man? How black?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, as black as ink."],"id":588,"m":"Why, as black as ink."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Let me read them."],"id":589,"m":"Let me read it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Fie on thee, jolt-head, thou canst not read."],"id":590,"m":"Shame on you, blockhead, you can't read."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Thou liest. I can."],"id":591,"m":"You're lying. I can."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["I will try thee. Tell me this, who begot thee?"],"id":592,"m":"I'll test you. Tell me this: who was your father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Marry, the son of my grandfather."],"id":593,"m":"Why, my grandfather's son."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["O, illiterate loiterer! It was the son of thy grandmother.","This proves that thou canst not read."],"id":594,"m":"Oh, you illiterate idler! It was your grandmother's son. That proves you can't read."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Come, fool, come; try me in thy paper."],"id":595,"m":"Come on, fool, come on; test me with your paper."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["[Gives him the paper] There; and Saint Nicholas be thy speed."],"id":596,"m":"[Gives him the paper] There, and Saint Nicholas, the patron of scholars, help you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Imprimis, She can milk."],"id":597,"m":"\"First, she can milk.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Ay, that she can."],"id":598,"m":"Yes, that she can."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She brews good ale."],"id":599,"m":"\"Next, she brews good ale.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["And thereof comes the proverb, \"Blessing of your heart, you brew good","ale.\""],"id":600,"m":"And that's where the saying comes from: \"Bless your heart, you brew good ale.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She can sew."],"id":601,"m":"\"Next, she can sew.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["That's as much as to say, \"Can she so?\""],"id":602,"m":"That's as good as asking, \"Can she, so?\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She can knit."],"id":603,"m":"\"Next, she can knit.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["What need a man care for a stock with a wench, when she can knit him a","stock?"],"id":604,"m":"Why should a man worry about a dowry with a girl, when she can knit him stockings?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She can wash and scour."],"id":605,"m":"\"Next, she can wash and scrub.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["A special virtue, for then she need not be washed and scoured."],"id":606,"m":"A special virtue, because then she won't need washing and scrubbing herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She can spin."],"id":607,"m":"\"Next, she can spin.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Then may I set the world on wheels, when she can spin for her living."],"id":608,"m":"Then I can take life easy, since she can spin for her living."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She hath many nameless virtues."],"id":609,"m":"\"Next, she has many nameless virtues.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["That's as much as to say, \"bastard virtues\", that indeed know not their","fathers, and therefore have no names."],"id":610,"m":"That's as good as saying \"bastard virtues\", which don't know their fathers and so have no names."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Here follow her vices."],"id":611,"m":"Here come her faults."},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Close at the heels of her virtues."],"id":612,"m":"Hard on the heels of her virtues."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She is not to be kissed fasting in respect of her breath."],"id":613,"m":"\"Next, she mustn't be kissed before breakfast, because of her breath.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Well, that fault may be mended with a breakfast.","Read on."],"id":614,"m":"Well, a breakfast will fix that fault. Read on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She hath a sweet mouth."],"id":615,"m":"\"Next, she has a sweet tooth.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["That makes amends for her sour breath."],"id":616,"m":"That makes up for her sour breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She doth talk in her sleep."],"id":617,"m":"\"Next, she talks in her sleep.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["It's no matter for that, so she sleep not in her talk."],"id":618,"m":"That doesn't matter, as long as she doesn't sleep while she's talking."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She is slow in words."],"id":619,"m":"\"Next, she is slow to speak.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["O villain, that set this down among her vices! To be slow in words is a","woman's only virtue. I pray thee, out with't, and place it for her","chief virtue."],"id":620,"m":"Oh, what a villain to put that among her faults! Being slow to speak is a woman's only virtue. Please, take it out of there and put it down as her chief virtue."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She is proud."],"id":621,"m":"\"Next, she is proud.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Out with that too; it was Eve's legacy and cannot be ta'en from her."],"id":622,"m":"Take that out too. It was Eve's legacy, and it can't be taken from her."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She hath no teeth."],"id":623,"m":"\"Next, she has no teeth.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["I care not for that neither, because I love crusts."],"id":624,"m":"I don't mind that either, because I love crusts."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She is curst."],"id":625,"m":"\"Next, she is bad-tempered.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Well, the best is, she hath no teeth to bite."],"id":626,"m":"Well, the good thing is, she has no teeth to bite with."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She will often praise her liquor."],"id":627,"m":"\"Next, she will often praise her drink.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["If her liquor be good, she shall; if she will not, I will, for good","things should be praised."],"id":628,"m":"If her drink is good, she can; if she won't, I will, because good things should be praised."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She is too liberal."],"id":629,"m":"\"Next, she is too free.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Of her tongue she cannot, for that's writ down she is slow of; of her","purse she shall not, for that I'll keep shut. Now, of another thing she","may, and that cannot I help. Well, proceed."],"id":630,"m":"Not with her tongue, because it says she's slow to speak; not with her purse, because I'll keep that shut. Now, she may be free with something else, and I can't help that. Well, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She hath more hair than wit, and more faults than hairs, and","more wealth than faults."],"id":631,"m":"\"Next, she has more hair than brains, and more faults than hairs, and more money than faults.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Stop there; I'll have her. She was mine and not mine twice or thrice in","that last article. Rehearse that once more."],"id":632,"m":"Stop there, I'll have her. She was mine and not mine two or three times in that last item. Read that once more."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Item, She hath more hair than wit—"],"id":633,"m":"\"Next, she has more hair than brains—\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["More hair than wit. It may be; I'll prove it: the cover of the salt","hides the salt, and therefore it is more than the salt; the hair that","covers the wit is more than the wit, for the greater hides the less.","What's next?"],"id":634,"m":"More hair than brains. That may be; I'll prove it. The lid of the salt cellar hides the salt, so it's bigger than the salt; the hair that covers the brains is more than the brains, because the bigger thing hides the smaller. What's next?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["And more faults than hairs."],"id":635,"m":"\"And more faults than hairs.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["That's monstrous! O, that that were out!"],"id":636,"m":"That's monstrous! Oh, I wish that were left out!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["And more wealth than faults."],"id":637,"m":"\"And more money than faults.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, that word makes the faults gracious. Well, I'll have her; an if it","be a match, as nothing is impossible—"],"id":638,"m":"Why, that word makes the faults attractive. Well, I'll have her; and if we marry, since nothing's impossible—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["What then?"],"id":639,"m":"What then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Why, then will I tell thee that thy master stays for thee at the North","Gate."],"id":640,"m":"Why, then I'll tell you that your master is waiting for you at the North Gate."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["For me?"],"id":641,"m":"For me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["For thee? Ay, who art thou? He hath stayed for a better man than thee."],"id":642,"m":"For you? Yes, who do you think you are? He's waited for a better man than you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["And must I go to him?"],"id":643,"m":"And do I have to go to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Thou must run to him, for thou hast stayed so long that going will","scarce serve the turn."],"id":644,"m":"You have to run to him, because you've stayed so long that walking won't be fast enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Why didst not tell me sooner? Pox of your love letters!"],"id":645,"m":"Why didn't you tell me sooner? Plague take your love letters!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":646},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Now will he be swinged for reading my letter; an unmannerly slave, that","will thrust himself into secrets. I'll after, to rejoice in the boy's","correction."],"id":647,"m":"Now he'll be beaten for reading my letter. The rude rascal, poking his nose into secrets. I'll follow him and enjoy watching the boy get punished."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":648},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. A room in the Duke's palace.","id":649},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke and Thurio.","id":650},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sir Thurio, fear not but that she will love you","Now Valentine is banished from her sight."],"id":651,"m":"Sir Thurio, don't worry: she'll love you now that Valentine is banished from her sight."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Since his exile she hath despised me most,","Forsworn my company and railed at me,","That I am desperate of obtaining her."],"id":652,"m":"Since he was exiled she has despised me more than ever. She refuses to see me and abuses me, so I've given up hope of winning her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This weak impress of love is as a figure","Trenched in ice, which with an hour's heat","Dissolves to water and doth lose his form.","A little time will melt her frozen thoughts,","And worthless Valentine shall be forgot."],"id":653,"m":"This faint imprint of love is like a figure carved in ice, which an hour's heat melts to water so that it loses its shape. A little time will thaw her frozen thoughts, and worthless Valentine will be forgotten."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus.","id":654},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["How now, Sir Proteus? Is your countryman,","According to our proclamation, gone?"],"cont":1,"id":655,"m":"Well, Sir Proteus? Has your fellow countryman gone, as our proclamation ordered?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Gone, my good lord."],"id":656,"m":"Gone, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["My daughter takes his going grievously."],"id":657,"m":"My daughter is taking his departure badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["A little time, my lord, will kill that grief."],"id":658,"m":"A little time will kill that grief, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["So I believe, but Thurio thinks not so.","Proteus, the good conceit I hold of thee,","For thou hast shown some sign of good desert,","Makes me the better to confer with thee."],"id":659,"m":"So I believe, but Thurio doesn't think so. Proteus, my good opinion of you, since you've shown signs of being trustworthy, makes me readier to consult you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Longer than I prove loyal to your Grace","Let me not live to look upon your Grace."],"id":660,"m":"If I ever stop being loyal to your grace, let me not live to see your grace again."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Thou know'st how willingly I would effect","The match between Sir Thurio and my daughter?"],"id":661,"m":"You know how much I want to bring about the marriage between Sir Thurio and my daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I do, my lord."],"id":662,"m":"I do, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["And also, I think, thou art not ignorant","How she opposes her against my will?"],"id":663,"m":"And I think you also know how she sets herself against my wishes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["She did, my lord, when Valentine was here."],"id":664,"m":"She did, my lord, while Valentine was here."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Ay, and perversely she persevers so.","What might we do to make the girl forget","The love of Valentine, and love Sir Thurio?"],"id":665,"m":"Yes, and she stubbornly keeps it up. What could we do to make the girl forget her love for Valentine and love Sir Thurio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["The best way is to slander Valentine","With falsehood, cowardice, and poor descent,","Three things that women highly hold in hate."],"id":666,"m":"The best way is to slander Valentine, accusing him of dishonesty, cowardice and low birth, three things women hate most."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Ay, but she'll think that it is spoke in hate."],"id":667,"m":"Yes, but she'll think it's said out of hatred."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Ay, if his enemy deliver it;","Therefore it must with circumstance be spoken","By one whom she esteemeth as his friend."],"id":668,"m":"Yes, if his enemy says it. So it must be said, with convincing detail, by someone she thinks of as his friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Then you must undertake to slander him."],"id":669,"m":"Then you must take on the job of slandering him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["And that, my lord, I shall be loath to do.","'Tis an ill office for a gentleman,","Especially against his very friend."],"id":670,"m":"And that, my lord, I'd be very unwilling to do. It's a nasty job for a gentleman, especially against his own close friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Where your good word cannot advantage him,","Your slander never can endamage him;","Therefore the office is indifferent,","Being entreated to it by your friend."],"id":671,"m":"Since your good word can't help him, your slander can't hurt him. So the job is neither good nor bad, especially when your friend asks you to do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["You have prevailed, my lord. If I can do it","By aught that I can speak in his dispraise,","She shall not long continue love to him.","But say this weed her love from Valentine,","It follows not that she will love Sir Thurio."],"id":672,"m":"You've persuaded me, my lord. If anything I can say against him will do it, she won't go on loving him for long. But say this weeds her love away from Valentine, it doesn't follow that she'll love Sir Thurio."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Therefore, as you unwind her love from him,","Lest it should ravel and be good to none,","You must provide to bottom it on me,","Which must be done by praising me as much","As you in worth dispraise Sir Valentine."],"id":673,"m":"So as you unwind her love from him, in case it tangles and is no good to anyone, you must make sure you wind it onto me. And that must be done by praising me as much as you run down Sir Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["And, Proteus, we dare trust you in this kind","Because we know, on Valentine's report,","You are already Love's firm votary","And cannot soon revolt and change your mind.","Upon this warrant shall you have access","Where you with Silvia may confer at large—","For she is lumpish, heavy, melancholy,","And, for your friend's sake, will be glad of you—","Where you may temper her by your persuasion","To hate young Valentine and love my friend."],"id":674,"m":"And, Proteus, we can trust you in this, because we know from Valentine that you're already a faithful servant of Love and won't quickly turn and change your mind. On that guarantee, you'll be allowed in to see Silvia and talk with her as much as you like. She's dull, heavy and melancholy, and for your friend's sake she'll be glad of your company. There you can use your powers of persuasion to soften her into hating young Valentine and loving my friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["As much as I can do I will effect.","But you, Sir Thurio, are not sharp enough.","You must lay lime to tangle her desires","By wailful sonnets, whose composed rhymes","Should be full-fraught with serviceable vows."],"id":675,"m":"I'll do as much as I can. But you, Sir Thurio, aren't eager enough. You must set traps to catch her desires, with mournful sonnets whose carefully made rhymes are packed with promises of service."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Ay, much is the force of heaven-bred poesy."],"id":676,"m":"Yes, heaven-sent poetry has great power."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Say that upon the altar of her beauty","You sacrifice your tears, your sighs, your heart.","Write till your ink be dry, and with your tears","Moist it again, and frame some feeling line","That may discover such integrity.","For Orpheus' lute was strung with poets' sinews,","Whose golden touch could soften steel and stones,","Make tigers tame, and huge leviathans","Forsake unsounded deeps to dance on sands.","After your dire-lamenting elegies,","Visit by night your lady's chamber-window","With some sweet consort; to their instruments","Tune a deploring dump; the night's dead silence","Will well become such sweet-complaining grievance.","This, or else nothing, will inherit her."],"id":677,"m":"Tell her that on the altar of her beauty you sacrifice your tears, your sighs, your heart. Write till your ink runs dry, then wet it again with your tears, and make up some moving line that shows how true you are. Because Orpheus's lute was strung with poets' heartstrings, and its golden touch could soften steel and stones, tame tigers and make huge sea monsters leave the bottomless deep to dance on the sand. After your sad, lamenting love poems, go by night to your lady's bedroom window with a group of sweet musicians. Have them play a mournful tune; the dead silence of the night will suit such sweet complaints of grief. This will win her, or nothing will."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["This discipline shows thou hast been in love."],"id":678,"m":"Your lessons show you've been in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["And thy advice this night I'll put in practice.","Therefore, sweet Proteus, my direction-giver,","Let us into the city presently","To sort some gentlemen well skilled in music.","I have a sonnet that will serve the turn","To give the onset to thy good advice."],"id":679,"m":"And I'll put your advice into practice tonight. So, sweet Proteus, my instructor, let's go into the city now to pick out some gentlemen who are good at music. I've got a sonnet that will do to make a start on your good advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["About it, gentlemen!"],"id":680,"m":"Get on with it, gentlemen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["We'll wait upon your Grace till after supper,","And afterward determine our proceedings."],"id":681,"m":"We'll attend your grace till after supper, and then decide what to do."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Even now about it! I will pardon you."],"id":682,"m":"Get on with it right now! I excuse you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":683},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"A forest between Milan and Verona.","id":684},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter certain Outlaws.","id":685},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["Fellows, stand fast. I see a passenger."],"id":686,"m":"Hold your ground, lads. I can see a traveller."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["If there be ten, shrink not, but down with 'em."],"id":687,"m":"If there are ten of them, don't back off. Knock them down."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine and Speed.","id":688},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD OUTLAW","lines":["Stand, sir, and throw us that you have about ye.","If not, we'll make you sit, and rifle you."],"id":689,"m":"Stop, sir, and throw down whatever you've got on you. If not, we'll make you sit and search you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Sir, we are undone: these are the villains","That all the travellers do fear so much."],"id":690,"m":"Sir, we're done for. These are the villains every traveller is so afraid of."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["My friends—"],"id":691,"m":"My friends—"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["That's not so, sir. We are your enemies."],"id":692,"m":"That's not so, sir. We're your enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Peace! We'll hear him."],"id":693,"m":"Quiet! Let's hear him."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD OUTLAW","lines":["Ay, by my beard, will we, for he is a proper man."],"id":694,"m":"Yes, by my beard, we will, because he's a fine-looking man."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Then know that I have little wealth to lose.","A man I am crossed with adversity;","My riches are these poor habiliments,","Of which if you should here disfurnish me,","You take the sum and substance that I have."],"id":695,"m":"Then know that I have little money to lose. I'm a man dogged by bad luck. My only wealth is these poor clothes, and if you strip me of them, you take everything I own."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Whither travel you?"],"id":696,"m":"Where are you travelling to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["To Verona."],"id":697,"m":"To Verona."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["Whence came you?"],"id":698,"m":"Where have you come from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["From Milan."],"id":699,"m":"From Milan."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD OUTLAW","lines":["Have you long sojourned there?"],"id":700,"m":"Did you stay there long?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Some sixteen months, and longer might have stayed","If crooked fortune had not thwarted me."],"id":701,"m":"About sixteen months, and I might have stayed longer if crooked fortune hadn't thwarted me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["What, were you banished thence?"],"id":702,"m":"What, were you banished from there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I was."],"id":703,"m":"I was."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["For what offence?"],"id":704,"m":"For what crime?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["For that which now torments me to rehearse;","I killed a man, whose death I much repent,","But yet I slew him manfully in fight,","Without false vantage or base treachery."],"id":705,"m":"For something it torments me to talk about. I killed a man, and I deeply regret his death; but I killed him bravely in a fair fight, without taking unfair advantage or using low tricks."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["Why, ne'er repent it, if it were done so.","But were you banished for so small a fault?"],"id":706,"m":"Why, never regret it, if it was done like that. But were you banished for such a small offence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I was, and held me glad of such a doom."],"id":707,"m":"I was, and I was glad to get such a light sentence."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Have you the tongues?"],"id":708,"m":"Can you speak foreign languages?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["My youthful travel therein made me happy,","Or else I often had been miserable."],"id":709,"m":"My travels as a young man gave me that skill, or I would often have been in trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD OUTLAW","lines":["By the bare scalp of Robin Hood's fat friar,","This fellow were a king for our wild faction."],"id":710,"m":"By the bald head of Robin Hood's fat friar, this fellow would make a king for our wild gang."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["We'll have him. Sirs, a word."],"id":711,"m":"We'll have him. Lads, a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"SPEED","lines":["Master, be one of them. It's an honourable kind of thievery."],"id":712,"m":"Master, join them. It's an honourable kind of thieving."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Peace, villain."],"id":713,"m":"Quiet, you rascal."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Tell us this: have you anything to take to?"],"id":714,"m":"Tell us this: have you anything to live on?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Nothing but my fortune."],"id":715,"m":"Nothing but my luck."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD OUTLAW","lines":["Know then that some of us are gentlemen,","Such as the fury of ungoverned youth","Thrust from the company of awful men.","Myself was from Verona banished","For practising to steal away a lady,","An heir, and near allied unto the Duke."],"id":716,"m":"Then know that some of us are gentlemen, driven out of respectable society by the wildness of our unruly youth. I was banished from Verona for trying to run off with a lady, an heiress and a close relative of the Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["And I from Mantua, for a gentleman","Who, in my mood, I stabbed unto the heart."],"id":717,"m":"And I from Mantua, for a gentleman I stabbed to the heart in a fit of temper."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["And I for suchlike petty crimes as these.","But to the purpose, for we cite our faults,","That they may hold excused our lawless lives;","And partly, seeing you are beautified","With goodly shape, and by your own report","A linguist, and a man of such perfection","As we do in our quality much want—"],"id":718,"m":"And I for petty crimes like these. But to the point. We tell you our crimes to excuse our lawless lives. And partly because we see you're blessed with good looks and, by your own account, can speak languages, and are the sort of accomplished man our trade badly needs—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Indeed because you are a banished man,","Therefore, above the rest, we parley to you.","Are you content to be our general?","To make a virtue of necessity","And live as we do in this wilderness?"],"id":719,"m":"To be honest, because you're a banished man, we're making you this offer above anyone else. Will you agree to be our leader, make a virtue of necessity and live as we do in this wilderness?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD OUTLAW","lines":["What sayst thou? Wilt thou be of our consort?","Say \"Ay\", and be the captain of us all,","We'll do thee homage and be ruled by thee,","Love thee as our commander and our king."],"id":720,"m":"What do you say? Will you join our band? Say \"Yes\" and be captain of us all. We'll swear loyalty to you and be ruled by you, and love you as our commander and our king."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["But if thou scorn our courtesy, thou diest."],"id":721,"m":"But if you scorn our kind offer, you die."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Thou shalt not live to brag what we have offered."],"id":722,"m":"You won't live to boast about what we offered you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I take your offer and will live with you,","Provided that you do no outrages","On silly women or poor passengers."],"id":723,"m":"I accept your offer and will live with you, provided you don't commit any outrages against defenceless women or poor travellers."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD OUTLAW","lines":["No, we detest such vile base practices.","Come, go with us; we'll bring thee to our crews","And show thee all the treasure we have got,","Which, with ourselves, all rest at thy dispose."],"id":724,"m":"No, we hate such vile, low behaviour. Come with us. We'll take you to the rest of our men and show you all the treasure we've got, which, along with ourselves, is all at your command."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":725},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Milan. The court of the Duke's palace.","id":726},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus.","id":727},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Already have I been false to Valentine,","And now I must be as unjust to Thurio.","Under the colour of commending him,","I have access my own love to prefer.","But Silvia is too fair, too true, too holy","To be corrupted with my worthless gifts.","When I protest true loyalty to her,","She twits me with my falsehood to my friend;","When to her beauty I commend my vows,","She bids me think how I have been forsworn","In breaking faith with Julia, whom I loved;","And notwithstanding all her sudden quips,","The least whereof would quell a lover's hope,","Yet, spaniel-like, the more she spurns my love,","The more it grows and fawneth on her still.","But here comes Thurio. Now must we to her window,","And give some evening music to her ear."],"id":728,"m":"I've already been false to Valentine, and now I must be just as unfair to Thurio. Pretending to speak up for him, I get the chance to push my own love. But Silvia is too beautiful, too faithful, too holy to be corrupted by my worthless gifts. When I swear true loyalty to her, she taunts me with my betrayal of my friend. When I offer my vows to her beauty, she tells me to remember how I broke my oath when I broke faith with Julia, whom I loved. And in spite of all her sharp retorts, the least of which would kill a lover's hope, my love, like a spaniel, grows the more she kicks it away, and goes on fawning on her. But here comes Thurio. Now we must go to her window and play her some evening music."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Thurio and Musicians.","id":729},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["How now, Sir Proteus, are you crept before us?"],"id":730,"m":"Well, Sir Proteus, have you crept here before us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Ay, gentle Thurio, for you know that love","Will creep in service where it cannot go."],"id":731,"m":"Yes, gentle Thurio, because you know that love will creep to serve where it can't walk."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Ay, but I hope, sir, that you love not here."],"id":732,"m":"Yes, but I hope, sir, that you don't love here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sir, but I do, or else I would be hence."],"id":733,"m":"But I do, sir, or else I wouldn't be here."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Who? Silvia?"],"id":734,"m":"Who? Silvia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Ay, Silvia, for your sake."],"id":735,"m":"Yes, Silvia, for your sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["I thank you for your own. Now, gentlemen,","Let's tune, and to it lustily awhile."],"id":736,"m":"Thank you for yours. Now, gentlemen, let's tune up and play heartily for a while."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Host and Julia in boy's clothes, as Sebastian.","id":737},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Now, my young guest, methinks you're allycholly. I pray you, why is it?"],"id":738,"m":"Now, my young guest, I think you're melancholy. Please tell me, why is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Marry, mine host, because I cannot be merry."],"id":739,"m":"Well, landlord, because I can't be merry."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Come, we'll have you merry. I'll bring you where you shall hear music,","and see the gentleman that you asked for."],"id":740,"m":"Come on, we'll make you merry. I'll take you where you'll hear music and see the gentleman you asked about."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["But shall I hear him speak?"],"id":741,"m":"But will I hear him speak?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Ay, that you shall."],"id":742,"m":"Yes, you will."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["That will be music."],"id":743,"m":"That will be music to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music plays.","id":744},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Hark, hark!"],"id":745,"m":"Listen, listen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Is he among these?"],"id":746,"m":"Is he among them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Ay; but peace, let's hear 'em."],"id":747,"m":"Yes; but quiet, let's hear them."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["~Who is Silvia? What is she,","~That all our swains commend her?","~Holy, fair, and wise is she;","~The heaven such grace did lend her,","~That she might admired be.","~Is she kind as she is fair?","~For beauty lives with kindness.","~Love doth to her eyes repair,","~To help him of his blindness;","~And, being helped, inhabits there.","~Then to Silvia let us sing,","~That Silvia is excelling;","~She excels each mortal thing","~Upon the dull earth dwelling.","~To her let us garlands bring."],"id":748,"m":"Who is Silvia, and what is she, that all our young men praise her? She is holy, beautiful and wise; heaven gave her such grace so that she would be admired. Is she as kind as she is beautiful? Beauty lives with kindness. Love, who is blind, goes to her eyes to be cured of his blindness, and once cured, he lives there. So let us sing to Silvia that she is supreme. She surpasses every mortal thing that lives on this dull earth. Let us bring her garlands."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["How now, are you sadder than you were before?","How do you, man? The music likes you not."],"id":749,"m":"What's this, are you sadder than you were before? What's the matter, man? Don't you like the music?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["You mistake; the musician likes me not."],"id":750,"m":"You've got it wrong; it's the musician who doesn't like me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Why, my pretty youth?"],"id":751,"m":"Why, my pretty young man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["He plays false, father."],"id":752,"m":"He plays false, old man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["How, out of tune on the strings?"],"id":753,"m":"What, out of tune on the strings?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Not so; but yet so false that he grieves my very heart-strings."],"id":754,"m":"Not that; but so false that he hurts my very heartstrings."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["You have a quick ear."],"id":755,"m":"You have a good ear."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Ay, I would I were deaf; it makes me have a slow heart."],"id":756,"m":"Yes, I wish I were deaf; it makes my heart heavy."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["I perceive you delight not in music."],"id":757,"m":"I can see you don't enjoy music."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Not a whit, when it jars so."],"id":758,"m":"Not a bit, when it's so out of tune."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Hark, what fine change is in the music!"],"id":759,"m":"Listen, what a lovely change of key in the music!"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Ay, that change is the spite."],"id":760,"m":"Yes, that change is what hurts."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["You would have them always play but one thing?"],"id":761,"m":"You'd like them to play only one thing all the time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["I would always have one play but one thing.","But, host, doth this Sir Proteus, that we talk on,","Often resort unto this gentlewoman?"],"id":762,"m":"I'd like one man always to play only one thing. But landlord, does this Sir Proteus we're talking about often visit this lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["I tell you what Lance, his man, told me: he loved her out of all nick."],"id":763,"m":"I'll tell you what Lance, his servant, told me: he loves her beyond all measure."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Where is Lance?"],"id":764,"m":"Where is Lance?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Gone to seek his dog, which tomorrow, by his master's command, he must","carry for a present to his lady."],"id":765,"m":"Gone to look for his dog, which his master has told him to take tomorrow as a present to his lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Peace, stand aside. The company parts."],"id":766,"m":"Quiet, stand aside. The group is breaking up."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sir Thurio, fear not you; I will so plead","That you shall say my cunning drift excels."],"id":767,"m":"Don't worry, Sir Thurio. I'll plead for you so well that you'll say my clever plan is brilliant."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Where meet we?"],"id":768,"m":"Where shall we meet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["At Saint Gregory's well."],"id":769,"m":"At Saint Gregory's well."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Farewell."],"id":770,"m":"Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Thurio and Musicians.","id":771},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Silvia above.","id":772},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Madam, good even to your ladyship."],"id":773,"m":"Madam, good evening to your ladyship."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["I thank you for your music, gentlemen.","Who is that that spake?"],"id":774,"m":"Thank you for your music, gentlemen. Who was that who spoke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["One, lady, if you knew his pure heart's truth,","You would quickly learn to know him by his voice."],"id":775,"m":"Someone, lady, whom you'd quickly learn to recognise by his voice, if you knew how true his heart is."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Sir Proteus, as I take it."],"id":776,"m":"Sir Proteus, I believe."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sir Proteus, gentle lady, and your servant."],"id":777,"m":"Sir Proteus, gentle lady, and your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["What's your will?"],"id":778,"m":"What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["That I may compass yours."],"id":779,"m":"To get what you want."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["You have your wish. My will is even this,","That presently you hie you home to bed.","Thou subtle, perjured, false, disloyal man,","Think'st thou I am so shallow, so conceitless,","To be seduced by thy flattery,","That hast deceived so many with thy vows?","Return, return, and make thy love amends.","For me, by this pale queen of night I swear,","I am so far from granting thy request","That I despise thee for thy wrongful suit,","And by and by intend to chide myself","Even for this time I spend in talking to thee."],"id":780,"m":"You have your wish. What I want is this: that you go home to bed at once. You sly, lying, false, disloyal man, do you think I'm so shallow, so stupid, that I'll be seduced by your flattery, when you've deceived so many with your vows? Go back, go back, and make it up to your love. As for me, I swear by this pale moon that I'm so far from granting your request that I despise you for your wicked courting, and in a moment I'll scold myself for even the time I'm spending talking to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I grant, sweet love, that I did love a lady,","But she is dead."],"id":781,"m":"I admit, sweet love, that I did love a lady, but she's dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] 'Twere false, if I should speak it,","For I am sure she is not buried."],"id":782,"m":"[Aside] That would be a lie if I said it, because I'm sure she's not buried."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Say that she be; yet Valentine thy friend","Survives, to whom, thyself art witness,","I am betrothed. And art thou not ashamed","To wrong him with thy importunacy?"],"id":783,"m":"Suppose she is; still, your friend Valentine is alive, and as you yourself know, I'm engaged to him. Aren't you ashamed to wrong him by pestering me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I likewise hear that Valentine is dead."],"id":784,"m":"I've also heard that Valentine is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["And so suppose am I, for in his grave,","Assure thyself, my love is buried."],"id":785,"m":"Then suppose I am too, because, believe me, my love is buried in his grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sweet lady, let me rake it from the earth."],"id":786,"m":"Sweet lady, let me dig it up from the earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Go to thy lady's grave and call hers thence,","Or, at the least, in hers sepulchre thine."],"id":787,"m":"Go to your lady's grave and call her love out of it, or at least bury yours in hers."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] He heard not that."],"id":788,"m":"[Aside] He didn't hear that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Madam, if your heart be so obdurate,","Vouchsafe me yet your picture for my love,","The picture that is hanging in your chamber;","To that I'll speak, to that I'll sigh and weep;","For since the substance of your perfect self","Is else devoted, I am but a shadow;","And to your shadow will I make true love."],"id":789,"m":"Madam, if your heart is so hard, at least grant me your picture for my love, the picture that hangs in your room. I'll speak to it, sigh and weep to it. Because since the real substance of your perfect self belongs to someone else, I'm only a shadow, and I'll make true love to your shadow."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] If 'twere a substance you would sure deceive it","And make it but a shadow, as I am."],"id":790,"m":"[Aside] If it were a real person, you'd surely deceive it and make it only a shadow, as I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["I am very loath to be your idol, sir;","But since your falsehood shall become you well","To worship shadows and adore false shapes,","Send to me in the morning, and I'll send it.","And so, good rest."],"id":791,"m":"I very much dislike being your idol, sir; but since it suits your falseness to worship shadows and adore false images, send for it in the morning and I'll send it to you. And so, good night."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":792},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["As wretches have o'ernight","That wait for execution in the morn."],"id":793,"m":"The kind of night a wretch has, waiting to be executed in the morning."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":794},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Host, will you go?"],"id":795,"m":"Landlord, shall we go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["By my halidom, I was fast asleep."],"id":796,"m":"By my holy relics, I was fast asleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Pray you, where lies Sir Proteus?"],"id":797,"m":"Tell me, where is Sir Proteus staying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Marry, at my house. Trust me, I think 'tis almost day."],"id":798,"m":"Why, at my inn. Believe me, I think it's almost daybreak."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Not so; but it hath been the longest night","That e'er I watched, and the most heaviest."],"id":799,"m":"Not yet; but it's been the longest night I've ever stayed awake through, and the saddest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":800},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"The same.","id":801},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Eglamour.","id":802},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["This is the hour that Madam Silvia","Entreated me to call and know her mind;","There's some great matter she'd employ me in.","Madam, madam!"],"id":803,"m":"This is the time Madam Silvia asked me to come and find out what she wants. There's some important business she wants me for. Madam, madam!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Silvia above.","id":804},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Who calls?"],"id":805,"m":"Who's calling?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["Your servant and your friend;","One that attends your ladyship's command."],"id":806,"m":"Your servant and your friend; someone who waits on your ladyship's orders."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Sir Eglamour, a thousand times good morrow."],"id":807,"m":"Sir Eglamour, a thousand good mornings."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["As many, worthy lady, to yourself.","According to your ladyship's impose,","I am thus early come to know what service","It is your pleasure to command me in."],"id":808,"m":"As many to you, worthy lady. As your ladyship ordered, I've come this early to find out what service you'd like me to do."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["O Eglamour, thou art a gentleman—","Think not I flatter, for I swear I do not—","Valiant, wise, remorseful, well accomplished.","Thou art not ignorant what dear good will","I bear unto the banished Valentine,","Nor how my father would enforce me marry","Vain Thurio, whom my very soul abhorred.","Thyself hast loved, and I have heard thee say","No grief did ever come so near thy heart","As when thy lady and thy true love died,","Upon whose grave thou vowed'st pure chastity.","Sir Eglamour, I would to Valentine,","To Mantua, where I hear he makes abode;","And for the ways are dangerous to pass,","I do desire thy worthy company,","Upon whose faith and honour I repose.","Urge not my father's anger, Eglamour,","But think upon my grief, a lady's grief,","And on the justice of my flying hence","To keep me from a most unholy match,","Which heaven and fortune still rewards with plagues.","I do desire thee, even from a heart","As full of sorrows as the sea of sands,","To bear me company and go with me;","If not, to hide what I have said to thee,","That I may venture to depart alone."],"id":809,"m":"Oh, Eglamour, you're a gentleman, and don't think I'm flattering you, because I swear I'm not: brave, wise, compassionate, accomplished. You know how much I love the banished Valentine, and how my father would force me to marry foolish Thurio, whom I hate with all my soul. You've loved yourself, and I've heard you say no grief ever cut so close to your heart as when your lady, your true love, died, and you vowed on her grave to live chaste for ever. Sir Eglamour, I want to go to Valentine, to Mantua, where I hear he's living. And since the roads are dangerous to travel, I'd like your worthy company, because I trust your loyalty and honour. Don't remind me of my father's anger, Eglamour. Think of my grief, a lady's grief, and how right I am to run away from here to escape a most unholy marriage, which heaven and fortune always punish with misery. I beg you, from a heart as full of sorrows as the sea is of sand, keep me company and come with me. If not, keep secret what I've told you, so that I can risk going alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["Madam, I pity much your grievances,","Which, since I know they virtuously are placed,","I give consent to go along with you,","Recking as little what betideth me","As much I wish all good befortune you.","When will you go?"],"id":810,"m":"Madam, I feel deeply for your troubles, and since I know your feelings are honourable, I agree to go with you, caring as little about what happens to me as I wish for good things to happen to you. When will you go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["This evening coming."],"id":811,"m":"This evening."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["Where shall I meet you?"],"id":812,"m":"Where shall I meet you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["At Friar Patrick's cell,","Where I intend holy confession."],"id":813,"m":"At Friar Patrick's cell, where I mean to go to confession."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["I will not fail your ladyship. Good morrow, gentle lady."],"id":814,"m":"I won't let your ladyship down. Good morning, gentle lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Good morrow, kind Sir Eglamour."],"id":815,"m":"Good morning, kind Sir Eglamour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":816},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"The same.","id":817},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lance with his dog Crab.","id":818},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["When a man's servant shall play the cur with him, look you, it goes","hard: one that I brought up of a puppy; one that I saved from drowning","when three or four of his blind brothers and sisters went to it. I have","taught him even as one would say precisely, \"Thus I would teach a dog.\"","I was sent to deliver him as a present to Mistress Silvia from my","master; and I came no sooner into the dining-chamber but he steps me to","her trencher and steals her capon's leg. O, 'tis a foul thing when a","cur cannot keep himself in all companies! I would have, as one should","say, one that takes upon him to be a dog indeed, to be, as it were, a","dog at all things. If I had not had more wit than he, to take a fault","upon me that he did, I think verily he had been hanged for't; sure as I","live, he had suffered for't. You shall judge. He thrusts me himself","into the company of three or four gentleman-like dogs under the Duke's","table; he had not been there—bless the mark!—a pissing-while but all","the chamber smelt him. \"Out with the dog!\" says one; \"What cur is","that?\" says another; \"Whip him out\", says the third; \"Hang him up\",","says the Duke. I, having been acquainted with the smell before, knew it","was Crab, and goes me to the fellow that whips the dogs. \"Friend,\"","quoth I, \"you mean to whip the dog?\" \"Ay, marry do I,\" quoth he. \"You do","him the more wrong,\" quoth I. \"'Twas I did the thing you wot of.\" He","makes me no more ado but whips me out of the chamber. How many masters","would do this for his servant? Nay, I'll be sworn I have sat in the","stock for puddings he hath stolen, otherwise he had been executed. I","have stood on the pillory for geese he hath killed, otherwise he had","suffered for't. [To Crab] Thou think'st not of this now. Nay, I","remember the trick you served me when I took my leave of Madam Silvia.","Did not I bid thee still mark me, and do as I do? When didst thou see","me heave up my leg and make water against a gentlewoman's farthingale?","Didst thou ever see me do such a trick?"],"id":819,"m":"When a man's servant behaves like a cur to him, you see, it's hard: one I brought up from a puppy, one I saved from drowning when three or four of his blind brothers and sisters went that way. I've trained him exactly as you'd say, \"That's how I'd train a dog.\" I was sent to give him as a present to Mistress Silvia from my master, and I'd hardly got into the dining room before he trots up to her plate and steals her chicken leg. Oh, it's a terrible thing when a dog can't behave himself in company! I'd want one that took it on himself to be a real dog, a dog, so to speak, at everything. If I hadn't had more sense than him and taken the blame for something he did, I truly think he'd have been hanged for it; as sure as I'm alive, he'd have paid for it. You can judge for yourselves. He pushes in among three or four gentlemanly dogs under the Duke's table, and he hadn't been there, excuse me, long enough to have a pee before the whole room could smell him. \"Out with the dog!\" says one; \"What mutt is that?\" says another; \"Whip him out,\" says a third; \"Hang him,\" says the Duke. I knew the smell already, so I knew it was Crab, and I go up to the fellow who whips the dogs. \"Friend,\" I say, \"are you going to whip the dog?\" \"Yes, I certainly am,\" he says. \"Then you do him all the more wrong,\" I say. \"It was me who did the thing you know about.\" Without more fuss he whips me out of the room. How many masters would do that for their servant? No, I swear I've sat in the stocks for puddings he's stolen, or else he'd have been executed. I've stood in the pillory for geese he's killed, or else he'd have suffered for it. [To Crab] You don't think about that now. No, I remember the trick you played on me when I said goodbye to Madam Silvia. Didn't I tell you always to watch me and do as I do? When did you ever see me lift my leg and pee against a lady's hooped skirt? Did you ever see me do a trick like that?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus and Julia disguised as Sebastian.","id":820},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sebastian is thy name? I like thee well,","And will employ thee in some service presently."],"id":821,"m":"Your name is Sebastian? I like you very much, and I'll give you some work to do right away."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["In what you please; I'll do what I can."],"id":822,"m":"Whatever you like; I'll do what I can."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I hope thou wilt. [To Lance] How now, you whoreson peasant,","Where have you been these two days loitering?"],"id":823,"m":"I hope you will. [To Lance] Well, you son-of-a-whore peasant, where have you been loafing these two days?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Marry, sir, I carried Mistress Silvia the dog you bade me."],"id":824,"m":"Why, sir, I took Mistress Silvia the dog you told me to."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["And what says she to my little jewel?"],"id":825,"m":"And what does she say about my little jewel?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Marry, she says your dog was a cur, and tells you currish thanks is","good enough for such a present."],"id":826,"m":"Well, she says your dog was a mutt, and that a dog's thanks is good enough for a present like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["But she received my dog?"],"id":827,"m":"But she accepted my dog?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["No, indeed, did she not. Here have I brought him back again."],"id":828,"m":"No, she certainly didn't. I've brought him back here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["What, didst thou offer her this from me?"],"id":829,"m":"What, did you offer her this one from me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LANCE","lines":["Ay, sir, the other squirrel was stolen from me by the hangman's boys in","the market-place, and then I offered her mine own, who is a dog as big","as ten of yours, and therefore the gift the greater."],"id":830,"m":"Yes, sir. The other little squirrel of a dog was stolen from me by the hangman's boys in the market square, and then I offered her my own, who's a dog as big as ten of yours, and so a bigger present."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Go, get thee hence, and find my dog again,","Or ne'er return again into my sight.","Away, I say. Stayest thou to vex me here?","A slave that still an end turns me to shame."],"id":831,"m":"Go on, get out of here and find my dog, or never come into my sight again. Go, I say. Are you staying here to annoy me? A slave who always brings me shame."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lance with Crab.","id":832},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Sebastian, I have entertained thee","Partly that I have need of such a youth","That can with some discretion do my business—","For 'tis no trusting to yond foolish lout—","But chiefly for thy face and thy behaviour,","Which, if my augury deceive me not,","Witness good bringing up, fortune, and truth.","Therefore, know thou, for this I entertain thee.","Go presently, and take this ring with thee,","Deliver it to Madam Silvia.","She loved me well delivered it to me."],"cont":1,"id":833,"m":"Sebastian, I've taken you on partly because I need a young man like you who can do my business with some discretion, since there's no trusting that foolish oaf; but mainly because of your face and your manner, which, if my judgement isn't wrong, show a good upbringing, good fortune and honesty. So know that this is why I'm employing you. Go at once and take this ring with you. Give it to Madam Silvia. The woman who gave it to me loved me very much."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["It seems you loved not her, to leave her token.","She's dead belike?"],"id":834,"m":"It seems you didn't love her, if you're giving away her keepsake. Is she dead, perhaps?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Not so; I think she lives."],"id":835,"m":"No; I think she's alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Alas!"],"id":836,"m":"Oh dear!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Why dost thou cry \"Alas\"?"],"id":837,"m":"Why do you say \"Oh dear\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["I cannot choose","But pity her."],"id":838,"m":"I can't help pitying her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Wherefore shouldst thou pity her?"],"id":839,"m":"Why should you pity her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Because methinks that she loved you as well","As you do love your lady Silvia.","She dreams on him that has forgot her love;","You dote on her that cares not for your love.","'Tis pity love should be so contrary;","And thinking on it makes me cry \"Alas.\""],"id":840,"m":"Because I think she loved you as much as you love your lady Silvia. She dreams of the man who has forgotten her love; you dote on the woman who cares nothing for yours. It's a pity love should be so contrary, and thinking about it makes me cry \"Oh dear.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Well, give her that ring, and therewithal","This letter. That's her chamber. Tell my lady","I claim the promise for her heavenly picture.","Your message done, hie home unto my chamber,","Where thou shalt find me sad and solitary."],"id":841,"m":"Well, give her that ring, and this letter with it. That's her room. Tell my lady I'm claiming the heavenly picture she promised me. When you've delivered your message, hurry home to my room, where you'll find me sad and alone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":842},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["How many women would do such a message?","Alas, poor Proteus, thou hast entertained","A fox to be the shepherd of thy lambs.","Alas, poor fool, why do I pity him","That with his very heart despiseth me?","Because he loves her, he despiseth me;","Because I love him, I must pity him.","This ring I gave him when he parted from me,","To bind him to remember my good will;","And now am I, unhappy messenger,","To plead for that which I would not obtain,","To carry that which I would have refused,","To praise his faith, which I would have dispraised.","I am my master's true confirmed love,","But cannot be true servant to my master","Unless I prove false traitor to myself.","Yet will I woo for him, but yet so coldly","As, heaven it knows, I would not have him speed."],"id":843,"m":"How many women would carry a message like this? Oh, poor Proteus, you've hired a fox to look after your lambs. Oh, you poor fool, why do I pity him, when he despises me with all his heart? Because he loves her, he despises me; because I love him, I have to pity him. I gave him this ring when he left me, to make him remember my love; and now I, an unhappy messenger, have to plead for something I don't want him to get, carry something I'd like to see refused, and praise his faithfulness, which I'd like to criticise. I'm my master's true and constant love, but I can't be a true servant to my master without being a false traitor to myself. Still, I'll woo her for him, but so half-heartedly that, heaven knows, I don't want him to succeed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Silvia attended.","id":844},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Gentlewoman, good day. I pray you be my mean","To bring me where to speak with Madam Silvia."],"cont":1,"id":845,"m":"Good day, lady. Please help me find where I can speak with Madam Silvia."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["What would you with her, if that I be she?"],"id":846,"m":"What do you want with her, if I'm she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["If you be she, I do entreat your patience","To hear me speak the message I am sent on."],"id":847,"m":"If you are, please be patient and hear the message I've been sent with."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["From whom?"],"id":848,"m":"From whom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["From my master, Sir Proteus, madam."],"id":849,"m":"From my master, Sir Proteus, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["O, he sends you for a picture?"],"id":850,"m":"Oh, he's sent you for a picture?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Ay, madam."],"id":851,"m":"Yes, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Ursula, bring my picture there."],"id":852,"m":"Ursula, bring my picture here."},{"t":"sd","text":"She is brought the picture.","id":853},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Go, give your master this. Tell him from me,","One Julia, that his changing thoughts forget,","Would better fit his chamber than this shadow."],"cont":1,"id":854,"m":"Go and give your master this. Tell him from me that a certain Julia, whom his fickle mind forgets, would suit his room better than this shadow."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Madam, please you peruse this letter."],"id":855,"m":"Madam, please read this letter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives her a letter.","id":856},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Pardon me, madam, I have unadvised","Delivered you a paper that I should not.","This is the letter to your ladyship."],"cont":1,"id":857,"m":"Forgive me, madam, I've carelessly given you a paper I shouldn't have. This is the letter to your ladyship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Takes back the letter and gives her another.","id":858},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["I pray thee, let me look on that again."],"id":859,"m":"Please, let me look at that one again."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["It may not be. Good madam, pardon me."],"id":860,"m":"I can't, good madam. Forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["There, hold.","I will not look upon your master's lines.","I know they are stuffed with protestations","And full of new-found oaths, which he will break","As easily as I do tear his paper."],"id":861,"m":"There, take it. I won't look at your master's writing. I know it's stuffed with declarations and full of new oaths, which he'll break as easily as I tear up his paper."},{"t":"sd","text":"She tears the second letter.","id":862},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Madam, he sends your ladyship this ring."],"id":863,"m":"Madam, he sends your ladyship this ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["The more shame for him that he sends it me;","For I have heard him say a thousand times","His Julia gave it him at his departure.","Though his false finger have profaned the ring,","Mine shall not do his Julia so much wrong."],"id":864,"m":"All the more shame to him for sending it to me, because I've heard him say a thousand times that his Julia gave it to him when he left. Though his false finger has dishonoured the ring, mine won't do his Julia such a wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["She thanks you."],"id":865,"m":"She thanks you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["What sayst thou?"],"id":866,"m":"What did you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["I thank you, madam, that you tender her.","Poor gentlewoman, my master wrongs her much."],"id":867,"m":"I thank you, madam, for being kind to her. Poor lady, my master wrongs her badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Dost thou know her?"],"id":868,"m":"Do you know her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Almost as well as I do know myself.","To think upon her woes, I do protest","That I have wept a hundred several times."],"id":869,"m":"Almost as well as I know myself. I swear I've wept a hundred times thinking about her troubles."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Belike she thinks that Proteus hath forsook her?"],"id":870,"m":"I suppose she thinks Proteus has abandoned her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["I think she doth, and that's her cause of sorrow."],"id":871,"m":"I think she does, and that's the cause of her sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Is she not passing fair?"],"id":872,"m":"Isn't she very beautiful?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["She hath been fairer, madam, than she is.","When she did think my master loved her well,","She, in my judgement, was as fair as you.","But since she did neglect her looking-glass","And threw her sun-expelling mask away,","The air hath starved the roses in her cheeks","And pinched the lily-tincture of her face,","That now she is become as black as I."],"id":873,"m":"She has been more beautiful than she is now, madam. When she believed my master loved her, she was, in my opinion, as beautiful as you. But since she stopped looking in her mirror and threw away the mask that kept off the sun, the weather has withered the roses in her cheeks and shrivelled the lily-white of her face, so that now she's become as brown as I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["How tall was she?"],"id":874,"m":"How tall was she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["About my stature; for at Pentecost,","When all our pageants of delight were played,","Our youth got me to play the woman's part,","And I was trimmed in Madam Julia's gown,","Which served me as fit, by all men's judgements,","As if the garment had been made for me;","Therefore I know she is about my height.","And at that time I made her weep agood,","For I did play a lamentable part.","Madam, 'twas Ariadne, passioning","For Theseus' perjury and unjust flight,","Which I so lively acted with my tears","That my poor mistress, moved therewithal,","Wept bitterly; and would I might be dead","If I in thought felt not her very sorrow."],"id":875,"m":"About my height. At Whitsun, when all our entertainments were put on, the young people got me to play the woman's part, and I was dressed in Madam Julia's gown, which everyone said fitted me as well as if it had been made for me. So I know she's about my height. And that time I made her cry her heart out, because I played a sad part. Madam, it was Ariadne, grieving over Theseus's broken vows and his cruel desertion. I acted it so vividly with my tears that my poor mistress was moved and wept bitterly; and I wish I may die if I didn't feel her sorrow in my own heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["She is beholding to thee, gentle youth.","Alas, poor lady, desolate and left!","I weep myself to think upon thy words.","Here, youth, there is my purse. I give thee this","For thy sweet mistress' sake, because thou lov'st her.","Farewell."],"id":876,"m":"She owes you a lot, gentle youth. Oh, poor lady, deserted and alone! It makes me cry myself to think about what you've said. Here, young man, here's my purse. I give you this for your sweet mistress's sake, because you love her. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And she shall thank you for't, if e'er you know her."],"id":877,"m":"And she'll thank you for it, if you ever meet her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Silvia and Attendants.","id":878},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["A virtuous gentlewoman, mild and beautiful.","I hope my master's suit will be but cold,","Since she respects my mistress' love so much.","Alas, how love can trifle with itself!","Here is her picture; let me see. I think","If I had such a tire, this face of mine","Were full as lovely as is this of hers;","And yet the painter flattered her a little,","Unless I flatter with myself too much.","Her hair is auburn, mine is perfect yellow;","If that be all the difference in his love,","I'll get me such a coloured periwig.","Her eyes are grey as glass, and so are mine.","Ay, but her forehead's low, and mine's as high.","What should it be that he respects in her","But I can make respective in myself,","If this fond Love were not a blinded god?","Come, shadow, come, and take this shadow up,","For 'tis thy rival. O thou senseless form,","Thou shalt be worshipped, kissed, loved, and adored;","And were there sense in his idolatry,","My substance should be statue in thy stead.","I'll use thee kindly for thy mistress' sake,","That used me so; or else, by Jove I vow,","I should have scratched out your unseeing eyes","To make my master out of love with thee."],"cont":1,"id":879,"m":"A virtuous lady, gentle and beautiful. I hope my master's wooing gets nowhere, since she cares so much about my mistress's love. Oh, how love can play games with itself! Here's her picture; let me look. I think if I had a headdress like that, this face of mine would be every bit as lovely as hers. And yet the painter flattered her a little, unless I'm flattering myself too much. Her hair is auburn; mine is perfectly golden. If that's the only difference in his love, I'll get myself a wig that colour. Her eyes are as grey as glass, and so are mine. Yes, but her forehead is low, and mine is high. What can it be that he values in her that I couldn't make him value in me, if foolish Love weren't a blind god? Come, shadow, come and pick up this shadow, because it's your rival. Oh, you unfeeling image, you'll be worshipped, kissed, loved and adored; and if there were any sense in his idolatry, my real self would be the statue in your place. I'll treat you kindly for your mistress's sake, since she treated me kindly; or else, I swear by Jove, I'd have scratched out your unseeing eyes to make my master fall out of love with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":880},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Milan. An abbey.","id":881},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Eglamour.","id":882},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["The sun begins to gild the western sky,","And now it is about the very hour","That Silvia at Friar Patrick's cell should meet me.","She will not fail, for lovers break not hours,","Unless it be to come before their time,","So much they spur their expedition."],"id":883,"m":"The sun is beginning to turn the western sky gold, and now it's just about the hour when Silvia should meet me at Friar Patrick's cell. She won't fail, because lovers don't miss appointments, unless it's to come early, they're in such a hurry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Silvia.","id":884},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["See where she comes. Lady, a happy evening!"],"cont":1,"id":885,"m":"Look, here she comes. Lady, a happy evening!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Amen, amen. Go on, good Eglamour,","Out at the postern by the abbey wall.","I fear I am attended by some spies."],"id":886,"m":"Amen to that. Lead on, good Eglamour, out through the back gate by the abbey wall. I'm afraid I'm being followed by spies."},{"t":"sp","who":"EGLAMOUR","lines":["Fear not. The forest is not three leagues off;","If we recover that, we are sure enough."],"id":887,"m":"Don't worry. The forest is less than three leagues away. If we get there, we're safe enough."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":888},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. A room in the Duke's palace.","id":889},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Thurio, Proteus and Julia.","id":890},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Sir Proteus, what says Silvia to my suit?"],"id":891,"m":"Sir Proteus, what does Silvia say to my courtship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["O, sir, I find her milder than she was,","And yet she takes exceptions at your person."],"id":892,"m":"Oh, sir, I find her gentler than she was, and yet she objects to your appearance."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["What? That my leg is too long?"],"id":893,"m":"What? That my leg is too long?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["No, that it is too little."],"id":894,"m":"No, that it's too thin."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["I'll wear a boot to make it somewhat rounder."],"id":895,"m":"I'll wear a boot to make it a bit rounder."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] But love will not be spurred to what it loathes."],"id":896,"m":"[Aside] But you can't spur love towards something it hates."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["What says she to my face?"],"id":897,"m":"What does she say about my face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["She says it is a fair one."],"id":898,"m":"She says it's a fair one."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Nay, then, the wanton lies; my face is black."],"id":899,"m":"No, then the flirt is lying; my face is dark."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["But pearls are fair; and the old saying is,","\"Black men are pearls in beauteous ladies' eyes.\""],"id":900,"m":"But pearls are fair, and the old saying goes, \"Dark men are pearls in beautiful ladies' eyes.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] 'Tis true, such pearls as put out ladies' eyes,","For I had rather wink than look on them."],"id":901,"m":"[Aside] That's true: the kind of pearls, cataracts, that blind ladies' eyes, because I'd rather shut my eyes than look at them."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["How likes she my discourse?"],"id":902,"m":"How does she like my conversation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Ill, when you talk of war."],"id":903,"m":"Badly, when you talk about war."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["But well when I discourse of love and peace."],"id":904,"m":"But well when I talk about love and peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] But better, indeed, when you hold your peace."],"id":905,"m":"[Aside] But better, actually, when you hold your peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["What says she to my valour?"],"id":906,"m":"What does she say about my courage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["O, sir, she makes no doubt of that."],"id":907,"m":"Oh, sir, she has no doubt about that."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] She needs not, when she knows it cowardice."],"id":908,"m":"[Aside] She doesn't need any, when she knows it's cowardice."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["What says she to my birth?"],"id":909,"m":"What does she say about my birth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["That you are well derived."],"id":910,"m":"That you're well descended."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] True, from a gentleman to a fool."],"id":911,"m":"[Aside] True: descended from a gentleman to a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Considers she my possessions?"],"id":912,"m":"Does she think about my estates?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["O, ay, and pities them."],"id":913,"m":"Oh yes, and she pities them."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Wherefore?"],"id":914,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] That such an ass should owe them."],"id":915,"m":"[Aside] Because such an ass owns them."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["That they are out by lease."],"id":916,"m":"Because they're let out on lease."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Here comes the Duke."],"id":917,"m":"Here comes the Duke."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Duke.","id":918},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["How now, Sir Proteus! How now, Thurio!","Which of you saw Sir Eglamour of late?"],"id":919,"m":"Well, Sir Proteus! Well, Thurio! Which of you has seen Sir Eglamour lately?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Not I."],"id":920,"m":"Not I."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Nor I."],"id":921,"m":"Nor I."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Saw you my daughter?"],"id":922,"m":"Have you seen my daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Neither."],"id":923,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Why then, she's fled unto that peasant Valentine,","And Eglamour is in her company.","'Tis true, for Friar Lawrence met them both","As he in penance wandered through the forest;","Him he knew well, and guessed that it was she,","But, being masked, he was not sure of it.","Besides, she did intend confession","At Patrick's cell this even, and there she was not.","These likelihoods confirm her flight from hence.","Therefore, I pray you, stand not to discourse,","But mount you presently and meet with me","Upon the rising of the mountain foot","That leads toward Mantua, whither they are fled.","Dispatch, sweet gentlemen, and follow me."],"id":924,"m":"Why then, she's run off to that peasant Valentine, and Eglamour is with her. It's true, because Friar Lawrence met them both as he was wandering through the forest doing penance. He knew Eglamour well and guessed it was her, but because she was masked he wasn't sure. Besides, she meant to go to confession at Patrick's cell this evening, and she wasn't there. These signs confirm that she's run away from here. So please don't stand here talking, but mount your horses at once and meet me at the foot of the hills on the road to Mantua, where they've fled. Hurry, sweet gentlemen, and follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":925},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Why, this it is to be a peevish girl","That flies her fortune when it follows her.","I'll after, more to be revenged on Eglamour","Than for the love of reckless Silvia."],"id":926,"m":"Why, this is what it is to be a contrary girl, who runs away from good fortune when it follows her. I'll go after her, more to get revenge on Eglamour than for love of reckless Silvia."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":927},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["And I will follow, more for Silvia's love","Than hate of Eglamour that goes with her."],"id":928,"m":"And I'll follow, more for love of Silvia than hatred of Eglamour, who's gone with her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":929},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And I will follow, more to cross that love","Than hate for Silvia, that is gone for love."],"id":930,"m":"And I'll follow, more to block that love than out of hatred for Silvia, who's gone for love."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":931},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Frontiers of Mantua. The forest.","id":932},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Silvia and Outlaws.","id":933},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["Come, come, be patient. We must bring you to our captain."],"id":934,"m":"Come on, come on, be patient. We have to take you to our captain."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["A thousand more mischances than this one","Have learned me how to brook this patiently."],"id":935,"m":"A thousand worse misfortunes than this one have taught me to bear it patiently."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Come, bring her away."],"id":936,"m":"Come on, bring her along."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["Where is the gentleman that was with her?"],"id":937,"m":"Where's the gentleman who was with her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","lines":["Being nimble-footed, he hath outrun us.","But Moyses and Valerius follow him.","Go thou with her to the west end of the wood;","There is our captain. We'll follow him that's fled.","The thicket is beset; he cannot 'scape."],"id":938,"m":"He was quick on his feet and outran us. But Moyses and Valerius are after him. You take her to the west end of the wood; our captain is there. We'll follow the one who ran. The thicket is surrounded; he can't escape."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Second and Third Outlaws.","id":939},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OUTLAW","lines":["Come, I must bring you to our captain's cave.","Fear not; he bears an honourable mind","And will not use a woman lawlessly."],"id":940,"m":"Come, I have to take you to our captain's cave. Don't be afraid. He's an honourable man and won't treat a woman lawlessly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["O Valentine, this I endure for thee!"],"id":941,"m":"Oh, Valentine, I'm going through this for you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":942},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another part of the forest.","id":943},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine.","id":944},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["How use doth breed a habit in a man!","This shadowy desert, unfrequented woods,","I better brook than flourishing peopled towns.","Here can I sit alone, unseen of any,","And to the nightingale's complaining notes","Tune my distresses and record my woes.","O thou that dost inhabit in my breast,","Leave not the mansion so long tenantless,","Lest, growing ruinous, the building fall","And leave no memory of what it was.","Repair me with thy presence, Silvia;","Thou gentle nymph, cherish thy forlorn swain."],"id":945,"m":"How custom turns into habit in a man! I like this shady wilderness, these lonely woods, better than busy, crowded towns. Here I can sit alone, seen by no one, and match my troubles to the nightingale's sad song and sing out my sorrows. Oh, you who live in my heart, don't leave the house empty for so long, in case it falls into ruin, collapses and leaves no memory of what it was. Rebuild me with your presence, Silvia. Gentle nymph, comfort your lonely shepherd."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shouts within.","id":946},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["What hallowing and what stir is this today?","These are my mates, that make their wills their law,","Have some unhappy passenger in chase.","They love me well; yet I have much to do","To keep them from uncivil outrages.","Withdraw thee, Valentine. Who's this comes here?"],"cont":1,"id":947,"m":"What shouting and commotion is this today? It's my companions, who make their own wishes their law, chasing some unlucky traveller. They love me well, yet I have a hard job keeping them from savage outrages. Step aside, Valentine. Who's this coming here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Steps aside.","id":948},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Proteus, Silvia and Julia as Sebastian.","id":949},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Madam, this service I have done for you—","Though you respect not aught your servant doth—","To hazard life, and rescue you from him","That would have forced your honour and your love.","Vouchsafe me for my meed but one fair look;","A smaller boon than this I cannot beg,","And less than this, I am sure, you cannot give."],"id":950,"m":"Madam, I've done you this service, though you care nothing for anything your servant does: I've risked my life to rescue you from the man who would have taken your honour and your love by force. As my reward, grant me just one kind look. I can't ask for a smaller favour than this, and I'm sure you can't give less."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["[Aside] How like a dream is this I see and hear!","Love, lend me patience to forbear awhile."],"id":951,"m":"[Aside] This is like a dream, what I'm seeing and hearing! Love, give me patience to hold back a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["O miserable, unhappy that I am!"],"id":952,"m":"Oh, how wretched and unhappy I am!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Unhappy were you, madam, ere I came;","But by my coming I have made you happy."],"id":953,"m":"You were unhappy, madam, before I came; but by coming I've made you happy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["By thy approach thou mak'st me most unhappy."],"id":954,"m":"By coming near me you make me most unhappy."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["[Aside] And me, when he approacheth to your presence."],"id":955,"m":"[Aside] And me, when he comes near you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["Had I been seized by a hungry lion,","I would have been a breakfast to the beast","Rather than have false Proteus rescue me.","O heaven, be judge how I love Valentine,","Whose life's as tender to me as my soul!","And full as much, for more there cannot be,","I do detest false perjured Proteus.","Therefore be gone, solicit me no more."],"id":956,"m":"If I'd been caught by a hungry lion, I would rather have been the beast's breakfast than have false Proteus rescue me. Oh, heaven, judge how much I love Valentine, whose life is as dear to me as my soul! And just as much, since there can't be more, I hate false, oath-breaking Proteus. So go away, and stop pestering me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["What dangerous action, stood it next to death,","Would I not undergo for one calm look!","O, 'tis the curse in love, and still approved,","When women cannot love where they're beloved."],"id":957,"m":"What dangerous deed, even one that came close to death, wouldn't I undertake for one gentle look! Oh, it's the curse of love, and always proved true, when women can't love where they are loved."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["When Proteus cannot love where he's beloved.","Read over Julia's heart, thy first best love,","For whose dear sake thou didst then rend thy faith","Into a thousand oaths; and all those oaths","Descended into perjury to love me.","Thou hast no faith left now, unless thou'dst two,","And that's far worse than none; better have none","Than plural faith, which is too much by one.","Thou counterfeit to thy true friend!"],"id":958,"m":"When Proteus can't love where he's loved. Read Julia's heart again, your first and best love, for whose dear sake you tore your loyalty into a thousand oaths; and all those oaths sank into lies when you loved me. You have no faith left now, unless you have two, and that's far worse than none. Better to have none than a double faith, which is one too many. You fake, to your true friend!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["In love","Who respects friend?"],"id":959,"m":"In love, who cares about a friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["All men but Proteus."],"id":960,"m":"Every man but Proteus."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Nay, if the gentle spirit of moving words","Can no way change you to a milder form,","I'll woo you like a soldier, at arms' end,","And love you 'gainst the nature of love—force ye."],"id":961,"m":"No, if the gentle power of moving words can't soften you at all, I'll woo you like a soldier, with weapons, and love you against the nature of love: I'll force you."},{"t":"sd","text":"He seizes her.","id":962},{"t":"sp","who":"SILVIA","lines":["O heaven!"],"id":963,"m":"Oh, heaven!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["I'll force thee yield to my desire."],"id":964,"m":"I'll force you to give in to my desire."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["[Comes forward] Ruffian, let go that rude uncivil touch,","Thou friend of an ill fashion!"],"id":965,"m":"[Comes forward] Thug, let go of her, that's a brutal, savage touch, you false kind of friend!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Valentine!"],"id":966,"m":"Valentine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Thou common friend, that's without faith or love,","For such is a friend now. Treacherous man,","Thou hast beguiled my hopes; nought but mine eye","Could have persuaded me. Now I dare not say","I have one friend alive; thou wouldst disprove me.","Who should be trusted, when one's right hand","Is perjured to the bosom? Proteus,","I am sorry I must never trust thee more,","But count the world a stranger for thy sake.","The private wound is deepest. O time most accurst,","'Mongst all foes that a friend should be the worst!"],"id":967,"m":"You worthless friend, without loyalty or love, because that's what friends are like now. Treacherous man, you've cheated my hopes. Nothing but my own eyes could have convinced me. Now I don't dare say I have one friend alive; you'd prove me wrong. Who can be trusted, when your own right hand betrays your heart? Proteus, I'm sorry that I must never trust you again, but treat the whole world as a stranger because of you. The private wound is the deepest. Oh, most cursed time, that of all enemies a friend should be the worst!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["My shame and guilt confounds me.","Forgive me, Valentine; if hearty sorrow","Be a sufficient ransom for offence,","I tender 't here. I do as truly suffer","As e'er I did commit."],"id":968,"m":"My shame and guilt overwhelm me. Forgive me, Valentine. If heartfelt sorrow is enough to pay for the wrong, I offer it here. I suffer as truly as I ever sinned."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Then I am paid,","And once again I do receive thee honest.","Who by repentance is not satisfied","Is nor of heaven nor earth, for these are pleased;","By penitence th' Eternal's wrath's appeased.","And that my love may appear plain and free,","All that was mine in Silvia I give thee."],"id":969,"m":"Then I'm repaid, and once again I accept you as an honest man. Anyone who isn't satisfied by repentance belongs neither to heaven nor earth, because they are both satisfied by it; repentance calms even God's anger. And so that my love for you is plain and generous, I give you everything that was mine in Silvia."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["O me unhappy!"],"id":970,"m":"Oh, how unhappy I am!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Swoons.","id":971},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Look to the boy."],"id":972,"m":"Look after the boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Why, boy!","Why, wag! How now? What's the matter? Look up; speak."],"id":973,"m":"Why, boy! Why, lad! What's this? What's the matter? Look up; speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["O good sir, my master charged me to deliver a ring to Madam Silvia,","which out of my neglect was never done."],"id":974,"m":"Oh, good sir, my master told me to deliver a ring to Madam Silvia, and I carelessly never did it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Where is that ring, boy?"],"id":975,"m":"Where's that ring, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Here 'tis; this is it."],"id":976,"m":"Here it is; this is it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives him a ring.","id":977},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["How, let me see.","Why, this is the ring I gave to Julia."],"id":978,"m":"What? Let me see. Why, this is the ring I gave to Julia."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["O, cry you mercy, sir, I have mistook.","This is the ring you sent to Silvia."],"id":979,"m":"Oh, I beg your pardon, sir, I've made a mistake. This is the ring you sent to Silvia."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shows another ring.","id":980},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["But how cam'st thou by this ring? At my depart","I gave this unto Julia."],"id":981,"m":"But how did you get this ring? When I left, I gave it to Julia."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And Julia herself did give it me,","And Julia herself have brought it hither."],"id":982,"m":"And Julia herself gave it to me, and Julia herself has brought it here."},{"t":"sd","text":"She reveals herself.","id":983},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["How? Julia?"],"id":984,"m":"What? Julia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["Behold her that gave aim to all thy oaths","And entertained 'em deeply in her heart.","How oft hast thou with perjury cleft the root!","O Proteus, let this habit make thee blush.","Be thou ashamed that I have took upon me","Such an immodest raiment, if shame live","In a disguise of love.","It is the lesser blot, modesty finds,","Women to change their shapes than men their minds."],"id":985,"m":"Look at the woman who was the target of all your oaths and held them deep in her heart. How often you've split that heart to the root with your lies! Oh, Proteus, let these clothes make you blush. Be ashamed that I've had to put on such immodest clothing, if there can be shame in a disguise put on for love. Modesty finds it a smaller stain for women to change their appearance than for men to change their minds."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Than men their minds! 'Tis true. O heaven, were man","But constant, he were perfect. That one error","Fills him with faults, makes him run through all th' sins;","Inconstancy falls off ere it begins.","What is in Silvia's face but I may spy","More fresh in Julia's with a constant eye?"],"id":986,"m":"Than men their minds! It's true. Oh heaven, if a man were only constant, he'd be perfect. That one fault fills him with faults and drives him through every sin. Inconstancy gives up before it has properly begun. What is there in Silvia's face that I couldn't see, fresher, in Julia's, with a faithful eye?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Come, come, a hand from either.","Let me be blest to make this happy close.","'Twere pity two such friends should be long foes."],"id":987,"m":"Come on, come on, a hand from each of you. Let me have the blessing of making this happy ending. It would be a pity for two such friends to be enemies for long."},{"t":"sp","who":"PROTEUS","lines":["Bear witness, heaven, I have my wish for ever."],"id":988,"m":"Heaven be my witness, I have what I wish for, for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"JULIA","lines":["And I mine."],"id":989,"m":"And I have mine."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Outlaws with Duke and Thurio.","id":990},{"t":"sp","who":"OUTLAWS","lines":["A prize, a prize, a prize!"],"id":991,"m":"A prize, a prize, a prize!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Forbear, forbear, I say! It is my lord the Duke.","Your Grace is welcome to a man disgraced,","Banished Valentine."],"id":992,"m":"Stop, stop, I say! It's my lord the Duke. Your grace is welcome to a disgraced man, the banished Valentine."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Sir Valentine!"],"id":993,"m":"Sir Valentine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Yonder is Silvia, and Silvia's mine."],"id":994,"m":"There's Silvia, and Silvia is mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Thurio, give back, or else embrace thy death;","Come not within the measure of my wrath.","Do not name Silvia thine; if once again,","Verona shall not hold thee. Here she stands;","Take but possession of her with a touch—","I dare thee but to breathe upon my love."],"id":995,"m":"Thurio, stand back, or else meet your death. Don't come within reach of my anger. Don't call Silvia yours; if you do it once more, Verona won't be safe for you. Here she stands. Just try to take possession of her with one touch. I dare you even to breathe on my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"THURIO","lines":["Sir Valentine, I care not for her, I.","I hold him but a fool that will endanger","His body for a girl that loves him not.","I claim her not, and therefore she is thine."],"id":996,"m":"Sir Valentine, I don't care about her, not I. I think anyone's a fool who'd risk his body for a girl who doesn't love him. I give up my claim, so she's yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["The more degenerate and base art thou","To make such means for her as thou hast done,","And leave her on such slight conditions.—","Now, by the honour of my ancestry,","I do applaud thy spirit, Valentine,","And think thee worthy of an empress' love.","Know then, I here forget all former griefs,","Cancel all grudge, repeal thee home again,","Plead a new state in thy unrivalled merit,","To which I thus subscribe: Sir Valentine,","Thou art a gentleman, and well derived;","Take thou thy Silvia, for thou hast deserved her."],"id":997,"m":"All the more low and contemptible you are, to go to such lengths to win her and then give her up on such easy terms. Now, by the honour of my ancestors, I applaud your spirit, Valentine, and think you worthy of an empress's love. Know, then, that I here forget all my old grievances, cancel every grudge and call you home again. On the strength of your unmatched merit, I declare a new standing for you, and I sign my name to it like this: Sir Valentine, you are a gentleman, and of good birth. Take your Silvia, because you have earned her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I thank your Grace; the gift hath made me happy.","I now beseech you, for your daughter's sake,","To grant one boon that I shall ask of you."],"id":998,"m":"Thank you, your grace; the gift has made me happy. Now I beg you, for your daughter's sake, to grant one favour I'll ask of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I grant it for thine own, whate'er it be."],"id":999,"m":"I grant it for your own sake, whatever it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["These banished men, that I have kept withal,","Are men endued with worthy qualities.","Forgive them what they have committed here,","And let them be recalled from their exile.","They are reformed, civil, full of good,","And fit for great employment, worthy lord."],"id":1000,"m":"These banished men I've lived with are men with good qualities. Forgive them for what they've done here, and let them be called back from exile. They've reformed; they're civilised, full of goodness and fit for important work, worthy lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Thou hast prevailed; I pardon them and thee.","Dispose of them as thou know'st their deserts.","Come, let us go; we will include all jars","With triumphs, mirth, and rare solemnity."],"id":1001,"m":"You've won; I pardon them and you. Use them as you know they deserve. Come, let's go. We'll end all quarrels with celebrations, merriment and splendid ceremony."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["And as we walk along, I dare be bold","With our discourse to make your Grace to smile.","What think you of this page, my lord?"],"id":1002,"m":"And as we walk along, I'll be bold enough to make your grace smile with our story. What do you think of this page, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["I think the boy hath grace in him; he blushes."],"id":1003,"m":"I think the boy has grace in him; he's blushing."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["I warrant you, my lord, more grace than boy."],"id":1004,"m":"I promise you, my lord, he has more grace than boy in him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["What mean you by that saying?"],"id":1005,"m":"What do you mean by that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["Please you, I'll tell you as we pass along,","That you will wonder what hath fortuned.","Come, Proteus, 'tis your penance but to hear","The story of your loves discovered.","That done, our day of marriage shall be yours,","One feast, one house, one mutual happiness."],"id":1006,"m":"If you please, I'll tell you as we go along, and you'll be amazed at what has happened. Come, Proteus, your only punishment is to hear the story of your loves told in full. After that, our wedding day will be yours too: one feast, one house, one shared happiness."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1007}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Valentine","group":"The two gentlemen","desc":"One of the two gentlemen of Verona. He mocks love in the first scene, then sails to Milan and falls for the Duke's daughter Silvia. They plan to elope with a rope ladder, but his best friend Proteus betrays the plan and the Duke banishes him. In the forest outlaws make him their captain. He saves Silvia from Proteus, forgives his friend at once and wins Silvia from the Duke."},{"name":"Proteus","group":"The two gentlemen","desc":"Valentine's closest friend, named after the sea god who could change his shape. He loves Julia in Verona, but in Milan he falls for Silvia at first sight. He betrays Valentine to the Duke, lies to Thurio, sends Julia's ring to Silvia and finally tries to force Silvia in the forest. Caught by Valentine, he repents in a few lines and returns to Julia when she reveals herself."},{"name":"Julia","group":"Verona","desc":"A lady of Verona who loves Proteus. She tears up his first letter, then pieces it together. When he leaves she gives him a ring, and later follows him to Milan dressed as a page called Sebastian. She hears him serenade Silvia, is hired to carry his love tokens to her rival, and reveals herself in the forest with the two rings."},{"name":"Lucetta","group":"Verona","desc":"Julia's waiting-woman. She recommends Proteus over Julia's other suitors, smuggles in his letter, teases her mistress and helps her plan the boy's costume for the journey."},{"name":"Antonio","group":"Verona","desc":"Proteus's father. Persuaded that young men should see the world, he orders Proteus to join Valentine at court the very next day."},{"name":"Pantino","group":"Verona","desc":"Antonio's servant, who advises him to send Proteus to court and hurries Proteus and Lance off to the ship."},{"name":"Silvia","group":"Milan","desc":"The Duke of Milan's daughter. Witty and clear-sighted, she gets Valentine to write a love letter to himself, loves him faithfully, refuses Thurio and scorns Proteus as a perjured friend. She escapes to find Valentine, is captured by outlaws and then seized by Proteus, and says nothing after Valentine offers her to him."},{"name":"The Duke of Milan","group":"Milan","desc":"Silvia's father, who wants her to marry rich Thurio. Warned by Proteus, he tricks Valentine into revealing the rope ladder under his cloak and banishes him. Captured by the outlaws at the end, he admires Valentine's spirit and gives him Silvia."},{"name":"Thurio","group":"Milan","desc":"A rich, foolish rival for Silvia, approved by her father. Valentine mocks his wit; Proteus pretends to help him and uses his serenade for his own wooing. In the forest he gives Silvia up rather than risk a fight."},{"name":"Sir Eglamour","group":"Milan","desc":"A gentleman who vowed chastity on his dead love's grave. Silvia trusts him to escort her to Valentine, but when outlaws attack in the forest he runs away."},{"name":"The Host","group":"Milan","desc":"The innkeeper where Julia stays in Milan. He takes her, as Sebastian, to hear the serenade and falls asleep during it."},{"name":"Speed","group":"Servants","desc":"Valentine's quick-witted page. He bandies words with Proteus, sees through Silvia's letter trick at once and reads out Lance's list of his sweetheart's qualities."},{"name":"Lance","group":"Servants","desc":"Proteus's clownish servant and the owner of the dog Crab. His two monologues about Crab, the unfeeling dog at his family's farewell and the dog who disgraces him at the Duke's dinner, show a loyalty that his master lacks."},{"name":"Crab","group":"Servants","desc":"Lance's dog, who never says a word. He sheds no tears when Lance leaves home, steals a capon leg at Silvia's dinner and is offered to her in place of Proteus's lap dog."},{"name":"The Outlaws","group":"The forest","desc":"Banished gentlemen living in the forest between Milan and Mantua. They threaten Valentine, admire his looks and languages and make him their captain. They capture Silvia and later the Duke, and are pardoned at the end."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"In Verona, Valentine says goodbye to his friend Proteus before sailing to Milan to see the world. He mocks Proteus for staying at home in love; Proteus says Julia has transformed him. Valentine's servant Speed arrives late, trades jokes about sheep with Proteus and reports that Julia gave him nothing for delivering Proteus's letter.","key":["The two friends are set up as opposites: Valentine seeks honour abroad, Proteus stays for love.","Valentine scorns love as foolishness, which the play will soon turn against him.","Speed's wordplay begins the servants' running commentary on their masters."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Valentine going?","o":["Padua","Milan","Mantua","Venice"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Proteus stay in Verona while Valentine travels?","o":["His love for Julia keeps him at home","His father has forbidden him to leave Verona","He is too ill to make the long journey","He has no money to pay for the voyage"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Valentine mock Proteus for being in love?","o":["He is secretly in love with Julia himself and jealous of his friend","He thinks Julia is not good enough for Proteus","He thinks love wastes youth that should be spent seeing the world","He wants Proteus to marry his own sister instead"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Proteus's claim that Julia has 'metamorphosed' him foreshadow?","o":["That Julia will turn him into a different animal","That he will become a great traveller just like Valentine","That he will stay faithful to Julia for ever","His name means changeable, and he will soon change again"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"How does the opening contrast set up the two friends?","o":["Both of them are equally and deeply in love from the very start of the play","Valentine scorns love and Proteus lives for it, roles that will soon reverse","Both despise love and want only to travel","Valentine loves Julia, while Proteus loves no one"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"view"}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Julia asks her maid Lucetta which of her suitors she should love. Lucetta picks Proteus and hands over his letter. Julia pretends to be angry and sends her away, then calls her back, tears up the letter in front of her, and, once alone, gathers the pieces and kisses the words 'Proteus' and 'Julia'.","key":["Julia shows the gap between what a woman is expected to say and what she feels.","The torn letter is the first of many letters and tokens in the play.","Lucetta's musical puns let her tease her mistress without open disrespect."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does Lucetta think is the best of Julia's suitors?","o":["Sir Eglamour","Mercatio","Proteus","Thurio"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Julia tear up Proteus's letter in front of Lucetta?","o":["She wants to hide her feelings and keep up a show of modesty","She truly hates Proteus and wants nothing more to do with him or his family","She thinks the letter is from another suitor","She is angry that Proteus wrote so little"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Julia kissing the torn pieces once alone reveal?","o":["Her scorn was an act, and she loves him deeply","She regrets wasting good paper on a quarrel","She is trying to read the letter in secret","She plans to send the pieces back to Proteus"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Julia pretend to be angry with Lucetta?","o":["She truly does not love Proteus at all, not one bit","Her father has forbidden her any letters from men","Lucetta has read the letter before her in secret","Maids say no to what they would have taken"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How does this scene show Julia as a comic heroine?","o":["She is calm, serious and never once changes her mind about anything","She is cruel to her maid and cares only for money","She is shy and says almost nothing at all","She is lively, contradictory and self-mocking in love"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"view"}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"Pantino tells Antonio that his brother thinks Proteus should not waste his youth at home, and suggests sending him to join Valentine at the Emperor's court. Proteus enters reading a letter from Julia; to hide it he says it is from Valentine, wishing he were with him. Antonio orders him to leave the next day.","key":["Proteus's first lie, small and panicky, leads straight to the separation he fears.","Fathers in this play decide their children's futures without asking.","His April-day image shows how changeable he is."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Proteus's lie about the letter lead to?","o":["It wins his father's permission to marry Julia at once","It makes Valentine return home from Milan","It backfires, and he is sent away from Julia to Milan","It persuades Antonio to let him stay in Verona"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does Proteus's quick lie to his father suggest about him?","o":["He finds deceit easy, a hint of what is to come","He is always honest except when he is very afraid","He loves his father more than he loves Julia","He thinks the letter really is from Valentine"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"When must Proteus leave?","o":["In a month","Next year","Tomorrow","After his wedding"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Antonio want to send Proteus away?","o":["To find a rich wife at the Duke's court","To fight for the Emperor in his wars","To be tried and tutored in the world","To keep him away from Julia's family"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Proteus say about his lie?","o":["He is glad that his father believed him so easily","He will tell his father the truth in the morning","He is sorry he did not tell the truth to Valentine","He shunned the fire and drenched himself in the sea"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"In Milan, Speed finds Silvia's glove and teases Valentine about the signs of his love. Silvia had asked Valentine to write a love letter for her to someone she loves. When he hands it over she makes him take it back for himself. Valentine does not understand; Speed explains that she has made him write a love letter to himself.","key":["The man who mocked love in Act 1 now has every symptom Speed lists.","Silvia is witty and in control, wooing by a trick.","The servant understands more than the master."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Speed pick up at the start?","o":["A letter","A glove","A ring","A picture"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Silvia ask Valentine to write a love letter for her?","o":["She cannot write and needs his help with the words","It is a trick to make him write his own love letter","She wants him to send it to Thurio, the suitor she prefers","She is testing whether he has good handwriting"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Speed see the trick when Valentine does not?","o":["Speed has read the letter before Valentine","Valentine is too dazed by love to see what is plain","Silvia told Speed about the trick in private","Speed is cleverer and wiser than anyone else in the city of Milan"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Speed's list of love's signs suggest about Valentine?","o":["He has fallen ill with a fever and needs a doctor","He is pretending to love Silvia to win the Duke's favour","The scorner of love has become a typical lover","He has become sad because he misses Verona"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How does the servant Speed function in this scene?","o":["He is a villain who secretly plans to betray Valentine to the Duke","He is a rival who also loves Silvia","He is a messenger with no real part to play","His wit exposes his master's folly to the audience"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"view"}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Proteus and Julia part in Verona. They exchange rings and seal their promise with a kiss. Proteus swears that if an hour ever passes without his sighing for her, some misfortune may punish him. Julia leaves without a word, and Pantino hurries Proteus to the ship.","key":["The exchange of rings is a betrothal: Proteus's later betrayal breaks a solemn vow.","His oath of constancy is the one the rest of the play tests.","Julia's silence speaks for her feelings."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do Proteus and Julia exchange?","o":["Letters","Rings","Gloves","Portraits"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is ironic about Proteus's oath at the parting?","o":["He keeps it perfectly for the rest of the play, whatever happens","Julia breaks her oath first, not Proteus","He will break it almost as soon as he reaches Milan","The oath is made to Valentine, not to Julia"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"Why does Proteus swear that misfortune may punish any hour without sighing?","o":["He fears that Julia will forget him at once","He wants Julia to promise the same in return","He knows already that he will be unfaithful","To prove how faithful he will be while away"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Julia not speak at the parting?","o":["She is angry that Proteus is leaving her for Milan","Her father is watching them both from the window","Truth has better deeds than words to grace it","She has already said all that she wished to say"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How do the rings exchanged here matter later?","o":["They are stolen by the outlaws in the forest and never seen again","They are sold by Lance to buy a new dog","They are given to Thurio as a wedding present","They become the tokens that reveal Julia in the last scene"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Lance, Proteus's servant, complains that his dog Crab shed not one tear when the whole family wept at his departure. He acts out the farewell using his shoes, staff and hat as his family. Pantino hurries him to the ship, and Lance confuses the tide with the tied dog.","key":["Lance's comic farewell parodies the lovers' parting just before.","His loyal love for an unfeeling dog mirrors Julia's for Proteus.","Prose and props turn grief into slapstick."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Lance's dog called?","o":["Crab","Speed","Ape","Sour"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why does Lance use his shoes and staff to act out the farewell?","o":["To show his family's grief in a comic one-man show","He has no family and must invent one","He is practising a play to perform for the Duke of Milan","He wants to teach his dog Crab to act"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why is Lance upset with Crab?","o":["Crab bit his mother as they said goodbye","Crab ran away from home and nearly made them miss the ship","The dog shed no tear while the whole family wept","Crab ate the food packed for the journey"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How does Lance's farewell scene parody Proteus and Julia's parting?","o":["It shows that Lance's love for his dog is truer than Proteus's love","It reveals that Lance is secretly in love with Julia","It mocks tearful lovers' farewells with a dog that feels nothing","It has no link to the lovers' story at all"],"a":2,"b":5,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does Lance's comic confusion add to the play?","o":["Plain, foolish loyalty that contrasts with the gentlemen's fickle love","A serious warning to the audience about the dangers of travelling by sea","A clue that Proteus will soon be shipwrecked","A reason for Julia to follow Proteus to Milan"],"a":0,"b":5,"p":"view"}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"Valentine and Thurio trade insults in front of Silvia. The Duke announces that Proteus has arrived, and Valentine praises him as a perfect gentleman. Silvia welcomes Proteus as a second servant. Alone, Valentine tells Proteus that he and Silvia are betrothed and plan to elope with a rope ladder. Left alone, Proteus confesses that he has fallen in love with Silvia and forgotten Julia.","key":["Valentine's generous praise of his friend makes the coming betrayal more painful.","Proteus falls in love at first sight with his friend's betrothed.","Valentine's trust gives Proteus the secret he will use."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is Valentine's rival for Silvia?","o":["Eglamour","Thurio","Antonio","Speed"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How do Valentine and Silvia plan to elope?","o":["By boat","Through a secret door","With a rope ladder","Disguised as pilgrims"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Proteus's sudden love for Silvia reveal?","o":["His love is fickle and driven by what he sees","He has always loved Silvia since they were children","He is pretending in order to help Valentine","He has learned that Julia is dead"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How does Valentine's trust in Proteus create dramatic irony?","o":["We know Proteus now loves Silvia as Valentine shares his secret","Valentine already knows that Proteus loves Silvia and is testing him","Proteus has already told the Duke about the plan","Silvia warns Valentine not to trust Proteus"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"How does Valentine say love has punished him?","o":["With a fever that keeps him in his bed for days","With a letter that broke his heart in two pieces","With bitter fasts, groans and nightly tears","With a wound from a rival in a duel at the court"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"information"}]},"a2s5":{"sum":"Speed welcomes Lance to Milan. Lance answers his questions about Proteus and Julia with riddles about standing and understanding, and tells him to ask the dog whether they will marry. They go off to the alehouse.","key":["The servants' banter keeps the comedy light between the betrayal scenes.","Lance's 'understand / stand under' joke is typical of his literal-minded wit.","Speed reports Valentine's love, which Lance mocks."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do Speed and Lance go?","o":["The palace","The alehouse","The harbour","The church"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Lance mean by saying his staff understands him?","o":["He puns on 'stand under', mocking Speed's question","His staff is magic and can speak to him when no one else is near","He has trained his staff like a dog","He means that Proteus will understand him"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why does Lance tell Speed to ask the dog about the marriage?","o":["The dog has heard Proteus and Julia talk of it many times in Verona","Crab can nod his head to answer questions","He wants Speed to stop asking him questions","He mocks the question, as if the answer were obvious or silly"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why might Lance call his master 'a notable lubber'?","o":["The servant sees his master's foolishness more clearly than others do","Proteus has been very rude to Lance about Crab during the whole long voyage to Milan","Lance is jealous that Proteus is rich","Proteus has fallen into the sea on the voyage"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does this comic scene contribute while the plot waits?","o":["It mocks the lovers through the servants' wordplay","It reveals Proteus's plan to betray Valentine","It explains how Julia will travel to Milan","It shows the Duke's plan to marry Silvia to Thurio"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"purpose"}]},"a2s6":{"sum":"In a soliloquy, Proteus argues himself into betraying both Julia and Valentine. He decides he must be true to himself, and plans to tell the Duke about the elopement so that Valentine is banished, then to outwit Thurio.","key":["Proteus uses clever logic to justify a betrayal: love excuses everything.","He admits he will break three oaths at once.","The audience now knows the plot that Valentine does not."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Proteus say he must break three oaths?","o":["He has made three different promises to the Duke that he now cannot keep","He owes money to three men in Verona","Loving Silvia means betraying Julia, Valentine and his own word","He swore three times never to come to Milan"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Who will Proteus tell about the elopement?","o":["Thurio, his rival","The Duke","Silvia, his love","Speed, Valentine's man"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How convincing is Proteus's argument that he must love himself more?","o":["Fully convincing, since self-love is the highest virtue of all for a man","Convincing, because Valentine has wronged him first","Unconvincing, because he does not truly love Silvia","It is self-serving logic that dresses up betrayal as reason"],"a":3,"b":5,"p":"view"},{"q":"How does Proteus justify his betrayal?","o":["He believes that Valentine has already betrayed him","He cannot leave to love, and must love himself more","He thinks that Julia has married another man at home","He is quite sure that Silvia already loves him back"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"question"},{"q":"What does Proteus calling Silvia a sun and Julia a star suggest?","o":["Julia was always the brighter and better lady","He measures love by dazzle, not faithfulness","He sees both women as equally lovely","He plans to marry both of them in turn"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"concept"}]},"a2s7":{"sum":"In Verona, Julia asks Lucetta to help her travel to Proteus. To avoid unwanted attention from men, she will dress as a page. They joke about breeches and codpieces. Lucetta doubts that Proteus will be pleased, but Julia insists that his oaths and tears prove he is faithful.","key":["Julia's journey is an act of love and courage, the opposite of Proteus's betrayal.","Her praise of Proteus's truth is painful dramatic irony.","The cross-dressing comedy anticipates later Shakespearean heroines."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Julia dress as a man?","o":["To fight a duel with Proteus","To avoid men's advances","To spy on her father","For a play"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Lucetta say Julia must do first?","o":["Cut her hair","Learn to fence","Write to Proteus","Sell her lands"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Julia's comparison of her love to a stream suggest?","o":["It will dry up quickly once summer comes","It is cold, deep and dangerous to anyone who dares to enter it","It runs away from Proteus towards another man","It cannot be stopped and will find its way to Proteus"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"concept"},{"q":"How does Julia say she will travel?","o":["In her own fine gowns and jewels","With an armed escort of servants","Hidden in a cart under straw","Like a well-reputed page"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"How well founded is Julia's faith that Proteus will be true?","o":["Fully, since Proteus writes to her every day","Fully, since Proteus has sent a letter asking her to come at once","Not at all: we already know he has turned to Silvia","Partly, since he has only met Silvia once"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Proteus tells the Duke of Valentine's planned elopement. The Duke tricks Valentine by pretending to need advice on wooing a lady of Verona, then borrows his cloak and finds the rope ladder and a love letter to Silvia. He banishes Valentine. Proteus pretends sympathy and sees him to the city gate. Lance reveals that he loves a milkmaid and has Speed read out a list of her qualities, then delays Speed so he will be beaten.","key":["Proteus's betrayal works because the Duke trusts him as an honest friend.","The Duke's cloak trick turns Valentine's own advice against him.","Lance's list of his milkmaid's faults and virtues mocks idealised love."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Duke's trick show about him?","o":["He is shrewd and uses Valentine's own advice against him","He is foolish and easily deceived by young men from Verona","He plans to marry Silvia off to Valentine","He has no idea of the planned elopement"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What punishment does Valentine receive?","o":["Prison for a year","Death on the scaffold","Banishment","A heavy fine"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Lance's list of his milkmaid's qualities comment on the lovers?","o":["His practical love contrasts with the gentlemen's high-flown passion","He loves her only for her great beauty, just as Proteus loves Silvia now","He plans to elope with her, just as Valentine planned","He is mocking Silvia by comparing her to a milkmaid"],"a":0,"b":5,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does Valentine say about life away from Silvia?","o":["He will forget her soon and find another lady in Verona","He will live in the forest until she comes to find him","He will fight the Duke for her and win her by the sword","Silvia is himself, and banished from her is self from self"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How does the Duke trick Valentine?","o":["He asks for help wooing a lady himself","He reads one of Valentine's letters aloud","He sends a spy to watch Valentine's room","He invites him to a feast and searches him"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"information"}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"The Duke and Thurio worry that Silvia still loves Valentine. Proteus agrees, reluctantly at first, to slander Valentine to her, and advises Thurio to woo her with sad sonnets and music under her window at night. The Duke gives Proteus free access to Silvia.","key":["Proteus pretends to help Thurio while using the access to woo Silvia himself.","He lists the lies he will tell: falsehood, cowardice and low birth.","The plan for a serenade leads to the most famous moment in the play."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Proteus's willingness to slander Valentine show?","o":["He is reluctant and only obeys the Duke's command","He will betray his friend completely to win Silvia","He truly believes Valentine is a coward","He wants to protect Silvia from a bad marriage"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does he advise Thurio to do at night?","o":["Climb her tower","Serenade her","Write to her father","Fight Valentine"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does the Duke give Proteus free access to Silvia?","o":["He wants Proteus to marry Silvia himself","He is too busy to watch over his daughter","Silvia has asked to see Proteus every day","He thinks Proteus will plead for Thurio"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does the Duke trust Proteus?","o":["He is Thurio's oldest and closest friend","He told the Duke of the elopement","He has served in the Emperor's court","He has paid the Duke a great sum of money"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What is ironic about Proteus advising Thurio how to woo Silvia?","o":["He has never been in love and knows nothing of wooing","Thurio already has Silvia's love and needs no help","He gives Thurio honest advice and wants him to win","He uses Thurio's wooing as cover for his own"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"In a forest, outlaws stop Valentine and Speed. Valentine claims he was banished for killing a man in a fair fight. Impressed by his looks and his languages, the outlaws, who are themselves banished gentlemen, offer to make him their captain or kill him. He agrees, on condition they never harm women or poor travellers.","key":["The forest becomes a world outside the court's law, as in later comedies.","Valentine's lie about killing a man makes him one of the outlaws.","His condition about women will matter in Act 5."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do the outlaws want Valentine as their leader?","o":["He is rich and can pay them well","His looks and his languages","He is the Duke's son","He killed their captain"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Valentine claim he did?","o":["Stole a horse from the Duke's own stable","Killed a man in a fight","Ran off with a lady from Verona","Insulted the Duke in front of his court"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Valentine's condition about women and the poor show?","o":["He is too weak to make the outlaws obey him","He wants to rob only rich men to grow rich himself","He keeps his honour even as an outlaw","He fears the Duke will find him in the forest"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why were the outlaws themselves banished?","o":["For refusing to pay their taxes to the Duke of Milan","For speaking against the Emperor in public places","For losing a battle against the army of Mantua","For crimes like stealing an heiress or stabbing a man"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How believable are the outlaws as dangerous robbers?","o":["Not very: they are gentlemen who want a polite captain","Very: they kill every single traveller who enters the forest","Very: they have robbed the Duke of all his treasure","Not at all: they are only actors in a play"],"a":0,"b":5,"p":"view"}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Proteus admits Silvia rejects him. Thurio arrives with musicians, and the song 'Who is Silvia?' is sung under her window. Julia, newly arrived in boy's clothes, watches with the Host and is heartbroken. When Thurio leaves, Proteus woos Silvia, who calls him perjured and false. He claims Julia and Valentine are dead and asks for her picture, which she agrees to send.","key":["Julia sees Proteus's betrayal with her own eyes: her musical puns show her pain.","Silvia's scorn shows she is constant where Proteus is not.","Proteus lies that Julia is dead, while Julia listens."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the song sung under Silvia's window?","o":["Who is Silvia?","Under the greenwood tree","O mistress mine","Sigh no more"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Proteus's claim that Julia is dead reveal?","o":["He truly believes Julia has died in Verona","He wants Silvia to pity him and his loss","He will lie about anything to win Silvia","He has heard a false report from Lance"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What is the effect of Julia, in disguise, watching Proteus woo Silvia?","o":["Dramatic irony: we share her pain as he betrays her before her eyes","It is purely comic, since Julia no longer cares about Proteus at all","It shows that Julia has already given up Proteus","It makes Silvia fall in love with the disguised Julia"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does the Host say about Julia's sadness?","o":["She seems melancholy","She is tired from the road","She looks ill and feverish","She is hungry for her supper"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Silvia agree to send her picture?","o":["She wants to make Valentine jealous","She hopes Proteus will leave her alone","She thinks Julia would want her to","She is very loath to be your idol, sir"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Silvia asks Sir Eglamour, a gentleman who vowed chastity when his love died, to help her escape to Valentine in Mantua and avoid marriage to Thurio. He agrees to meet her that evening at Friar Patrick's cell.","key":["Silvia takes action to stay faithful, like Julia.","Eglamour seems an ideal knight, which makes his later flight ironic.","Both heroines leave home to follow the men they love."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Silvia's escape plan show her character?","o":["She is brave and faithful, acting on her own to reach Valentine","She is helpless and simply waits for Valentine to come and rescue her","She is fickle and plans to run off with Eglamour","She is obedient and agrees to marry Thurio"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does she trust Eglamour?","o":["He is her cousin","He vowed chastity on his love's grave","He is a priest","He hates her father"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Where will they meet?","o":["The North Gate","Friar Patrick's cell","Saint Gregory's well","The harbour"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why is Eglamour's vow of chastity important to Silvia's plan?","o":["He has promised to marry her once they reach Mantua","He is a priest who can marry her to Valentine","He can protect her without any risk to her honour","He knows the way to Mantua better than anyone"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Silvia want to leave Milan?","o":["To avoid marriage to Thurio and find Valentine","To visit her mother who lives in Mantua","To escape from Proteus's letters and his gifts","To see the Emperor and ask him for help"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Lance tells how Crab disgraced him at the Duke's dinner and how he took a whipping for him. He offered Silvia Crab instead of Proteus's lap dog, which was stolen. Proteus hires Julia, as Sebastian, and sends her to Silvia with the ring Julia once gave him. Silvia refuses the letter and the ring, pities Julia, and gives Sebastian her picture for Proteus. Alone, Julia compares herself with the portrait.","key":["Lance suffers for his dog as Julia suffers for Proteus: two kinds of devoted love.","Julia must woo her rival for the man she loves.","Silvia's refusal of the ring shows her loyalty to a woman she has never met."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Lance take a whipping for Crab?","o":["He was ordered to by Proteus as a punishment for losing the dog","His loyalty to the dog outweighs his own comfort","He thought the whipping was a kind of game","He wanted to impress Silvia with his courage"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What is painful about Proteus sending Julia with her own ring to Silvia?","o":["He knows it is Julia in disguise and wants to hurt her deliberately","He makes her the messenger of his betrayal, using her own love-token","The ring is cheap and worthless, and Silvia will only laugh at the gift","Julia has asked to be sent with the ring"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"How does Silvia's treatment of 'Sebastian' affect our view of her?","o":["Her pity for Julia and scorn for Proteus show her true faith","She is cruel and laughs openly at the poor page's sadness and tears","She is tempted by Proteus's ring and keeps it","She is indifferent to Julia and Proteus alike"],"a":0,"b":5,"p":"view"},{"q":"What did Crab steal at the dinner?","o":["A capon's leg","A whole cheese","Some bread","A fish"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Julia praise Silvia's picture?","o":["It shows her own colouring is as good","It is the finest painting she has seen","It makes her love Proteus all the more","It shows Silvia as plain and dull"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"At evening, Eglamour meets Silvia at the abbey, and they slip out by the back gate towards the forest, which is less than three leagues away.","key":["A short scene that sets the escape in motion.","Silvia fears spies, which raises the tension.","The forest now draws every main character towards it."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do Silvia and Eglamour meet?","o":["At the abbey","At the palace","At the inn","At the city gate"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why do Silvia and Eglamour leave by the back gate?","o":["To avoid paying the toll at the city gate","Because the main gate is closed for repairs","To meet the outlaws who will guide them","To slip out unseen by the Duke's men"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why might this scene be so brief?","o":["It was always meant to be cut from every performance of the play","It gives the actors time to change their costumes","It hurries the lovers towards the forest where all will meet","It shows that Silvia has changed her mind"],"a":2,"b":5,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Eglamour say they will be safe?","o":["The outlaws in the forest will guard them","The forest is not three leagues off","The Duke's own soldiers are at the gate","Friar Lawrence will hide them in his cell"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How safe is Silvia's trust in Eglamour, judging by what follows?","o":["Fully justified, since he fights off all the outlaws","Fully justified, since he brings her to Valentine","Misplaced, since he hands her over to the Duke","Misplaced: he runs away when the outlaws attack"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Thurio asks Proteus how Silvia likes him, and Julia mocks him in asides. The Duke enters: Silvia has fled with Eglamour towards Mantua. He orders them all to follow. Thurio goes for revenge on Eglamour, Proteus for love of Silvia, and Julia to stop that love.","key":["Julia's asides let the audience laugh at Thurio and feel for her.","The chase gathers every character into the forest.","Each follower's reason shows his or her values."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who has Silvia fled with?","o":["Valentine","Eglamour","Thurio","Speed"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does the Duke order everyone to follow Silvia?","o":["He fears she has run to Valentine and wants her back","He wants to give her his blessing to marry","He has heard the outlaws have captured her","He is worried that Eglamour will harm her in the dark forest"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Julia follow?","o":["To find Valentine and bring him home to Milan","To stop Proteus's love for Silvia","To go home to her father in Verona","To help the Duke find his daughter"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What do the different reasons for following Silvia show about each pursuer?","o":["All of them follow for the very same reason, out of loyal duty to the Duke of Milan","Thurio follows for love, Proteus only for revenge","None of them really cares where Silvia has gone","Each follows his own desire: revenge, love, or a lover to win back"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"view"},{"q":"What do Julia's mocking asides about Thurio add?","o":["They prove that she has fallen in love with Thurio","They warn the Duke that Thurio is a coward","They show her wit survives even her heartbreak","They make Proteus realise who she really is"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"The outlaws capture Silvia. Eglamour has run away. One outlaw promises to take her to their captain, who will treat her honourably.","key":["Eglamour fails Silvia when she most needs him.","Valentine's rule about women is about to be tested.","Silvia endures this for Valentine's sake."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happened to Eglamour?","o":["He was killed","He ran away","He was captured","He fought bravely"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why do the outlaws promise Silvia their captain will treat her honourably?","o":["Valentine has forbidden them to harm women","They want a large ransom from the Duke","They are afraid of Silvia's father","They have mistaken her for a nun"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Eglamour's flight show about him?","o":["He is the bravest man in the forest","His noble promises fail at the first real danger","He has gone off to fetch Valentine so that he can rescue her","He planned to betray Silvia all along"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What is ironic about Silvia being taken to the outlaws' captain?","o":["The captain is Thurio, the very suitor she has run away from","Their captain is Valentine, the man she is looking for","The captain is her own father in disguise","The captain is Eglamour, who planned the attack"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What do the outlaws say about Eglamour?","o":["He fought them and died","He was too quick for them","He has paid them gold","He went to fetch help"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Valentine hides as Proteus arrives, having rescued Silvia from the outlaws. She rejects him and he tries to force her. Valentine stops him and condemns him. Proteus repents in a few lines, Valentine forgives him and offers him all his claim to Silvia. Julia faints; the rings reveal her identity, and Proteus returns to her. The outlaws bring in the Duke and Thurio. Thurio gives Silvia up, the Duke gives her to Valentine, the outlaws are pardoned and a double wedding is planned.","key":["The sudden forgiveness and Valentine's offer of Silvia are the most debated moments in the play.","Silvia says nothing after Proteus's attack, a silence actors and critics read in many ways.","The ring exchange undoes the betrayal of Act 4 and restores the original couples."],"quiz":[{"q":"How do many modern audiences respond to Valentine offering Silvia to Proteus?","o":["With delight, since it is a fair, noble and generous reward for repentance","With unease: friendship is valued above Silvia's own feelings","With relief, since Silvia wanted to marry Proteus","With no surprise, since Silvia agreed to it beforehand"],"a":1,"b":5,"p":"view"},{"q":"What does Valentine offer Proteus after forgiving him?","o":["His sword and his honour","His claim to Silvia","His lands","His servant"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Julia reveal who she is?","o":["She takes off her hat and lets down her hair","Lance recognises her and tells everyone","Silvia guesses and names her aloud","She shows the ring Proteus gave her at parting"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Valentine say about a private wound?","o":["It is deepest when a friend gives it","It heals quickly with the help of time","It hurts less than one in the battle","It should be kept from the eyes of others"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How convincing is Proteus's sudden repentance?","o":["Fully convincing, since he weeps and pleads for forgiveness at length","Convincing, since Julia has forgiven him long before","Too quick to be fully believed, which makes the ending uneasy","Unconvincing, since he refuses to repent at all"],"a":2,"b":5,"p":"view"}]}},"notes":{"2":"The first words set up the play's debate: honour and travel against love and home.","6":"Leander swam the Hellespont each night to his lover Hero, and drowned. The story returns when Valentine plans to climb Silvia's tower.","24":"Proteus's name comes from a Greek sea god who changed his shape at will. He says Julia has 'metamorphosed' him, and he will change again.","42":"A 'laced mutton' was slang for a prostitute. Speed's joke at Julia's expense is crude, and Proteus lets it pass.","69":"A proverb: he who is born to be hanged will never be drowned. Proteus jokes that Speed will die on the gallows.","111":"Julia knows the rules of modest courtship: say 'no' and hope the man hears 'yes'.","127":"'Light o' Love' was a popular tune. Julia puns on light (cheerful, and wanton) and heavy (sad, and weighty).","138":"Lucetta's musical terms (sharp, flat, descant, mean, base) turn the quarrel into a song. 'Bid the base' means to challenge, as in a game of prisoner's base.","144":"Julia treats the torn scraps of paper as if they were the lovers themselves, a comic picture of real feeling.","163":"The play calls the ruler in Milan both Emperor and Duke. Editors think Shakespeare changed his mind while writing.","182":"Proteus's April day, all sun then cloud, is an image of how quickly his own feelings change.","203":"Speed's list of a lover's symptoms (folded arms, sighing, fasting, sleeplessness) is a catalogue of the stock lovesick young man.","231":"Going ungartered, with stockings sagging, was a well-known sign of a lover too distracted to dress properly.","267":"Speed sees at once what Valentine cannot: Silvia has made her lover write a love letter to himself.","285":"'I speak in print' means precisely, as if from a printed book.","308":"Lance's monologue is written in prose and full of props. A real dog played Crab, and the scene usually stops the show.","313":"Lance confuses 'tide' with 'tied', the dog on its lead.","350":"Chameleons were thought to live on air. Valentine calls Thurio one because he changes colour with anger.","370":"Valentine's generous praise of Proteus is dramatic irony: the audience will soon see how little it fits.","376":"In the language of courtly love, a lady's 'servant' was her devoted admirer. Silvia now has two servants, and soon three.","420":"Valentine's boasting is the extravagant praise expected of a courtly lover, and Proteus mocks it as 'braggartism'.","431":"Proteus admits he has only seen Silvia's 'picture', her outward appearance. The play later gives him her real picture.","471":"Proteus uses neat logic (lose them, find myself) to argue that betraying his friend is being true to himself.","481":"Julia's image of the stream, raging when blocked and gentle when free, is one of the most admired passages in the play.","487":"A farthingale was a hooped petticoat. Julia's joke: asking about breeches is as silly as asking a lord about his skirt.","488":"A codpiece was a pouch on the front of men's breeches, often padded. Lucetta's bawdy joke points to the comedy of a boy actor playing a girl playing a boy.","497":"Julia's praise of Proteus's truth is painful dramatic irony: he has already betrayed her.","506":"Proteus pretends that duty forces him to betray his friend, and asks the Duke to hide where he heard it.","521":"The Duke's 'lady of Verona' is invented. He uses Valentine's love of giving advice to trap him.","524":"Valentine's advice to the Duke is a list of conventional wooing tips, delivered with cheerful confidence just before his fall.","544":"Phaethon begged to drive his father Apollo's sun chariot, lost control and nearly burned the world. The Duke calls Valentine a nobody aiming too high.","546":"Valentine's lament (to be banished from Silvia is to be banished from himself) matches Romeo's response to banishment in Romeo and Juliet.","568":"Valentine plays on his own name: 'no Valentine', no true lover, if Silvia has rejected him.","581":"Lance's list uses legal terms: 'Imprimis' (first) and 'Item' (next) were used in inventories and wills.","594":"A joke on 'reading': only a mother knows for certain who the father is, so Speed should have said 'grandmother's son'.","634":"Lance argues, like a schoolman, that the hair hiding the wit must be bigger than the wit.","653":"The Duke's image of a figure carved in ice predicts that Silvia's love will melt. It does not.","677":"Orpheus could charm animals, trees and stones with his music. Proteus's advice on serenading is about the power of art over feeling.","710":"Robin Hood's band included the fat Friar Tuck. The outlaws see themselves as gentlemen bandits in his tradition.","716":"The outlaws are gentlemen who have committed real crimes, including kidnap and murder. Valentine's pardon for them at the end is generous.","719":"'Make a virtue of necessity' was already a proverb; Chaucer uses it in The Knight's Tale.","748":"'Who is Silvia?' is one of Shakespeare's most famous songs, later set to music by Schubert. In the text Proteus sings it, though some productions give it to a musician.","752":"Julia's musical puns (false, strings, heart-strings, change) let her express grief in front of the Host without revealing herself.","780":"Silvia swears by the moon, the 'pale queen of night', a symbol of chastity, and calls Proteus perjured to his face.","789":"Proteus asks for Silvia's picture, a 'shadow', in place of the real woman: a sign that he loves an image, not a person.","809":"Eglamour vowed chastity on his dead lady's grave, so Silvia feels safe travelling with him.","819":"Lance's second monologue: he takes a whipping, sits in the stocks and stands in the pillory for his dog. His loyalty is absurd and moving.","833":"Proteus's praise of Sebastian's face and manner is ironic: he is looking at Julia without seeing her.","843":"Julia sums up her impossible position: to be a true servant to her master she must be a traitor to herself.","864":"Silvia refuses to wear Julia's ring: she is loyal to a woman she has never met.","875":"Julia invents a story about playing Ariadne, abandoned by Theseus, in Julia's own gown. She is describing her own grief.","879":"Julia compares herself feature by feature with Silvia's portrait and finds no reason for Proteus's change, except that Love is blind.","924":"The text names 'Friar Lawrence', though Silvia's meeting was set at Friar Patrick's cell: one of the play's small inconsistencies, and the same name as the friar in Romeo and Juliet.","945":"Valentine's opening lines in the forest praise the shady wilderness above the court, a theme Shakespeare developed in As You Like It.","961":"Proteus's 'force ye' is an attempted rape. Modern productions often play the scene as shocking rather than comic.","967":"'The private wound is deepest': Valentine says a friend's betrayal hurts more than any enemy's.","969":"Valentine's offer of 'All that was mine in Silvia' is the most argued-over line in the play. Some read it as the ideal of friendship placed above love; others as treating Silvia as property.","985":"Julia turns her disguise into an argument: women changing their clothes is a smaller fault than men changing their minds.","986":"Proteus's couplet makes inconstancy the root of every fault, in a line that judges himself.","995":"Valentine threatens Thurio with 'Verona shall not hold thee', though the scene is near Milan: another sign of the play's shifting geography.","1006":"The play ends with a double wedding planned: 'One feast, one house, one mutual happiness'."},"gloss":{"homely":"plain, dull","sluggardized":"made lazy","shapeless":"aimless","haply":"perhaps; by chance","hap":"luck","environ":"surround","beadsman":"someone paid to pray for another","Hellespont":"the strait Leander swam to reach Hero","over shoes":"deeply (as if wading)","give me not the boots":"don't mock me","boots thee not":"does you no good","circumstance":"argument; detailed reasoning","cavil":"find fault","canker":"grub that eats buds","ere it blow":"before it opens","blasting":"withering","verdure":"freshness","votary":"devoted follower","fond":"foolish","road":"harbour","shipped":"on board ship","Betideth":"happens","bechance":"happen to","metamorphosed":"transformed","'save you":"God save you","laced mutton":"slang for a loose woman","overcharged":"overstocked","pound":"enclosure for stray animals","pinfold":"pen for stray animals","noddy":"fool","Beshrew me":"curse me (a mild oath)","testerned":"tipped with a sixpence","wrack":"wreck","post":"messenger","parle":"talk","censure":"pass judgement","broker":"go-between","wanton":"lustful; playful","ruminate":"think it over","o'erlooked":"read through","chid":"scolded","testy":"irritable","remission":"forgiveness","stomach":"temper; appetite","Light o' Love":"a popular tune","burden":"refrain of a song; load","minion":"hussy","descant":"melody sung over a tune","mean":"middle part in music; means","coil":"fuss","throughly":"thoroughly","sovereign":"healing; supreme","sith":"since","month's mind":"strong desire","wink":"close my eyes","preferment":"advancement","impeachment":"discredit","importune":"urge","tilts":"jousts","expedition":"speed","break with him":"tell him","exhibition":"allowance of money","peremptory":"determined","sirrah":"form of address to an inferior","malcontent":"gloomy, discontented man","Hallowmas":"All Saints' Day (1 November), when beggars went begging","puling":"whiningly","urinal":"glass for examining urine","hard-favoured":"ugly","ungartered":"with stockings hanging loose","swinged":"beat","motion":"puppet show","clerkly":"skilfully, like a scholar","quaintly":"cleverly","jerkin":"close-fitting jacket","doublet":"man's close jacket","exchequer":"treasury","liveries":"servants' uniforms","beset":"besieged","enfranchised":"set free","pawn":"pledge","fealty":"loyalty","braggartism":"boasting","prodigious":"Lance's slip for prodigal","proportion":"Lance's slip for portion, a share","wood":"mad","shot":"bill at an inn","closed":"embraced","block":"blockhead","lubber":"clumsy lout","forsworn":"guilty of breaking an oath","Ethiope":"African (used of dark skin)","drift":"plan","charactered":"written","sedge":"reed","Elysium":"heaven of the ancient world","weeds":"clothes","farthingale":"hooped petticoat","codpiece":"pouch on the front of men's breeches","unstaid":"reckless","timeless":"untimely","pretence":"plan","shelving":"leaning outwards","nice":"fussy","coy":"aloof","Forwhy":"because","Phaëthon":"son of the sun god, who crashed his father's chariot","Merops":"Phaethon's mortal stepfather","overweening":"arrogant","So-ho":"a hunting cry","doom":"sentence","repeal":"recall from exile","Imprimis":"first (in a list)","jolt-head":"blockhead","Saint Nicholas":"patron saint of scholars","jade":"worn-out horse","curst":"bad-tempered","liberal":"free; loose","cate-log":"Lance's spelling of catalogue","lime":"birdlime, a sticky trap","dump":"sad tune","consort":"band of musicians","Orpheus":"legendary musician who charmed beasts and stones","leviathans":"sea monsters","habiliments":"clothes","the tongues":"foreign languages","awful":"law-abiding; full of awe","allycholly":"the Host's slip for melancholy","halidom":"holy relic (in an oath)","out of all nick":"beyond all reckoning","sepulchre":"bury","obdurate":"hard-hearted","remorseful":"compassionate","abhorred":"hated","postern":"back gate","trencher":"wooden plate","capon":"chicken","pissing-while":"a very short time","entertained":"taken into service","augury":"prophecy, judgement","Pentecost":"Whitsun, a spring festival","agood":"heartily","Ariadne":"princess abandoned by Theseus","tire":"headdress","periwig":"wig","wag":"lad","cry you mercy":"I beg your pardon","raiment":"clothing","endued":"gifted","jars":"quarrels","triumphs":"public celebrations","fortuned":"happened","deploring":"mournful","proclamation":"public order","Valentinus":"Valentine (Latin form)","Mercatio":"one of Julia's suitors"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,4,"Stop trying to talk me round, my dear Proteus. Young men who stay at home always have small-town minds. If love didn't tie your young life to the sweet looks of the girl you adore, I'd rather beg you to come with me"],[5,9,"and see the wonders of the world than watch you stay at home, dull and lazy, wasting your youth in aimless idleness. But since you are in love, keep loving, and may it go well for you, as I'd hope it would for me if I ever start."]],"18":[[0,5,"And writers also say that just as the earliest bud is eaten by the grub before it can open, so love turns a young, tender mind to foolishness. It withers in the bud, losing its freshness in its prime and all the good things it promised for the future."],[6,9,"But why am I wasting my time advising you, when you're devoted to foolish desire? Once more, goodbye. My father is waiting at the harbour to see me off on the ship."]],"111":[[0,6,"And yet I wish I'd read the letter. It would be embarrassing to call her back and beg her to do the very thing I scolded her for. What a fool she is! She knows I'm a girl, so why didn't she force me to read it? Girls say \"no\", out of modesty, to things they want the person offering to take as \"yes\"."],[7,16,"Honestly, how contrary this foolish love is! Like a bad-tempered baby, it scratches the nurse and then, all meek, kisses the stick it was smacked with. How rudely I drove Lucetta away, when I really wanted her to stay! How angrily I made myself frown, when inside I was so happy I wanted to smile! My punishment will be to call Lucetta back and ask forgiveness for my silliness. Hello there! Lucetta!"]],"144":[[0,8,"No, I wish I were angry with this same one! Oh, hateful hands, to tear up such loving words! Spiteful wasps, to feed on such sweet honey and kill the bees that make it with your stings! I'll kiss each scrap of paper to make up for it. Look, here it says \"kind Julia\". Unkind Julia! To punish you for being so ungrateful, I'll throw your name against the hard stones and trample on your scorn."],[9,18,"And here it says \"love-wounded Proteus\". Poor wounded name, my breast will be your bed until your wound is fully healed, and I'll treat it with a healing kiss. But \"Proteus\" was written two or three times. Be calm, good wind, don't blow away a single word until I've found every letter in the letter, except my own name. Let a whirlwind carry that off to a jagged, terrifying, overhanging rock and throw it from there into the raging sea."],[19,25,"Look, here's his name twice in one line: \"Poor forlorn Proteus, passionate Proteus, to the sweet Julia.\" I'll tear that part off. And yet I won't, since he joins it so prettily to his own sad names. I'll fold them one on top of the other like this. Now kiss, embrace, wrestle, do whatever you like."]],"161":[[0,6,"He wondered why your lordship lets him spend his youth at home, while other men of much lower standing send their sons out to make their way in the world: some to the wars, to try their luck there; some to discover islands far away; some to the universities to study."],[7,12,"He said your son Proteus was fit for any or all of these, and he asked me to urge you not to let him stay at home any longer. It would be a great disadvantage to him when he's older if he'd never travelled when young."]],"182":[[0,5,"So I've avoided the fire for fear of being burned, and jumped into the sea, where I'm drowning. I was afraid to show my father Julia's letter in case he objected to my love, and he has used my own excuse against me and done the most damage to my love."],[6,9,"Oh, how this springtime of love is like the changeable glory of an April day, which one moment shows all the beauty of the sun, and the next a cloud takes it all away."]],"203":[[0,5,"Well, by these special signs. First, you've learned, like Sir Proteus, to fold your arms like a gloomy malcontent; to enjoy a love song, like a robin redbreast; to walk alone, like someone with the plague; to sigh, like a schoolboy who's lost his alphabet book; to weep, like a young girl who's buried her grandmother; to fast, like someone on a diet; to stay up all night, like someone afraid of burglars; to whine,"],[6,10,"like a beggar on All Saints' Day. You used to crow like a cockerel when you laughed, and swagger like a lion when you walked. When you fasted, it was right after dinner; when you looked sad, it was because you were short of money. And now a mistress has transformed you so much that when I look at you, I can hardly believe you're my master."]],"308":[[0,9,"No, it'll be an hour before I've finished crying. All the Lance family have this same fault. I've had my share of the inheritance, like the prodigal son, and I'm going with Sir Proteus to the Emperor's court. I think my dog Crab is the hardest-hearted dog alive. My mother was weeping, my father wailing, my sister crying, our maid howling, our cat wringing her hands and our whole house in a terrible state, and yet this cruel-hearted mutt didn't shed one tear. He's a stone, a real pebble, and he's got no more pity in him than a dog. Even a Jew would have wept to see us part. Why, my grandmother, who's got no eyes, look, cried herself blind when I left."],[10,17,"No, I'll show you how it went. This shoe is my father. No, this left shoe is my father; no, no, this left shoe is my mother. No, that can't be right either. Yes, it is, it is: it's got the worse sole. This shoe with the hole in it is my mother, and this one is my father. Curse it, there it is. Now, sir, this stick is my sister, because, look, she's as white as a lily and as thin as a twig. This hat is Nan, our maid. I'm the dog. No, the dog is himself, and I'm the dog. Oh, the dog is me, and I'm myself. Yes, that's it, that's it."],[18,23,"Now I come to my father: \"Father, your blessing.\" Now the shoe should be too choked with tears to say a word. Now I should kiss my father. Well, he keeps on weeping. Now I come to my mother. Oh, if only she could talk now like a woman out of her mind! Well, I kiss her. Why, there it is: that's my mother's breath exactly. Now I come to my sister. Listen to her moaning. And all this time the dog doesn't shed a tear or say a word; but look how I'm settling the dust with my tears."]],"370":[[0,6,"I knew him like my own self, because we've talked and spent our time together since we were children. And though I've been a lazy truant, wasting the precious chance to use my time to make my older self perfect, Sir Proteus, for that's his name, has used his days well and to good advantage."],[7,12,"He's young in years but old in experience; his head isn't yet grey, but his judgement is mature. In short, since all the praise I'm giving him falls far short of what he's worth, he's perfect in body and in mind, with every good quality that can grace a gentleman."]],"408":[[0,7,"Yes, Proteus, but my life has changed now. I've been punished for despising Love. His proud, commanding mind has made me suffer bitter fasting, groans of repentance, tears at night and heartsick sighs by day. To pay me back for scorning love, Love has driven sleep from my captive eyes and made them stay awake to watch my own heart's sorrow."],[8,14,"Oh, gentle Proteus, Love is a mighty lord, and he's humbled me so much that I admit there's no pain like his punishment, and no joy on earth like serving him. Now I want no conversation except about love. Now I can eat breakfast, dinner and supper, and sleep, on nothing but the bare word \"love\"."]],"424":[[0,5,"Not for the world! Why, man, she's mine, and I'm as rich in having such a jewel as twenty seas would be if all their sand were pearls, their water nectar and their rocks pure gold. Forgive me for not thinking about you, because you can see I'm infatuated with my love."],[6,9,"My foolish rival, whom her father likes only because his estates are so huge, has gone with her, and I must follow, because love, as you know, is full of jealousy."]],"431":[[0,6,"Just as one heat drives out another, or as one nail is forced out by another, so the memory of my old love has been completely forgotten for a newer one. Is it my eyes, or Valentine's praise, her real perfection or my false wrongdoing, that makes me reason so unreasonably?"],[7,14,"She's beautiful; and so is Julia, whom I love— whom I did love, because now my love has melted, and like a wax figure in front of a fire it keeps no trace of what it was. I think my devotion to Valentine has cooled, and I don't love him as I used to. Oh, but I love his lady far too much, and that's why I love him so little."],[15,22,"How much more will I dote on her when I've thought it over, if I begin to love her like this without thinking? I've only seen her outward appearance so far, and that's dazzled the light of my reason; but when I see her inner perfections, I'll certainly be blinded. If I can stop my straying love, I will; if not, I'll use all my skill to win her."]],"471":[[0,7,"If I leave my Julia, I'll break my vow; if I love beautiful Silvia, I'll break my vow; if I wrong my friend, I'll break my vow worst of all. And the very same power that made me swear in the first place drives me to break three oaths at once. Love told me to swear, and Love tells me to break my word. Oh, sweetly tempting Love, if you've sinned, teach me, your tempted subject, how to excuse it."],[8,15,"At first I adored a twinkling star, but now I worship a heavenly sun. Thoughtless vows may be broken thoughtfully, and a man lacks sense if he lacks the willpower to teach his mind to swap bad for better. Shame, shame, irreverent tongue, to call her bad, when you've so often declared she ruled over all, with twenty thousand oaths sworn on your soul."],[16,25,"I can't stop loving, and yet I do; but I stop loving where I should love. I lose Julia, and I lose Valentine. If I keep them, I'll have to lose myself. If I lose them, then by losing them I find myself in place of Valentine, and Silvia in place of Julia. I'm dearer to myself than any friend, because love always values itself most; and Silvia, as heaven that made her beautiful is my witness, makes Julia look like a sunburnt African."],[26,34,"I'll forget that Julia is alive, remembering that my love for her is dead; and I'll treat Valentine as an enemy, aiming at Silvia as a sweeter friend. I can't be true to myself now without using some treachery against Valentine. Tonight he plans to climb up to heavenly Silvia's bedroom window with a rope ladder, and I, his rival, am in on the secret."],[35,42,"I'll tell her father straight away about their disguises and planned escape. He'll be furious and banish Valentine, because he means Thurio to marry his daughter. But once Valentine is gone, I'll quickly use some sly trick to block dull Thurio's slow courtship. Love, lend me wings to make my plan move fast, as you've lent me the wit to plot it."]],"481":[[0,8,"The more you dam it up, the more it burns. You know that a stream that glides along with a gentle murmur rages impatiently when it's blocked; but when its smooth course isn't held back, it makes sweet music over the shining pebbles and gives a gentle kiss to every reed it passes on its pilgrimage. And so it wanders through many winding bends, playing happily, to the wild ocean."],[9,14,"So let me go, and don't block my course. I'll be as patient as a gentle stream and make a game of every tiring step, until the last step brings me to my love. And there I'll rest, as a blessed soul rests in heaven after much trouble."]],"499":[[0,5,"Now, if you love me, don't wrong him by thinking badly of his faithfulness. Just earn my love by loving him. Come with me to my room right now and make a list of everything I need for my longed-for journey."],[6,10,"I leave everything I own in your hands: my goods, my lands, my good name. In return, just get me on my way. Come on, don't answer, just get to it at once. I can't bear waiting."]],"506":[[0,8,"My gracious lord, the law of friendship tells me to keep secret what I'm about to reveal. But when I remember the kindness you've shown me, undeserving as I am, my duty spurs me to say what nothing in the world could otherwise drag out of me. Know, worthy prince, that my friend Sir Valentine intends to steal your daughter away tonight. I've been let in on the plot."],[9,17,"I know you've decided to give her to Thurio, whom your gentle daughter hates, and if she were stolen away from you like this, it would be a great grief to you in your old age. So, out of duty, I chose to block my friend's plan rather than keep quiet and pile on your head a load of sorrows that, if nothing prevented them, would weigh you down into an early grave."]],"507":[[0,10,"Proteus, thank you for your honest concern. To repay it, call on me as long as I live. I've often seen this love of theirs myself, perhaps when they thought I was fast asleep, and I've often meant to ban Sir Valentine from her company and from my court. But I was afraid my suspicions might be wrong and so disgrace the man unfairly, a rash thing I've always avoided. So I was friendly to him, in order to find out what you've now told me."],[11,15,"And so that you can see how I've feared this, knowing that young people are easily tempted, I make her sleep each night in an upper tower, and I always keep the key myself. She can't be carried off from there."]],"508":[[0,5,"Know, noble lord, they've worked out a way for him to climb up to her bedroom window and bring her down with a rope ladder. The young lover has gone to fetch it now, and he'll come this way with it soon. Here you can stop him, if you like."],[6,9,"But, my good lord, do it so cleverly that no one guesses I told you. It's love for you, not hatred for my friend, that has made me reveal this plan."]],"519":[[0,5,"No, believe me, she's sulky, moody, contrary, proud, disobedient, stubborn and undutiful. She cares nothing that she's my child, and she isn't afraid of me as she should be of her father. And, I can tell you, this pride of hers, after some thought, has turned my love away from her."],[6,11,"I thought the rest of my old age would be cared for by her duty as a daughter, but now I've firmly decided to take a wife and throw her out to whoever will take her in. Then let her beauty be her dowry, since she cares nothing for me or my wealth."]],"524":[[0,6,"A woman sometimes rejects what pleases her most. Send her another; never give up on her, because scorn at first makes love stronger later. If she frowns, it isn't because she hates you, but to make you love her more. If she scolds you, it isn't because she wants you gone, because the silly things go mad if they're left alone."],[7,12,"Don't be put off, whatever she says, because when she says \"Get out\" she doesn't mean \"Go away!\" Flatter and praise them, admire and extol their charms; however dark-skinned they are, say they have angels' faces. I say the man who has a tongue is no man if he can't win a woman with it."]],"544":[[0,9,"[Reads] \"My thoughts stay with my Silvia every night, and they are slaves to me, who sends them flying to her. Oh, if their master could come and go as easily, he would lie himself where they lie, unfeeling. My messenger thoughts rest in your pure breast, while I, their king, who urges them to go there, curse the good luck that has blessed them so, because I lack my servants' fortune. I curse myself, because I send them, so that they lodge where their lord should be.\""],[10,16,"What's this? [Reads] \"Silvia, tonight I will set you free.\" So that's it; and here's the ladder for the job. Why, Phaethon, for you're a nobody like Merops's son, will you try to drive the sun's chariot and burn up the world with your reckless folly? Will you reach for the stars just because they shine on you?"],[17,29,"Go, low intruder, presumptuous slave! Give your fawning smiles to your equals, and think that my patience, not what you deserve, is what allows you to leave. Thank me for this more than for all the favours I've given you, far too many. But if you stay in my lands longer than the fastest journey takes to leave our royal court, by heaven, my anger will far exceed any love I ever had for my daughter or for you. Get out. I won't listen to your useless excuses. If you value your life, leave here quickly."]],"546":[[0,7,"And why not death, rather than this living torture? To die is to be banished from myself, and Silvia is myself. To be banished from her is to be cut off from my own self: a deadly banishment. What light is light, if I can't see Silvia? What joy is joy, if Silvia isn't near? Unless I imagine she's near and feed on the shadow of perfection."],[8,17,"Unless I'm with Silvia at night, there's no music in the nightingale. Unless I see Silvia by day, there's no daylight for me to see. She's my very being, and I'll cease to exist if her lovely influence doesn't nourish, light, cherish and keep me alive. I don't escape death by escaping his death sentence: if I stay here, I'm only waiting for death, but if I go away from here, I'm running away from life."]],"574":[[0,6,"Yes, yes; and she has offered up against the sentence, which still stands, a sea of melting pearls that some call tears. She poured them at her father's harsh feet, and with them, on her knees, she offered her humble self, wringing her hands, whose whiteness suited them so well it was as if they had just turned pale with grief."],[7,14,"But neither bended knees, pure hands raised up, sad sighs, deep groans nor silver tears could move her pitiless father: if Valentine is caught, he must die. Besides, her pleading for your pardon made him so angry that he ordered her locked up, with many bitter threats that she would stay there."]],"576":[[0,6,"Stop grieving over what you can't help, and work out how to help what you're grieving over. Time is the nurse and parent of everything good. If you stay here, you can't see your love; besides, staying will cut your life short. Hope is a lover's walking stick: walk away from here with it and use it to fight off despairing thoughts."],[7,15,"Your letters can be here even if you're away. If you write them to me, I'll deliver them right into your love's milk-white bosom. Now isn't the time to argue. Come, I'll take you through the city gate, and before we part we'll talk at length about everything to do with your love. If you love Silvia, even if you don't care about yourself, think of the danger and come with me."]],"581":[[0,7,"I'm only a fool, you see, and yet I've got the sense to think my master is a kind of knave; but that's all one, as long as he's only one knave. There's no one alive who knows I'm in love, and yet I'm in love, but a team of horses won't drag that out of me, nor who it is I love. And yet it's a woman, but which woman I won't tell even myself. And yet it's a milkmaid. And yet she's not a maid, because she's had a baby christened. And yet she is a maid, because she's her master's maid and works for wages. She has more good qualities than a water spaniel, which is a lot for a mere Christian. [Pulls out a paper]"],[8,11,"Here's the catalogue of her qualities. \"First, she can fetch and carry.\" Why, a horse can do no more than that; no, a horse can't fetch, only carry, so she's better than a nag. \"Next, she can milk.\" Look, that's a sweet virtue in a girl with clean hands."]],"674":[[0,5,"And, Proteus, we can trust you in this, because we know from Valentine that you're already a faithful servant of Love and won't quickly turn and change your mind. On that guarantee, you'll be allowed in to see Silvia and talk with her as much as you like."],[6,9,"She's dull, heavy and melancholy, and for your friend's sake she'll be glad of your company. There you can use your powers of persuasion to soften her into hating young Valentine and loving my friend."]],"677":[[0,8,"Tell her that on the altar of her beauty you sacrifice your tears, your sighs, your heart. Write till your ink runs dry, then wet it again with your tears, and make up some moving line that shows how true you are. Because Orpheus's lute was strung with poets' heartstrings, and its golden touch could soften steel and stones, tame tigers and make huge sea monsters leave the bottomless deep to dance on the sand."],[9,14,"After your sad, lamenting love poems, go by night to your lady's bedroom window with a group of sweet musicians. Have them play a mournful tune; the dead silence of the night will suit such sweet complaints of grief. This will win her, or nothing will."]],"728":[[0,5,"I've already been false to Valentine, and now I must be just as unfair to Thurio. Pretending to speak up for him, I get the chance to push my own love. But Silvia is too beautiful, too faithful, too holy to be corrupted by my worthless gifts."],[6,16,"When I swear true loyalty to her, she taunts me with my betrayal of my friend. When I offer my vows to her beauty, she tells me to remember how I broke my oath when I broke faith with Julia, whom I loved. And in spite of all her sharp retorts, the least of which would kill a lover's hope, my love, like a spaniel, grows the more she kicks it away, and goes on fawning on her. But here comes Thurio. Now we must go to her window and play her some evening music."]],"748":[[0,4,"Who is Silvia, and what is she, that all our young men praise her? She is holy, beautiful and wise; heaven gave her such grace so that she would be admired."],[5,9,"Is she as kind as she is beautiful? Beauty lives with kindness. Love, who is blind, goes to her eyes to be cured of his blindness, and once cured, he lives there."],[10,14,"So let us sing to Silvia that she is supreme. She surpasses every mortal thing that lives on this dull earth. Let us bring her garlands."]],"780":[[0,5,"You have your wish. What I want is this: that you go home to bed at once. You sly, lying, false, disloyal man, do you think I'm so shallow, so stupid, that I'll be seduced by your flattery, when you've deceived so many with your vows?"],[6,11,"Go back, go back, and make it up to your love. As for me, I swear by this pale moon that I'm so far from granting your request that I despise you for your wicked courting, and in a moment I'll scold myself for even the time I'm spending talking to you."]],"809":[[0,10,"Oh, Eglamour, you're a gentleman, and don't think I'm flattering you, because I swear I'm not: brave, wise, compassionate, accomplished. You know how much I love the banished Valentine, and how my father would force me to marry foolish Thurio, whom I hate with all my soul. You've loved yourself, and I've heard you say no grief ever cut so close to your heart as when your lady, your true love, died, and you vowed on her grave to live chaste for ever."],[11,20,"Sir Eglamour, I want to go to Valentine, to Mantua, where I hear he's living. And since the roads are dangerous to travel, I'd like your worthy company, because I trust your loyalty and honour. Don't remind me of my father's anger, Eglamour. Think of my grief, a lady's grief, and how right I am to run away from here to escape a most unholy marriage, which heaven and fortune always punish with misery."],[21,25,"I beg you, from a heart as full of sorrows as the sea is of sand, keep me company and come with me. If not, keep secret what I've told you, so that I can risk going alone."]],"819":[[0,9,"When a man's servant behaves like a cur to him, you see, it's hard: one I brought up from a puppy, one I saved from drowning when three or four of his blind brothers and sisters went that way. I've trained him exactly as you'd say, \"That's how I'd train a dog.\" I was sent to give him as a present to Mistress Silvia from my master, and I'd hardly got into the dining room before he trots up to her plate and steals her chicken leg. Oh, it's a terrible thing when a dog can't behave himself in company! I'd want one that took it on himself to be a real dog, a dog, so to speak, at everything."],[10,20,"If I hadn't had more sense than him and taken the blame for something he did, I truly think he'd have been hanged for it; as sure as I'm alive, he'd have paid for it. You can judge for yourselves. He pushes in among three or four gentlemanly dogs under the Duke's table, and he hadn't been there, excuse me, long enough to have a pee before the whole room could smell him. \"Out with the dog!\" says one; \"What mutt is that?\" says another; \"Whip him out,\" says a third; \"Hang him,\" says the Duke. I knew the smell already, so I knew it was Crab, and I go up to the fellow who whips the dogs. \"Friend,\" I say, \"are you going to whip the dog?\" \"Yes, I certainly am,\" he says. \"Then you do him all the more wrong,\" I say. \"It was me who did the thing you know about.\" Without more fuss he whips me out of the room."],[21,28,"How many masters would do that for their servant? No, I swear I've sat in the stocks for puddings he's stolen, or else he'd have been executed. I've stood in the pillory for geese he's killed, or else he'd have suffered for it. [To Crab] You don't think about that now. No, I remember the trick you played on me when I said goodbye to Madam Silvia. Didn't I tell you always to watch me and do as I do? When did you ever see me lift my leg and pee against a lady's hooped skirt? Did you ever see me do a trick like that?"]],"833":[[0,7,"Sebastian, I've taken you on partly because I need a young man like you who can do my business with some discretion, since there's no trusting that foolish oaf; but mainly because of your face and your manner, which, if my judgement isn't wrong, show a good upbringing, good fortune and honesty. So know that this is why I'm employing you."],[8,10,"Go at once and take this ring with you. Give it to Madam Silvia. The woman who gave it to me loved me very much."]],"843":[[0,6,"How many women would carry a message like this? Oh, poor Proteus, you've hired a fox to look after your lambs. Oh, you poor fool, why do I pity him, when he despises me with all his heart? Because he loves her, he despises me; because I love him, I have to pity him."],[7,17,"I gave him this ring when he left me, to make him remember my love; and now I, an unhappy messenger, have to plead for something I don't want him to get, carry something I'd like to see refused, and praise his faithfulness, which I'd like to criticise. I'm my master's true and constant love, but I can't be a true servant to my master without being a false traitor to myself. Still, I'll woo her for him, but so half-heartedly that, heaven knows, I don't want him to succeed."]],"875":[[0,6,"About my height. At Whitsun, when all our entertainments were put on, the young people got me to play the woman's part, and I was dressed in Madam Julia's gown, which everyone said fitted me as well as if it had been made for me. So I know she's about my height."],[7,14,"And that time I made her cry her heart out, because I played a sad part. Madam, it was Ariadne, grieving over Theseus's broken vows and his cruel desertion. I acted it so vividly with my tears that my poor mistress was moved and wept bitterly; and I wish I may die if I didn't feel her sorrow in my own heart."]],"879":[[0,8,"A virtuous lady, gentle and beautiful. I hope my master's wooing gets nowhere, since she cares so much about my mistress's love. Oh, how love can play games with itself! Here's her picture; let me look. I think if I had a headdress like that, this face of mine would be every bit as lovely as hers. And yet the painter flattered her a little, unless I'm flattering myself too much."],[9,16,"Her hair is auburn; mine is perfectly golden. If that's the only difference in his love, I'll get myself a wig that colour. Her eyes are as grey as glass, and so are mine. Yes, but her forehead is low, and mine is high. What can it be that he values in her that I couldn't make him value in me, if foolish Love weren't a blind god?"],[17,25,"Come, shadow, come and pick up this shadow, because it's your rival. Oh, you unfeeling image, you'll be worshipped, kissed, loved and adored; and if there were any sense in his idolatry, my real self would be the statue in your place. I'll treat you kindly for your mistress's sake, since she treated me kindly; or else, I swear by Jove, I'd have scratched out your unseeing eyes to make my master fall out of love with you."]],"924":[[0,8,"Why then, she's run off to that peasant Valentine, and Eglamour is with her. It's true, because Friar Lawrence met them both as he was wandering through the forest doing penance. He knew Eglamour well and guessed it was her, but because she was masked he wasn't sure. Besides, she meant to go to confession at Patrick's cell this evening, and she wasn't there. These signs confirm that she's run away from here."],[9,13,"So please don't stand here talking, but mount your horses at once and meet me at the foot of the hills on the road to Mantua, where they've fled. Hurry, sweet gentlemen, and follow me."]],"945":[[0,5,"How custom turns into habit in a man! I like this shady wilderness, these lonely woods, better than busy, crowded towns. Here I can sit alone, seen by no one, and match my troubles to the nightingale's sad song and sing out my sorrows."],[6,11,"Oh, you who live in my heart, don't leave the house empty for so long, in case it falls into ruin, collapses and leaves no memory of what it was. Rebuild me with your presence, Silvia. Gentle nymph, comfort your lonely shepherd."]],"967":[[0,4,"You worthless friend, without loyalty or love, because that's what friends are like now. Treacherous man, you've cheated my hopes. Nothing but my own eyes could have convinced me. Now I don't dare say I have one friend alive; you'd prove me wrong."],[5,10,"Who can be trusted, when your own right hand betrays your heart? Proteus, I'm sorry that I must never trust you again, but treat the whole world as a stranger because of you. The private wound is the deepest. Oh, most cursed time, that of all enemies a friend should be the worst!"]],"997":[[0,5,"All the more low and contemptible you are, to go to such lengths to win her and then give her up on such easy terms. Now, by the honour of my ancestors, I applaud your spirit, Valentine, and think you worthy of an empress's love."],[6,11,"Know, then, that I here forget all my old grievances, cancel every grudge and call you home again. On the strength of your unmatched merit, I declare a new standing for you, and I sign my name to it like this: Sir Valentine, you are a gentleman, and of good birth. Take your Silvia, because you have earned her."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"love":["Love and desire","Love at first sight, lovesick symptoms, serenades and the blind god Love."],"friend":["Friendship","Valentine and Proteus: the bond between men, tested by love for the same woman."],"faith":["Constancy and betrayal","Oaths kept and broken: Julia's faith against Proteus's changes."],"disguise":["Disguise and identity","Julia as Sebastian, pictures and shadows, and who people really are."],"tokens":["Letters, rings and tokens","Torn letters, a rope ladder, two rings and a portrait carry the plot."],"fathers":["Fathers and authority","Antonio and the Duke decide their children's lives."],"servants":["Servants and clowns","Speed, Lance and Crab mock their masters' love from below."],"wild":["Court and forest","Exile, outlaws and the wild wood where everything is settled."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"VALENTINE","text":"Home-keeping youth have ever homely wits.","themes":["friend","wild"],"note":"Valentine's first argument for travel."},{"id":"12","who":"VALENTINE","text":"To be in love, where scorn is bought with groans,","themes":["love"],"note":"Valentine mocks love as a bad bargain."},{"id":"18","who":"VALENTINE","text":"Is turned to folly, blasting in the bud,","themes":["love"],"note":"Love as the grub that kills young promise."},{"id":"24","who":"PROTEUS","text":"He after honour hunts, I after love.","themes":["love","friend"],"note":"Proteus's first soliloquy."},{"id":"24","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Thou, Julia, thou hast metamorphosed me,","themes":["love","disguise"],"note":"Proteus blames Julia for changing him."},{"id":"66","who":"SPEED","text":"Give her no token but stones, for she's as hard as steel.","themes":["servants","tokens"],"note":"Speed's report of Julia."},{"id":"90","who":"LUCETTA","text":"I think him so because I think him so.","themes":["love"],"note":"Lucetta's 'woman's reason' for choosing Proteus."},{"id":"98","who":"LUCETTA","text":"O, they love least that let men know their love.","themes":["love"],"note":"Lucetta on hidden love."},{"id":"111","who":"JULIA","text":"Since maids in modesty say \"No\" to that","themes":["love","disguise"],"note":"Julia on modest courtship."},{"id":"111","who":"JULIA","text":"Fie, fie, how wayward is this foolish love","themes":["love"],"note":"Julia scolds herself."},{"id":"141","who":"JULIA","text":"Here is a coil with protestation! [Tears the letter]","themes":["tokens","love"],"note":"Julia tears up Proteus's letter."},{"id":"144","who":"JULIA","text":"O hateful hands, to tear such loving words!","themes":["tokens","love"],"note":"Julia regrets tearing the letter."},{"id":"162","who":"ANTONIO","text":"Experience is by industry achieved","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Antonio decides Proteus must travel."},{"id":"178","who":"ANTONIO","text":"For what I will, I will, and there an end.","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Antonio's command."},{"id":"182","who":"PROTEUS","text":"The uncertain glory of an April day,","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Proteus on the changeable nature of love."},{"id":"203","who":"SPEED","text":"to sigh, like a schoolboy that had lost his ABC;","themes":["servants","love"],"note":"Speed lists the symptoms of love."},{"id":"229","who":"SPEED","text":"Because Love is blind.","themes":["love","servants"],"note":"Speed's verdict on Valentine."},{"id":"267","who":"SPEED","text":"O jest unseen, inscrutable, invisible,","themes":["servants","tokens"],"note":"Speed sees through Silvia's trick."},{"id":"273","who":"SPEED","text":"To yourself. Why, she woos you by a figure.","themes":["tokens","love"],"note":"Speed explains Silvia's letter."},{"id":"294","who":"JULIA","text":"Keep this remembrance for thy Julia's sake.","themes":["tokens","faith"],"note":"Julia gives Proteus a ring."},{"id":"299","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Here is my hand for my true constancy.","themes":["faith","tokens"],"note":"Proteus's vow at parting."},{"id":"301","who":"PROTEUS","text":"For truth hath better deeds than words to grace it.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Proteus on Julia's silent leaving."},{"id":"304","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Alas, this parting strikes poor lovers dumb.","themes":["love"],"note":"Proteus leaves Verona."},{"id":"308","who":"LANCE","text":"I think Crab my dog be the sourest-natured dog that lives:","themes":["servants"],"note":"Lance's first monologue."},{"id":"308","who":"LANCE","text":"This shoe is my father. No, this left shoe is my father;","themes":["servants"],"note":"Lance acts out the farewell."},{"id":"370","who":"VALENTINE","text":"He is complete in feature and in mind,","themes":["friend"],"note":"Valentine praises Proteus to the Duke."},{"id":"381","who":"VALENTINE","text":"Upon a homely object, Love can wink.","themes":["love"],"note":"Valentine mocks Thurio."},{"id":"408","who":"VALENTINE","text":"O gentle Proteus, Love's a mighty lord,","themes":["love"],"note":"Valentine confesses his conversion."},{"id":"424","who":"VALENTINE","text":"Not for the world! Why, man, she is mine own,","themes":["love","friend"],"note":"Valentine's joy in Silvia."},{"id":"431","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Even as one heat another heat expels,","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Proteus falls for Silvia."},{"id":"431","who":"PROTEUS","text":"O, but I love his lady too too much,","themes":["friend","faith"],"note":"Proteus admits his new love."},{"id":"431","who":"PROTEUS","text":"If I can check my erring love, I will;","themes":["faith"],"note":"Proteus's resolution."},{"id":"452","who":"LANCE","text":"Why, stand-under and under-stand is all one.","themes":["servants"],"note":"Lance's pun."},{"id":"471","who":"PROTEUS","text":"To leave my Julia, shall I be forsworn;","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Proteus's betrayal soliloquy."},{"id":"471","who":"PROTEUS","text":"I to myself am dearer than a friend,","themes":["friend","faith"],"note":"Proteus chooses himself."},{"id":"471","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Shows Julia but a swarthy Ethiope.","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Proteus compares Julia with Silvia."},{"id":"471","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Love, lend me wings to make my purpose swift,","themes":["faith"],"note":"Proteus resolves to betray Valentine."},{"id":"479","who":"JULIA","text":"As seek to quench the fire of love with words.","themes":["love"],"note":"Julia refuses to be talked out of her journey."},{"id":"481","who":"JULIA","text":"The more thou damm'st it up, the more it burns.","themes":["love"],"note":"Julia on love that is blocked."},{"id":"481","who":"JULIA","text":"I'll be as patient as a gentle stream","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Julia's journey as a stream to the sea."},{"id":"483","who":"JULIA","text":"Not like a woman, for I would prevent","themes":["disguise"],"note":"Julia decides to travel in disguise."},{"id":"497","who":"JULIA","text":"His words are bonds, his oaths are oracles,","themes":["faith"],"note":"Julia's faith in Proteus."},{"id":"506","who":"PROTEUS","text":"The law of friendship bids me to conceal,","themes":["friend","faith"],"note":"Proteus betrays Valentine to the Duke."},{"id":"519","who":"DUKE","text":"I now am full resolved to take a wife","themes":["fathers"],"note":"The Duke's invented complaint."},{"id":"524","who":"VALENTINE","text":"That man that hath a tongue, I say, is no man","themes":["love"],"note":"Valentine's advice on wooing."},{"id":"544","who":"DUKE","text":"Why, Phaëthon—for thou art Merops' son—","themes":["fathers","love"],"note":"The Duke banishes Valentine."},{"id":"546","who":"VALENTINE","text":"And Silvia is myself; banished from her","themes":["love","wild"],"note":"Valentine's lament."},{"id":"546","who":"VALENTINE","text":"What light is light, if Silvia be not seen?","themes":["love"],"note":"Valentine on life without Silvia."},{"id":"568","who":"VALENTINE","text":"No Valentine indeed for sacred Silvia.","themes":["love","disguise"],"note":"Valentine puns on his own name."},{"id":"576","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Hope is a lover's staff; walk hence with that","themes":["friend","faith"],"note":"Proteus comforts Valentine."},{"id":"581","who":"LANCE","text":"Here is the cate-log of her condition.","themes":["servants","love"],"note":"Lance's list of his milkmaid's qualities."},{"id":"620","who":"LANCE","text":"woman's only virtue.","themes":["servants"],"note":"Lance on the virtue of silence."},{"id":"653","who":"DUKE","text":"Trenched in ice, which with an hour's heat","themes":["fathers","faith"],"note":"The Duke on Silvia's love."},{"id":"666","who":"PROTEUS","text":"With falsehood, cowardice, and poor descent,","themes":["friend","faith"],"note":"Proteus plans to slander Valentine."},{"id":"677","who":"PROTEUS","text":"For Orpheus' lute was strung with poets' sinews,","themes":["love"],"note":"Proteus advises Thurio on serenading."},{"id":"719","who":"SECOND OUTLAW","text":"To make a virtue of necessity","themes":["wild"],"note":"The outlaws offer Valentine leadership."},{"id":"723","who":"VALENTINE","text":"On silly women or poor passengers.","themes":["wild","faith"],"note":"Valentine's condition for joining the outlaws."},{"id":"728","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Yet, spaniel-like, the more she spurns my love,","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Proteus on Silvia's rejection."},{"id":"748","who":"PROTEUS","text":"~Who is Silvia? What is she,","themes":["love"],"note":"The serenade."},{"id":"754","who":"JULIA","text":"Not so; but yet so false that he grieves my very heart-strings.","themes":["disguise","faith"],"note":"Julia hears Proteus's serenade."},{"id":"762","who":"JULIA","text":"I would always have one play but one thing.","themes":["faith","disguise"],"note":"Julia wishes Proteus were constant."},{"id":"780","who":"SILVIA","text":"Thou subtle, perjured, false, disloyal man,","themes":["faith"],"note":"Silvia rejects Proteus."},{"id":"781","who":"PROTEUS","text":"But she is dead.","themes":["faith","disguise"],"note":"Proteus lies about Julia."},{"id":"789","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Is else devoted, I am but a shadow;","themes":["disguise","tokens"],"note":"Proteus asks for Silvia's picture."},{"id":"819","who":"LANCE","text":"When a man's servant shall play the cur with him, look you, it goes","themes":["servants"],"note":"Lance's second monologue."},{"id":"819","who":"LANCE","text":"How many masters would do this for his servant?","themes":["servants","faith"],"note":"Lance on his loyalty to Crab."},{"id":"840","who":"JULIA","text":"She dreams on him that has forgot her love;","themes":["faith","disguise"],"note":"Julia speaks of herself as another."},{"id":"843","who":"JULIA","text":"A fox to be the shepherd of thy lambs.","themes":["disguise","faith"],"note":"Julia on being hired by Proteus."},{"id":"843","who":"JULIA","text":"I am my master's true confirmed love,","themes":["faith","disguise"],"note":"Julia's divided duty."},{"id":"864","who":"SILVIA","text":"Mine shall not do his Julia so much wrong.","themes":["tokens","faith"],"note":"Silvia refuses Julia's ring."},{"id":"875","who":"JULIA","text":"Madam, 'twas Ariadne, passioning","themes":["disguise"],"note":"Julia's invented story of playing Ariadne."},{"id":"879","who":"JULIA","text":"Her hair is auburn, mine is perfect yellow;","themes":["disguise","love"],"note":"Julia compares herself with Silvia's picture."},{"id":"879","who":"JULIA","text":"If this fond Love were not a blinded god?","themes":["love"],"note":"Julia on why Proteus changed."},{"id":"924","who":"DUKE","text":"Why then, she's fled unto that peasant Valentine,","themes":["fathers"],"note":"The Duke learns Silvia has fled."},{"id":"945","who":"VALENTINE","text":"How use doth breed a habit in a man!","themes":["wild"],"note":"Valentine in the forest."},{"id":"945","who":"VALENTINE","text":"Repair me with thy presence, Silvia;","themes":["love","wild"],"note":"Valentine longs for Silvia."},{"id":"956","who":"SILVIA","text":"I do detest false perjured Proteus.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Silvia rejects Proteus again."},{"id":"958","who":"SILVIA","text":"Than plural faith, which is too much by one.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Silvia on Proteus's two loves."},{"id":"959","who":"PROTEUS","text":"Who respects friend?","themes":["friend","love"],"note":"Proteus puts love above friendship."},{"id":"961","who":"PROTEUS","text":"And love you 'gainst the nature of love—force ye.","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Proteus tries to force Silvia."},{"id":"967","who":"VALENTINE","text":"The private wound is deepest. O time most accurst,","themes":["friend","faith"],"note":"Valentine on Proteus's betrayal."},{"id":"969","who":"VALENTINE","text":"By penitence th' Eternal's wrath's appeased.","themes":["friend","faith"],"note":"Valentine forgives Proteus."},{"id":"969","who":"VALENTINE","text":"All that was mine in Silvia I give thee.","themes":["friend","love"],"note":"Valentine's offer to Proteus."},{"id":"985","who":"JULIA","text":"Women to change their shapes than men their minds.","themes":["disguise","faith"],"note":"Julia reveals herself."},{"id":"986","who":"PROTEUS","text":"But constant, he were perfect. That one error","themes":["faith"],"note":"Proteus admits his fault."},{"id":"1006","who":"VALENTINE","text":"One feast, one house, one mutual happiness.","themes":["friend","love"],"note":"The play's last line."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Valentine and Proteus are close friends in Verona. Valentine sails to Milan to see the world; Proteus stays behind, in love with Julia, until his father sends him to Milan too. The lovers exchange rings and vows before he leaves.","In Milan, Valentine has fallen in love with Silvia, the Duke's daughter, whom her father means to marry to rich, foolish Thurio. Proteus falls for Silvia at first sight, betrays Valentine's plan to elope, and gets him banished. Meanwhile Julia travels to Milan disguised as a page, Sebastian, and is hired by Proteus to carry his gifts to Silvia.","Valentine becomes captain of a band of outlaws. Silvia runs away to find him, and everyone ends up in the forest. Valentine saves Silvia from Proteus's attempt to force her, forgives his friend at once, and Julia reveals herself. Proteus returns to Julia, the Duke gives Silvia to Valentine, and a double wedding is planned."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Valentine leaves Verona for Milan","id":2},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Proteus stays for love of Julia","id":24},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Julia tears up Proteus's letter, then kisses the pieces","id":144},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Antonio orders Proteus to Milan","id":178},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Silvia makes Valentine write a love letter to himself","id":267},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Proteus and Julia exchange rings","id":299},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Lance's farewell with his dog Crab","id":308},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Valentine praises Proteus to the Duke","id":370},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Valentine reveals the elopement plan","id":426},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Proteus falls in love with Silvia","id":431},{"s":"a2s6","t":"Proteus decides to betray Valentine","id":471},{"s":"a2s7","t":"Julia plans to follow Proteus in disguise","id":483},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Proteus tells the Duke of the elopement","id":506},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The Duke finds the rope ladder and banishes Valentine","id":544},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Lance's list of his milkmaid's qualities","id":581},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Proteus agrees to slander Valentine to Silvia","id":672},{"s":"a4s1","t":"The outlaws make Valentine their captain","id":723},{"s":"a4s2","t":"'Who is Silvia?': Julia watches the serenade","id":748},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Silvia rejects Proteus; he claims Julia is dead","id":781},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Silvia asks Eglamour to help her escape","id":809},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Lance takes a whipping for Crab","id":819},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Proteus sends Julia, as Sebastian, with her own ring","id":833},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Silvia refuses the ring and pities Julia","id":864},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The Duke learns that Silvia has fled","id":924},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Outlaws capture Silvia; Eglamour runs away","id":938},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Proteus tries to force Silvia; Valentine stops him","id":965},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Valentine forgives Proteus and offers him Silvia","id":969},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Julia reveals herself through the rings","id":982},{"s":"a5s4","t":"The Duke gives Silvia to Valentine; a double wedding","id":1006}],"irony":{"299":"Proteus swears constancy with his hand on it; within days he will forget Julia entirely.","370":"Valentine praises Proteus as a perfect gentleman to the man Proteus will soon deceive.","426":"Valentine tells his plan to the one person who will betray it.","497":"Julia praises Proteus's oaths as oracles after he has already broken them.","509":"The Duke promises secrecy to protect the informer, while seeming to trust Valentine.","524":"Valentine eagerly teaches the Duke how to woo, not knowing the Duke is setting a trap for him.","576":"Proteus comforts Valentine about a banishment he caused.","666":"Proteus accuses Valentine of falsehood, the fault that is truly his own.","673":"Thurio asks Proteus to wind Silvia's love onto him; Proteus means to wind it onto himself.","781":"Proteus tells Silvia Julia is dead while Julia stands listening.","822":"Proteus hires his own betrothed as his page without recognising her.","833":"Proteus praises Sebastian's face and honesty, not seeing that it is Julia.","840":"Julia describes her own grief as if it were someone else's, to the man who caused it.","875":"Julia says Julia's gown fitted her perfectly: the audience knows why.","1003":"The Duke says the page has grace and blushes, not knowing the page is a woman."},"devices":{"love":"Blind Love and Cupid","change":"Change and shape-shifting","shadow":"Shadows, pictures and substance","water":"Water, streams and the sea","animals":"Sheep, dogs and beasts","music":"Music and song"},"dev":{"6":[["water","Leander swam the Hellespont for love: deep water stands for deep love.","Hellespont"]],"18":[["change","Love is a grub that blights the bud before it opens.","blasting in the bud"]],"24":[["change","Julia has 'metamorphosed' Proteus, whose name means a shape-changer.","metamorphosed me"]],"29":[["animals","Speed is a straying sheep and Valentine his shepherd.","a sheep doth very often stray"]],"138":[["music","The quarrel becomes a song: sharp, flat, descant and bass.","too harsh a descant"]],"182":[["change","Love is like an April day: sun one moment, cloud the next.","April day"]],"229":[["love","Love is blind, so the lover cannot see clearly.","Love is blind"]],"350":[["change","Thurio is a chameleon that changes colour.","chameleon"]],"379":[["love","Love has twenty pairs of eyes, or none at all.","twenty pair of eyes"]],"431":[["change","One love drives out another like a nail, or melts like a wax image before a fire.","waxen image"],["love","He has only seen her 'picture', her outward looks.","her picture"]],"471":[["love","Love tempted him into perjury: 'sweet-suggesting Love'.","sweet-suggesting Love"]],"481":[["water","Julia's love is a stream that rages when dammed and dances when free.","gentle stream"]],"544":[["change","Phaethon tried to drive the sun and burned the world.","Phaëthon"]],"653":[["water","Love is a figure carved in ice that melts in an hour.","Trenched in ice"]],"677":[["music","Orpheus's music tamed tigers and drew sea monsters to dance.","Orpheus' lute"]],"728":[["animals","Proteus fawns on Silvia like a spaniel that is kicked.","spaniel-like"]],"748":[["music","The serenade praises Silvia, and Love lives in her eyes.","Who is Silvia?"],["love","Blind Love goes to Silvia's eyes to be cured.","To help him of his blindness"]],"754":[["music","Julia's puns on false playing and heart-strings.","heart-strings"]],"789":[["shadow","Proteus will love Silvia's picture, a shadow of the real woman.","I am but a shadow"]],"790":[["shadow","Julia is herself only a shadow of who she was.","make it but a shadow"]],"819":[["animals","Crab's misdeeds and Lance's punishment for them.","play the cur"]],"843":[["animals","Julia is a fox put in charge of lambs.","A fox to be the shepherd"]],"879":[["shadow","Julia talks to the portrait as her rival.","Come, shadow"],["love","Love is a blinded god.","blinded god"]],"945":[["music","Valentine tunes his sorrows to the nightingale.","nightingale's complaining notes"]],"985":[["change","Women change their shapes, men change their minds.","change their shapes"]]},"stage":{"street":"A street or open place","garden":"Julia's garden in Verona","house":"A room in a house in Verona","palace":"The Duke's palace in Milan","abbey":"The abbey in Milan","forest":"The forest near Mantua"},"arc":{"Romantic love":[5,7,6,7,8,4,8,5,7,5,6,4,6,8,7,7,6,6,7,10],"Valentine and Proteus's friendship":[9,4,5,5,6,4,9,4,1,4,1,2,1,2,2,3,1,1,2,9]},"arcnote":"Love rises from Valentine's mockery to his passion for Silvia, Julia's journey and the serenade, while the friendship that starts the play falls apart the moment Proteus sees Silvia. It hits bottom with the betrayal to the Duke and the slander plan, stays low through the forest scenes and is restored in a few lines at the very end, when love and friendship peak together in the double wedding.","context":[["Date","Probably one of Shakespeare's earliest plays, written around 1589 to 1591; the Oxford editors place it first of all. Francis Meres names it in 1598. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623, which is the only early text."],["Sources","The Julia plot comes from the story of Felix and Felismena in Jorge de Montemayor's Spanish romance Diana (1559), where a woman follows her faithless lover disguised as a page and is sent to woo another woman for him. The friendship plot draws on the old tale of two friends, Titus and Gisippus, in which one friend gives up his bride to the other."],["Friendship and love","Renaissance writers often praised friendship between men as nobler than love for women, because it was based on equality and virtue. Many debates and stories asked which should win when the two clashed: in John Lyly's popular prose romance Euphues (1578), two close friends fall out over the same woman. The play stages that debate, and Valentine's offer of Silvia is its extreme answer."],["Courtly love","Valentine and Proteus speak the language of courtly love: the lady is a goddess, the lover her 'servant', and love is a lord who punishes scorn. Speed and Lance keep puncturing these conventions."],["Travel and education","Gentlemen's sons were expected to finish their education by travelling or attending a great court, where they learned languages, fighting and manners. Antonio sends Proteus away for exactly this reason."],["Fathers and marriage","Daughters of rich families were expected to marry the man their father chose. The Duke's plan to marry Silvia to Thurio for his wealth was normal, and her flight was a serious defiance."],["Cross-dressing","On the Elizabethan stage boys played all the women's parts, so Julia was a boy playing a girl playing a boy. Shakespeare used the same device later for Portia, Rosalind and Viola."],["Outlaws and Robin Hood","The outlaws compare themselves with Robin Hood's band. Stories of gentlemen forced into the greenwood by injustice were popular, and the forest offers a place outside the court's rules where things can be put right."],["Lance and Crab","Lance was probably written for the company's clown, perhaps Will Kemp. A live dog playing Crab was a guaranteed laugh, and the two monologues are among Shakespeare's first great comic solos."],["Setting and slips","The text shifts between calling the ruler Duke and Emperor, and at one point puts Verona where Milan should be. Most editors think Shakespeare revised as he went and never tidied the details."],["The ending","The final scene is very short: Proteus repents in a few lines, Valentine offers Silvia to him, and Silvia never speaks again. Critics have argued about it for centuries, and modern productions often play it with discomfort rather than easy joy."],["Performance","There is no record of a performance in Shakespeare's lifetime. The play was rarely staged until the twentieth century. It has since been revived often, sometimes set among modern young people, and the song 'Who is Silvia?' is famous through Schubert's setting."]],"critics":{"friend":[["Thomas Hanmer, edition of Shakespeare (1744)","Paraphrased: Hanmer doubted that Shakespeare wrote the play at all, judging most of it below his usual standard."],["Samuel Johnson, edition of Shakespeare (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson found the play full of careless mistakes about places, but judged its language easy and natural, and thought it clearly Shakespeare's."]],"faith":[["William Hazlitt, Characters of Shakespear's Plays (1817)","Paraphrased: Hazlitt called it little more than an outline of a play, but found in it many passages of real beauty, and singled out Julia's devotion."],["Arthur Quiller-Couch, introduction to the Cambridge edition (1921)","Paraphrased: Quiller-Couch thought the ending so absurd that no gentleman in the audience could accept Valentine's offer, and blamed it on the play's crude source."]],"servants":[["Harold Bloom, Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human (1998)","Paraphrased: Bloom considered Lance and Crab the best things in the play, and Lance the first of Shakespeare's great clowns."]],"love":[["Inga-Stina Ewbank, essay on the play (1972)","Paraphrased: Ewbank argued that the play is about the gap between the language of love and its reality: characters keep using fine words that the action shows to be empty."]]},"insults":[{"id":42,"f":"a laced mutton","who":"Speed","n":"Speed's crude name for Julia shows servants mocking their masters' romance."},{"id":69,"f":"Being destined to a drier death on shore","who":"Proteus","n":"Proteus jokes that Speed was born to be hanged."},{"id":137,"f":"You, minion, are too saucy","who":"Julia","n":"Julia puts her maid in her place when the teasing goes too far."},{"id":207,"f":"like the water in an urinal","who":"Speed","n":"Speed's earthy simile for how obvious his master's lovesickness is."},{"id":341,"f":"Wise","who":"Valentine","n":"Valentine's one-word sneer: Thurio only seems wise."},{"id":360,"f":"they live by your bare words","who":"Valentine","n":"Valentine mocks Thurio's stinginess to his servants."},{"id":447,"f":"What an ass art thou!","who":"Speed","n":"Speed and Lance trade insults like friends."},{"id":461,"f":"thou whoreson ass","who":"Speed","n":"Common servants' abuse, part of their banter."},{"id":544,"f":"base intruder, overweening slave","who":"Duke","n":"The Duke attacks Valentine's rank: he is not good enough for Silvia."},{"id":590,"f":"jolt-head","who":"Lance","n":"Lance calls Speed a blockhead before testing his reading."},{"id":780,"f":"Thou subtle, perjured, false, disloyal man","who":"Silvia","n":"Silvia's list of insults names Proteus's exact faults."},{"id":823,"f":"you whoreson peasant","who":"Proteus","n":"Proteus is harsh to his servant, unlike Lance's loyalty to his dog."},{"id":831,"f":"A slave that still an end turns me to shame","who":"Proteus","n":"Proteus blames his servant for his own embarrassment."},{"id":924,"f":"that peasant Valentine","who":"Duke","n":"The Duke still sneers at Valentine's rank."},{"id":958,"f":"Thou counterfeit to thy true friend!","who":"Silvia","n":"Silvia calls Proteus a fake, a forged coin of friendship."},{"id":965,"f":"Ruffian","who":"Valentine","n":"Valentine finally names his friend for what he is doing."},{"id":997,"f":"The more degenerate and base art thou","who":"Duke","n":"The Duke turns on Thurio for giving Silvia up so easily."}],"pronouns":[{"id":3,"f":"Wilt thou be gone? Sweet Valentine, adieu.","n":"The friends use intimate 'thou' to each other."},{"id":294,"f":"Keep this remembrance for thy Julia's sake.","n":"Julia uses 'thy' at the tender moment of parting."},{"id":177,"f":"As one relying on your lordship's will","n":"Proteus uses formal 'your lordship' to his father, who calls him 'thou'."},{"id":245,"f":"Sir Valentine and servant, to you two thousand.","n":"Silvia keeps formal 'you' with Valentine in public."},{"id":780,"f":"Thou subtle, perjured, false, disloyal man","n":"Silvia switches to 'thou' to show contempt for Proteus."},{"id":822,"f":"In what you please; I'll do what I can.","n":"Julia, as a page, must call Proteus 'you' while he calls her 'thee'."},{"id":967,"f":"Thou common friend, that's without faith or love","n":"Valentine's 'thou' to Proteus is now angry, not affectionate."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Proteus goes with Valentine","Proteus stays in Verona"],"a1s2":["Julia sends the letter back unread","Julia secretly reads and treasures the letter"],"a1s3":["Antonio lets Proteus stay","Antonio sends Proteus to Milan"],"a2s1":["Silvia rejects Valentine","Silvia is secretly in love with Valentine"],"a2s2":["Proteus and Julia marry before he goes","They part with vows and rings"],"a2s3":["Lance leaves the dog behind","Lance takes Crab to Milan"],"a2s4":["Proteus is loyal to Valentine","Proteus falls for Silvia"],"a2s5":["Speed and Lance quarrel","They go off drinking together"],"a2s6":["Proteus fights his new love","Proteus plans to betray Valentine"],"a2s7":["Julia waits at home","Julia follows Proteus in disguise"],"a3s1":["The elopement succeeds","The Duke discovers the plan"],"a3s2":["Proteus refuses to slander his friend","Proteus agrees, for his own reasons"],"a4s1":["The outlaws rob and kill Valentine","The outlaws make Valentine their leader"],"a4s2":["Silvia accepts Proteus","Silvia rejects Proteus"],"a4s3":["Silvia marries Thurio","Silvia escapes to find Valentine"],"a4s4":["Julia reveals herself to Silvia","Julia keeps her disguise"],"a5s1":["Silvia is caught at once","Silvia and Eglamour get away"],"a5s2":["The Duke lets her go","The Duke chases after her"],"a5s3":["Eglamour protects Silvia","Eglamour runs away"],"a5s4":["Valentine kills Proteus","Valentine forgives Proteus"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Valentine and Proteus","pts":["Valentine leaves home for honour; Proteus stays for love, then leaves on his father's order.","Valentine mocks love, then becomes its servant; Proteus swears constancy, then breaks every oath.","Valentine trusts completely; Proteus exploits that trust."],"ids":[2,471]},{"t":"Julia and Silvia","pts":["Both stay faithful to the man they love and both leave home to follow him.","Julia hides her feelings at first; Silvia uses a clever trick to show hers.","Julia suffers in disguise; Silvia speaks her scorn openly."],"ids":[481,780]},{"t":"Speed and Lance","pts":["Speed is quick and sharp-tongued; Lance is slow, literal-minded and comic.","Speed mocks his master's love; Lance shows touching love for his dog.","Both servants see their masters more clearly than the masters see themselves."],"ids":[267,819]},{"t":"Lance and Proteus","pts":["Lance suffers whippings and the stocks for his dog's faults.","Proteus lets his friend be banished for his own advantage.","The servant's absurd loyalty shows up the gentleman's treachery."],"ids":[819,506]},{"t":"Thurio and Valentine","pts":["Thurio is rich but foolish; Valentine has wit but less wealth.","Thurio gives Silvia up at the first threat; Valentine risks his life for her.","The Duke prefers Thurio for money, then Valentine for spirit."],"ids":[996,995]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Two Gentlemen of Verona"],["Written","About 1589 to 1591, probably among Shakespeare's first plays; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Romantic comedy"],["Setting","Verona, Milan and a forest near Mantua, in Italy"],["Protagonists","Valentine and Proteus, the two gentlemen; Julia"],["Antagonists","Proteus's own inconstancy; the Duke's will; Thurio as a rival"],["Inciting incident","Proteus arrives in Milan and falls in love with Silvia"],["Turning point","Proteus betrays the elopement and Valentine is banished"],["Climax","In the forest Proteus tries to force Silvia and Valentine stops him"],["Resolution","Proteus repents, Julia reveals herself, and two marriages are planned"],["Major themes","Love against friendship; constancy and betrayal; disguise; the power of letters and tokens"],["Point of view","Comedy shared between lovers and servants, with soliloquies that let the audience into Proteus's and Julia's minds"]],"analysis":{"PROTEUS":["Proteus is named after a Greek sea god who could change his shape, and changeability is his whole character. He begins as a devoted lover, swearing constancy to Julia on his hand.","Within moments of seeing Silvia he forgets Julia, and in a series of soliloquies he talks himself into betraying both his love and his friend, using clever logic to make selfishness sound like reason.","He lies to his father, his friend, the Duke, Thurio and Silvia. His treachery peaks when he tries to force Silvia in the forest.","His repentance is sudden and brief. Some readers see true shame; others see a man who changes as easily for good as for bad, which is why the ending troubles many audiences."],"VALENTINE":["Valentine starts by mocking love and urging his friend to see the world, then falls headlong for Silvia, a comic reversal the servants enjoy.","He is generous and trusting, praising Proteus and telling him every secret, which makes him easy to betray. He is also slow to see Silvia's love-letter trick.","In exile he becomes a natural leader, insisting that the outlaws spare women and travellers.","At the end he forgives Proteus instantly and offers him Silvia. Defenders call this the ideal of friendship; critics call it treating Silvia as a possession."],"JULIA":["Julia is the play's emotional centre. She hides her love at first, tearing up Proteus's letter, then pieces it together when alone.","She shows courage and initiative by travelling to Milan disguised as a boy, the first of Shakespeare's cross-dressed heroines.","In disguise she watches Proteus woo Silvia, hears him say she is dead, and has to carry his gifts to her rival. Her asides and her story of playing Ariadne turn pain into wit.","At the end it is her action, the swoon and the rings, that brings Proteus back. Her line about women changing shapes and men changing minds judges him."],"SILVIA":["Silvia is witty and bold: she gets Valentine to write a love letter to himself, a trick only his servant understands.","She stays loyal to Valentine against her father's will and Thurio's money, and openly scorns Proteus as perjured and false.","She shows sympathy for Julia without knowing her, refusing the ring Julia gave Proteus.","After Proteus's attack and Valentine's offer she does not speak again. Directors must decide what her silence means."],"LANCE":["Lance is Proteus's servant and a clown whose long prose monologues are the comic highlights of the play.","His love for his sour dog Crab, who never returns it, parodies the lovers' devotion, especially Julia's for Proteus.","He takes whippings and punishments for his dog's faults, a loyalty that shames his master's treachery.","His list of his milkmaid's virtues and vices brings romantic idealism down to earth."],"SPEED":["Speed is Valentine's quick-witted page, a master of puns and logic games.","He sees through Silvia's letter trick at once and mocks his master's lovesick symptoms.","With Lance he provides a down-to-earth commentary on the lovers' fine words."],"DUKE":["The Duke of Milan is a typical comic father who wants to marry his daughter to the richest suitor.","He is clever enough to trap Valentine with an invented love story and the cloak trick.","At the end he admires Valentine's spirit, despises Thurio's cowardice and gives Silvia to Valentine, pardoning the outlaws too."]},"motifs":[["Blind Love","From Speed's 'Love is blind' to the serenade and Julia's 'blinded god', the play keeps asking why lovers see so badly.",[16,26,58,72]],["Changing shape","Proteus's name, metamorphoses, April weather, wax melting and Julia's disguise all show identity as changeable.",[4,14,29,83,84]],["Letters and rings","Torn letters, a letter written to oneself, a letter under a cloak and two rings carry the plot and expose the truth.",[10,11,17,19,69]],["Shadows and pictures","Proteus loves Silvia's 'picture' and asks for her portrait, while Julia becomes a shadow of herself.",[63,71,72]],["Dogs and servants","Lance's loyalty to Crab mirrors and mocks the lovers' devotion, and Proteus compares himself to a spaniel.",[23,57,64,65]],["Water and streams","The Hellespont, Julia's gentle stream and the ice figure that melts all picture love's force and change.",[38,39,52]]],"symbols2":[["Julia's ring","Given to Proteus as a pledge, sent to Silvia as a gift, and used to reveal Julia: faithfulness passed round and returned."],["The rope ladder","Valentine's means of escape with Silvia, found under his cloak: secret love exposed."],["Silvia's picture","A 'shadow' of the real woman, which Proteus prefers: love of appearance over substance."],["Crab the dog","Unfeeling and unresponsive, yet loved completely: a comic version of Proteus himself."],["The torn letter","Julia's scraps of paper: love hidden, destroyed and pieced back together."],["The forest","A place outside law where outlaws live, rank is forgotten and wrongs are put right."]],"bigq":[["Should Valentine have offered Silvia to Proteus?",["It shows friendship valued above everything, as Renaissance ideals taught","It treats Silvia as property without asking her","It may be a test of Proteus's repentance","Silvia's silence leaves the moment open"],[82,81,78,79]],["Is Proteus's repentance believable?",["He changes his mind as easily as he changed his love","His shame is sudden but strongly worded","Julia's presence brings back his first love","The play needs a quick comic ending"],[84,29,83,14]],["Is this play about love or about friendship?",["It begins and ends with the two friends","The plot is driven by love for Silvia","Proteus asks 'In love who respects friend?'","The final line joins both"],[3,78,30,85]],["Who is the play's real hero?",["Valentine is loyal and brave","Julia acts with courage and constancy","Silvia stays true against everyone","Lance shows the most loyalty of all"],[46,39,76,65]],["What is the role of Lance and Crab?",["Comic relief between betrayals","A parody of the lovers","A mirror of loyalty and its lack","Proof that the servant sees more than the master"],[23,64,65,57]],["Does disguise help Julia or hurt her?",["It lets her travel safely","It forces her to woo for her rival","It lets her speak the truth indirectly","It lets her win Proteus back"],[40,67,66,83]]],"symbols":{"Love":"\\blove\\b|\\bloves\\b","Faith and oaths":"\\boaths?\\b|\\bfaith\\b|\\bforsworn\\b|\\bperjur","Friend":"\\bfriends?\\b","Letters":"\\bletters?\\b|\\bpaper\\b","Ring":"\\bring\\b","Shadow":"\\bshadow|\\bpicture\\b"},"stagerules":[["forest","forest"],["abbey","abbey"],["garden","garden"],["antonio|julia","house"],["street|open place","street"],["palace|court|duke","palace"],["the same","palace"]],"solo":{"24":1,"111":1,"144":1,"182":1,"308":1,"431":1,"471":1,"546":1,"581":1,"647":1,"728":1,"819":1,"843":1,"879":1,"945":1},"iago":[["a2s4","Proteus falls in love with Silvia at first sight and begins to forget Julia."],["a2s6","He decides to betray Julia, Valentine and his own oaths at once."],["a3s1","He tells the Duke about Valentine's planned elopement."],["a3s1","He pretends to comfort the banished Valentine and sees him out of the city."],["a3s2","He agrees to slander Valentine to Silvia while pretending to help Thurio."],["a4s2","He uses Thurio's serenade to woo Silvia for himself and says Julia is dead."],["a4s2","He asks Silvia for her portrait as a substitute for her love."],["a4s4","He sends Julia's own ring to Silvia, by Julia herself in disguise."],["a5s2","He chases Silvia into the forest, for love of her rather than duty."],["a5s4","He tries to force Silvia and is stopped by Valentine."],["a5s4","He repents, is forgiven, and returns to Julia when she reveals herself."]],"rel":{"1":[["PROTEUS","JULIA","love"],["JULIA","PROTEUS","love"],["VALENTINE","PROTEUS","trust"],["PROTEUS","VALENTINE","trust"],["LUCETTA","JULIA","trust"],["PROTEUS","ANTONIO","deceive"]],"2":[["VALENTINE","SILVIA","love"],["SILVIA","VALENTINE","love"],["PROTEUS","SILVIA","love"],["VALENTINE","PROTEUS","trust"],["THURIO","VALENTINE","hate"],["JULIA","PROTEUS","love"],["LANCE","CRAB","love"]],"3":[["PROTEUS","VALENTINE","deceive"],["DUKE","VALENTINE","hate"],["DUKE","PROTEUS","trust"],["PROTEUS","THURIO","deceive"],["SILVIA","VALENTINE","love"],["VALENTINE","PROTEUS","trust"]],"4":[["PROTEUS","SILVIA","love"],["SILVIA","PROTEUS","hate"],["JULIA","PROTEUS","love"],["PROTEUS","JULIA","deceive"],["SILVIA","EGLAMOUR","trust"],["SILVIA","JULIA","love"],["LANCE","CRAB","love"]],"5":[["PROTEUS","SILVIA","hate"],["VALENTINE","PROTEUS","hate"],["PROTEUS","VALENTINE","trust"],["VALENTINE","SILVIA","love"],["JULIA","PROTEUS","love"],["PROTEUS","JULIA","love"],["DUKE","VALENTINE","trust"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present the conflict between love and friendship?",[3,30,34,78,82,85]],["How does Shakespeare present Proteus as changeable?",[4,14,29,33,84,31]],["How does Shakespeare present Julia's constancy?",[9,39,41,68,71,83]],["How does Shakespeare use disguise in the play?",[40,59,62,66,70,83]],["How does Shakespeare use the servants and the clown?",[15,17,23,50,64,65]],["How does Shakespeare present fathers and authority?",[12,13,43,45,52,73]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, dramatic irony, disguise, comic subplot) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation","Referred to the whole play, not just one scene","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"place":{"a1s1":"Verona","a1s2":"Verona","a1s3":"Verona","a2s1":"Milan","a2s2":"Verona","a2s3":"Verona","a2s4":"Milan","a2s5":"Milan","a2s6":"Milan","a2s7":"Verona","a3s1":"Milan","a3s2":"Milan","a4s1":"The forest","a4s2":"Milan","a4s3":"Milan","a4s4":"Milan","a5s1":"Milan","a5s2":"Milan","a5s3":"The forest","a5s4":"The forest"},"models":[[78,"Shakespeare presents love and friendship as rival loyalties, and shows love winning in Proteus until friendship is restored at the end.","Proteus's question 'In love who respects friend?' is short and blunt, stripped of the elaborate wordplay he usually hides behind.","The audience hears the selfish logic of his soliloquies spoken aloud to Silvia, and she answers it at once: 'All men but Proteus.'","Renaissance readers often ranked friendship between men above romantic love, so Proteus's choice would have seemed a deep betrayal of a sacred bond.","Yet Valentine's quick forgiveness and offer of Silvia suggest the play may be testing the friendship ideal as much as praising it."],[29,"Shakespeare presents Proteus as a man who changes as easily as his name suggests.","The image of 'one heat another heat expels' makes love mechanical: one feeling simply replaces another, like a nail.","The audience watches him explain away his betrayal in reasonable-sounding verse, which makes his inconstancy both clear and disturbing.","His name comes from the shape-changing sea god of Greek myth, a hint audiences who knew Ovid would catch.","Some argue his repentance at the end is another change of shape rather than real reform."],[39,"Shakespeare presents Julia's constancy through gentle natural imagery that contrasts with Proteus's restlessness.","The 'gentle stream' that wanders 'to the wild ocean' makes her love patient and natural, sure of its goal.","The audience admires her, but also fears for her, because they already know Proteus has changed.","Women were expected to wait at home; Julia's decision to travel, even in disguise, is bold.","A sceptical reading notes that her faith is also blindness: she trusts oaths that the audience has heard broken."],[66,"Shakespeare uses disguise to let Julia tell the truth while hiding who she is.","'She dreams on him that has forgot her love' describes Julia in the third person, so she can accuse Proteus without his knowing.","The audience enjoys the dramatic irony and feels her pain at the same time.","Boy actors played all women's roles, so the disguise added a layer of theatrical play for Elizabethan audiences.","The disguise also traps her, forcing her to woo her rival for the man she loves."],[64,"Shakespeare uses the clown Lance to mirror and mock the lovers' devotion.","Lance's complaint that his servant 'shall play the cur with him' reverses master and servant: the dog has let him down.","The audience laughs at the absurd story, but notices that Lance's loyalty is greater than his master's.","Clowns like Will Kemp were stars, and these solos were written to show off their skill with a live dog.","Some critics see Lance as the moral centre of the play, the one character whose love never changes."],[13,"Shakespeare presents fathers as powerful figures who decide their children's lives without consulting them.","Antonio's 'what I will, I will, and there an end' uses short, final words that shut down any reply.","The audience sees how Proteus's small lie about the letter leads straight to this command.","Elizabethan fathers had legal power over their children's education and marriages.","By the end, though, the Duke gives way to Valentine's spirit, so authority bends to love."]],"lang":{"un":[[24,"Made wit with musing weak, heart sick with thought.","Made my mind weak with daydreaming and my heart sick with worry."],[481,"And so by many winding nooks he strays With willing sport to the wild ocean.","And so he wanders playfully through many winding bends to the wild ocean."],[431,"So the remembrance of my former love Is by a newer object quite forgotten.","So the memory of my former love is quite forgotten for a newer one."],[985,"It is the lesser blot, modesty finds, Women to change their shapes than men their minds.","Modesty finds it a smaller stain for women to change their appearance than for men to change their minds."]],"pun":[[313,"Why, he that's tied here, Crab, my dog.","tied","pun","Tide (the sea) and tied (on a lead): Lance turns Pantino's warning into a joke about his dog."],[452,"Why, stand-under and under-stand is all one.","under-stand","pun","Lance proves his stick understands him because it stands under him."],[568,"No Valentine indeed for sacred Silvia.","Valentine","pun","His own name means a true lover: without Silvia he is no Valentine."],[754,"Not so; but yet so false that he grieves my very heart-strings.","heart-strings","pun","Strings of an instrument and strings of the heart: Julia's grief hidden in music talk."]],"st":[[959,"In love Who respects friend?",[["love","Love is the excuse for everything."],["Who","A challenge: name one man who would."],["friend","The word he now treats as worthless."]]],[969,"All that was mine in Silvia I give thee.",[["All","A total gift, holding nothing back."],["mine","Silvia as something owned."],["give","Generosity, or a transaction?"]]],[985,"Women to change their shapes than men their minds.",[["shapes","Her disguise, a surface change."],["men","Turning the accusation on him."],["minds","His real fault, an inner change."]]]]},"dir":[[471,"To leave my Julia, shall I be forsworn;","How should Proteus deliver this soliloquy?",[["Agonised, fighting himself","He knows it is wrong and struggles: we half pity him."],["Coolly, working it out","A calculating schemer: the audience distrusts him."],["Playfully, to the audience","He makes us his accomplices, which is uncomfortable."]]],[308,"I think Crab my dog be the sourest-natured dog that lives:","How should Lance play the scene with the dog?",[["Deadpan, as if serious","The grief is real to him, which makes it funnier."],["Broadly, playing to the crowd","Pure clowning: the dog steals the show."],["Gently, with real sadness","The comedy carries a touch of genuine loss."]]],[748,"~Who is Silvia? What is she,","How should the serenade be staged?",[["Sweet and sincere","Beautiful music makes Julia's pain sharper."],["Overdone and comic","Thurio's hired musicians mock courtly love."],["With Proteus singing, watching Silvia's window","Shows Proteus taking over Thurio's serenade."]]],[961,"And love you 'gainst the nature of love—force ye.","How should Proteus's attack on Silvia be played?",[["As real violence","Shocking: the comedy breaks, and the forgiveness becomes harder to accept."],["As a sudden loss of control","He is horrified at himself, making the repentance possible."],["Half-hearted, almost comic","Keeps the comedy safe but downplays Silvia's danger."]]],[969,"All that was mine in Silvia I give thee.","How should Silvia react to Valentine's offer?",[["Shocked silence","Her silence speaks: she is treated as a gift."],["Quietly trusting Valentine","She sees it as a test he knows Proteus will refuse."],["Turning away from both men","A modern reading: neither friend deserves her."]]],[985,"Behold her that gave aim to all thy oaths","How should Julia reveal herself?",[["Angry and accusing","She finally speaks her hurt plainly."],["Tearful and tender","Her love is stronger than her anger."],["Proud and witty","She wins with words, like Silvia."]]]],"why":{"0":"Sets up the play's contrast between staying home for love and travelling for honour.","1":"The scornful lover is about to fall in love himself, a classic comic reversal.","2":"Valentine's image of love destroying youth will fit Proteus more than anyone.","3":"States the two paths the friends take, and the choice the play keeps testing.","4":"His name means changeable, and this is the first change: more will follow.","5":"The servant's sour joke shows how far Julia hides her feelings.","6":"A comic line that also shows love is not reasoned but felt.","7":"Raises the question of whether love should be shown, which the whole play explores.","8":"Shows the gap between what a woman says and what she wants.","9":"Her self-mocking honesty makes her the most sympathetic lover in the play.","10":"The first of many letters torn, swapped or intercepted.","11":"Comic and tender: she treats the scraps as if they were the lovers.","12":"A father's maxim that uproots his son without asking him.","13":"Fathers rule absolutely: no discussion is allowed.","14":"A beautiful image that also describes Proteus himself.","15":"The servant turns the lover's conventions into comedy.","16":"Blind Love returns at the end, when Julia wonders why Proteus prefers Silvia.","17":"The servant understands the lovers' games better than his master.","18":"Silvia is witty and in control of the courtship.","19":"This ring will travel to Silvia and back, revealing Julia at the end.","20":"The oath that he breaks within a few scenes.","21":"Ironic: Proteus's own words prove empty, while Julia's deeds prove true.","22":"A neat couplet that sounds sincere, which makes his change more shocking.","23":"The comic parallel to the lovers' parting: devotion that gets nothing back.","24":"Physical comedy that turns grief into a muddle of props.","25":"The generous praise makes the coming betrayal more painful.","26":"Witty court banter about blind Love.","27":"The scorner of love is now its servant: the comic reversal is complete.","28":"He tells Proteus everything, trust that Proteus will exploit.","29":"He compares love to one nail driving out another: love as something that can be replaced.","30":"Love and friendship collide in a single line.","31":"The couplet shows his choice: he will try, but if not, he will scheme.","32":"The clowns play with words as their masters play with oaths.","33":"He counts three broken oaths and goes ahead anyway.","34":"The plainest statement of selfishness in the play.","35":"Uses the period's prejudice about dark skin to dismiss the woman he swore to love.","36":"He makes Love the excuse for treachery.","37":"Her love is action, not words.","38":"Opens her famous image of love as a stream.","39":"A gentle, natural image of constancy against Proteus's restless changes.","40":"The first of Shakespeare's heroines to dress as a boy.","41":"Painful dramatic irony: the audience knows his oaths are already broken.","42":"He names the law of friendship as he breaks it.","43":"His show of anger at Silvia is a trap for Valentine.","44":"His confident advice is turned against him minutes later.","45":"A myth of a young man aiming too high and burning the world.","46":"Love has become his whole identity.","47":"Rhetorical questions that make love the source of all meaning.","48":"Without Silvia he is no longer himself.","49":"False comfort from the man who caused the banishment.","50":"A down-to-earth parody of lovers' idealised praise.","51":"Mocks a common Elizabethan view of women, and sets up Silvia's silence at the end.","52":"He is wrong: Silvia's love does not melt.","53":"His friend's three supposed faults are really his own falsehood.","54":"The power of poetry and music to move a lover.","55":"A proverb that sums up Valentine's new life in exile.","56":"His rule against harming women matters when Proteus attacks Silvia.","57":"He degrades himself like a beaten dog, a link to Lance and Crab.","58":"The play's most famous lines, sung while Julia watches her lover court another.","59":"Musical puns let her speak her grief while in disguise.","60":"A double meaning only the audience can hear.","61":"She names his faults directly: her constancy exposes his.","62":"Julia, in disguise, hears herself declared dead.","63":"He chooses an image over a real woman.","64":"Lance suffers for his dog as Julia suffers for Proteus.","65":"A clown shows more faithfulness than the gentleman he serves.","66":"Disguise lets her tell the truth to the man who wronged her.","67":"She will woo for him, but coldly.","68":"Her loyalty is split between her master and herself.","69":"Silvia's loyalty to a woman she has never met.","70":"A story inside a disguise that tells her own grief.","71":"Finds no reason for Proteus's change but blind Love.","72":"The theme of blind Love from Act 2 returns.","73":"He still sees Valentine as beneath his daughter.","74":"The wilderness has become a home, as in later comedies.","75":"Love as what keeps him whole.","76":"Her steady scorn never changes.","77":"A sharp, witty judgement on faithlessness.","78":"The play's central conflict in three words.","79":"The darkest moment in the play: love turned to violence.","80":"A friend's betrayal is worse than an enemy's.","81":"Christian forgiveness, given with startling speed.","82":"The most debated line in the play: friendship above love, or a woman treated as property?","83":"Her disguise is a smaller fault than his inconstancy.","84":"Inconstancy named as the root of all faults.","85":"Friendship and love joined in a double wedding."},"ev":{"ctx":[["2","24","229"],["483","875","985"],["969","959","506"],["408","381","748"],["162","178","2"],["363","519","661"],["483","875","985"],["710","719","723"],["308","452","819"],["308","371"],["959","969","956"],["748","308"]],"cr":{"friend":[["969","308"],["959","969","424"]],"faith":[["431","471","748"],["969","959","961"]],"servants":[["308","819","620"]],"love":[["471","945","961"]]}}};
